      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>DEATH AND FUNERAL OF MR. JOHN</head>
        <p>DEATH AND FUNERAL OF MR. JOHN</p>
        <p>HARRISON.</p>
        <p>Death has removed a well-known and highly- respected townsman in the person of Mr. John Harri- son, who passed away at his residence in Egypt-street on Saturday morning. His genial and kindly dis- position had won for him a large circle of friends, and no man had fewer enemies than the white-hatted and courteous gentleman whom everyone knew as Mr. John Harrison. Latterly it had been noticed, with anxiety, that his health was far from satisfactory ; his step became feebler, and there were signs that his long life was gradually approaching the inevitable end. There was nothing to indicate, however, that the end was so near. He attended with his accus- tomed regularity, Wycliffe Congregational Church on Sunday morning, where he had been a worshipper since the erection of the church, and prior to that, in the old Wycliffe chapel. On Monday,he visited a friend, and had the misfortune to slip down when returning. Erysipelas supervened and he passed away on Saturday morning last. Born on the 7th of June, 1827, he was therefore in his 77th year. A tailor by trade, he was for many years a partner in the old firm of Messrs. Holmes and Harrison, Bridge- street, and on a dissolution of partnership taking place, he carried on business privately. In 1870 he succeeded in obtaining a seat on the Town Council, a position from which he retired in 1876 in order to take up the position of electiie auditor. For over 20 years he served the town in that capacity and only retired from the position in March, 1903. He was also a trustee of the Warrington Savings Bank. He was a consistent Liberal throughout his career and was a useful mem- ber lf the Liberal Association and Liberal Club. As a mark of respect, the flags at the Town Hall and the Liberal Club were lowered half mast. The Rev. John Yonge also made a sympathetic allusion to the death of Mr. Harrison at the morning service in Wycliffe Church. He referred to Mr. Harrison's long 'association with that place of worship and to the fact that on the previous Sunday morning he was worship- ping with them. The grave closed over the remains in Warrington Cemetery' on Monday afternoon. Shortly after two o'clock, the cortege left the deceased gentleman's late residence in Egypt-street. The relatives present were: Mr. Josiah Longland (son-in-law), Mr. Robert W. Thomas, Mr. A. H. Thomas and Mr. A. P. Thomas, of Liverpool (nephews), Mr. W. Brookes (Southport), Mr. John Forrest and Mr. Alfred Hallwood. There were also present: Mr. F. Monks, J.P., Mr. H. Roberts, J.P., Alderman Bennett, Mr. J. R. Young, J.P:, Mr. T. Birtles, Mr. T. J. Lee, Mrs. Clay, Mr. W. A. Proud (Warrington Savings Bank), Messrs. George Walling- ton, Edward Wallington, E. S. Garland, David Owen, W. Jeffrey (Wycliffe Congregational Church), Messrs. T. Critchley, Harvey and Whittle (representing the master tailors). Mr. S. Howard, an old employe of %Ir. Harrison for 48 years, was also present to pay a last tribute of respect. The service was impressively conducted by the Rev. John Yonge. Floral tributes had been sent by the following : —Mr. and Mrs. W. Harrison (Countesthorpe), "Auntie Bessie," "John and Cissie," "His Loving Children," Family of the late Mr. Peter Leigh, Mr. and Mrs. E. S. Garland, Mr. and IMrs. Hallwood and family, Mr. and Mrs. J. C. Young, Mr. and Mrs. J.. R. Young. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>HOLIDAY BREAKDOWN.</head>
        <p>A HOLIDAY BREAKDOWN.</p>
        <p>A temporary breakdown in health occurring at the holiday season may spoil all one's enjoyment. A bad headache, a day's 'biliousness, or an attack of indiges- tion, are each of them capable of driving away all possibility of pleasure. Chas. Forde's bile beans will prevent your holiday being spoiled in this way. They are the world's specific for indigestion and all liver complaints. Biliousness, headache, debility, constipa- tion, piles, foul breath, pimples, etc., all arise primarily from liver and stomach disorders; and bile beans, by correcting the organs responsible for these symptoms, drive them out completely. Bile beans may be con- veniently-carried loose in the pocket, thus being always handy. A dose taken after a trying day's holiday-mak- ing will prevent all ill-effects, and impart a feeling of healthy exhilaration upon rising next morning.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>EDUCATION IN THE COUNTIES.</head>
        <p>EDUCATION IN THE COUNTIES.</p>
        <p>Lancashire.</p>
        <p>MORE DECENTRALISATION DESIRED.</p>
        <p>At the initial meeting of the newly constituted Lancashire Education Committee held at Preston on Monday, Sir Henry Hibbert was unanimously re-elected chairman. Archdeacon Fletcher, of Tarleton, was re-elected vice-chairman. Upon the proposal that Horwich and Westhoughton should be constituted separate areas coming before the committee, Alderman W. W. B. Hulton said there was strong feeling in both townships, and he thought the presence of the one authority was perhaps calculated to temper the want of discretion on the other. A resolution was unanimously passed,agreeing to take into consideration the co-operation of the county boroughs of Lancashire and Cneshire and the Cheshire County Council for the provision of training colleges for teachers. Dr. Snape, the director of education, submitted an application for further additions to his staff. It was unanimously decided to vote a sum of £6OO per annum for the purposes required, but during the discussion the feeling was strongly expressed that the congestion of w4rk at headquarters could be greatly relieved by the decentralisation of work, and by the fixing of greater responsibilities upon the local area sub- committees, who were capable and willing of fulfilling increased duties. It was agreed that a sub-committee should be appoin- ted to inquire generally into tne committees' system of working. Cheshire. FINANCIAL DIFFICULTY AT CHESTER. A meeting of this committee was held at Chester Town Hall on Monday, Mr. H. T. Brown presiding. The Rev. J. K. Montgomery, with reference to the migration of children from school to school, drew attention to the practice of canvassing carried on by the clergy and laity visitors, and thought that they should be requested not to interfere with the children. The Bishop of Chester: The clergy of all denomina- tions. (Laughter.) The Chairman said canvassing ought not to be allowed, but it was very difficult to stop that kind of thing. People did it on the quiet. After further discussion the matter was dropped. It was agreed that the proportion of school fees, after consultation with managers, under section 14 of the Education Act, 1902, should not exceed one-half. A letter was received from the clerk to the governing body of the Chester School of Science and Art andi Technical Schools with reference to the continuation of the classes held at the Grosvenor Museum. The governors of the school asked whether the committee would guarantee to cover any loss in carrying on the schools until August, 1905, when the governing body would hind over the property and apparatus, value £950. Mr. J. G. Holmes pointed out to the committee that the work of the schools was now carried on at an annual loss of £2.50, and it would be a very sad thing for the town if the institution was closed. Several members spoke in high appreciation of the work carried on by the institution,and it wasultimately decided to refer the matter to the Higher Education Committee, with a view to the matter being dealt with as earlyas possible.</p>
