      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Strawberries as a Political Barometer.—lt is early yet to be thinking of strawberries, though when Easter is past we really begin to turn thoughts towards the good time of summer. It is then that the tea and strawberry season opens on the terrace of the House of Commons, but it seems that this year it is thought quite possible there will be no fashionable gatherings on the river front of the Houses of Parliament. Hitherto it has been the custom of thelKitchenDepartment to enter into aeon- tract for a supply of strawberries for the season,but the political outlook is regarded as so uncertain, that it has been decided not to enter into any contract this year. Thus, as straws show which way the wind blows,. so strawberries may in this way indieate an approaching dissolution of Parliament. The Rush of Fashionable Weddings.—The after-Lent weddings will now come on with a great rush, quite a hundred or more in the fashionable world being fixed for this month. On some days there will be so many that great difficulty has been experienced in making the limited number of favourite churches go round,and Lady , Warwick's little son has been much in request for his ser- vices as page. He has so often acted in this capacity, I that he must now be quite an authority upon all that is I correct and proper at a fashionable wedding. Amongst others that will take place this month, will be the wedding of Lord Herries' younger daughter,Miss Angela Constable-Maxwell, sister of the Duchess of Norfolk, who is to marry M. Eric Drummond. The marriage of Lord Hindlev and Miss Agatha Thynne will be solemnised in Westminster Abbey,and another wedding that will be of general interest in Society will be that of Lord Ingestre, and Miss Winifred Paget. The marriage of the Earl of Dunmore's daughter to Mr Gilbert Follett will be of special interest throughout the Highlands, where Lord and Lady Dunmore are very popular. The Tax of Wedding Presents.—ln all ages and in all countries it has been the custom to give presents in some form or other to newly wedded couples. It has therefore become too firmly established to be shaken by the protests which are made every now and then against the heavy tax which this custom involves upon people with a large circle of relatives and friends. Amongst leading families in society there are probably several upon whom the giving of wedding presents will fall as a daily obligation this month, and the difficulty of choosing gifts to people who already possess everything is no small one, apart from the question of expense. It does not matter, of course, if money' is no object, but there are numbers of people of no great possessions, to whom the giving of wedding presents involves a tax upon their resources. Children's Drawings.—Whilst the great art galleries are arranging their exhibitions for the season, one of minor interest—that of drawings done by little boys and girls, may be seen at the rooms of the Royal Draw- ing Society. There are over 2,000 drawings, mostly representing inoidents of every day life, and some of considerable artistic merit. Princess Louise (Duohess ef Argyle) always gives two prizes for the beet snap- shot drawing by a girl or boy. The Princess is a mem- ber of the Royal Society of Painters in Water Colours, and is a most competent judge of art. She is also one of the very few successful lady sculptors. The late Sir Edgar Boehm taught her, and she derived much assist- ance and encouragement irom the late Mrs. Thorneycro ft, who, at one time, was attached to Queen Victoria's household. One of the principal monuments to her art in sculpture is the statue of the late Queen in Kensington Gardens. The Demand for Women Workers in Canada.—The emigration of the factory worker is a problem recently taken up by the British Women's Emigration Associa- tion. During the twenty years of its existence, the Society has been instrumental in sending out about 8,000 women settlers to the Colonies, mostly of the teaching and domestic servant classes.—Lately,however, the Society has sent out two large parties of young women to Canada, who will be employed as factory workers. The officials have taken caret.) satisfy them- selves that there is a real demand in the Dominion for women workers, and such hands as blouse makers, umbrella makers and machinists have no difficulty in finding highly pad employment. There is always a sad side to the necessity of having to leave one's country, but to young people with the almost certain prospect of improving their position, this is not a very serious and lasting matter. The Habit of Losing Things.—lt it scarcely surpris- ing that women should have a greater capacity than men for losing their belongings in streets, cabs, omnibuses or elsewhere. Sometimes this habit is made the occa- sion for a disparaging allusion to their mental powers, but the fact that women have to go without pockets, in which to put keys, purses, and small parcels, is sufficient to account for their greater misfortunes. The mere man may say that it is a sign of mental deficiency that feminine attire should be unprovided with pockets, but that is another question which no dressmaker would admit his right to discuss. Equally out of place were the unsympathetic remarks of a magistrate in Loudon, who, upon a question of this kind being brought before him, complained that "women were always losing their things, and deserved the consequences as they wouldnot take proper care of them.'' The Becomingpess of Collars.—Paris seems to have decided upon going collarless this year,but happily our modistes show no signs of following this example. As a matter of fact it is only the very few who look well in a collarless gown, and those few would probably look better still if a band of some kind encircled their throat. This band may be simply a cobweb of lace, but it shou:d be there, and if possible it should be black. The differ- ence ahigh bin -k neck-band makes to the average com- plexion, hair and eyes, is simply marvellous and once tried, this idea will never be abandoned. The semi- transparent day blouse will not be worn by the well- dressed woman,an item we cannot sufficiently app:e3iate There is somethinr, particularly ill-bred in appearing in the streets in a something or blouse suitable only for indoor evening wear, and moreover such a garment on a hot sultry day is anything but cool or comfortable. Lace Blouses.—A great many black Chantilly lace blouses are to be seen just now at the restaurants and theatres. The lace is first mountedupon chiffon—white preferably—and then made up over a lining of soft white satin.—Such a blouse also looks well, and can be worn with either a black or black and white skirt. Brides, too, are shewing a great preference for complete blouses or vests of real lace to wear with their travelling gowns. The lace is mounted upon white chiffon and mounted up over white satin, and worn with a white cloth or serge skirt, and little bolero coat. Summer Fabrics.—lt seems early days to think of inuslins, but though they make one think of hot flannel and mustard and water, they should be chosen and secured so soon as Easter iv gone. Thu prettiest designs are quickly sold out, and it is not always possible to get them repeated. Stripes and spots abound, but the most desirable of the new muslins are those over which garlands of tiny flowers wander vaguely at their own sweet will.</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>LIVELY SCENES AT ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>LIVELY SCENES AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>A furnace at Messrs. Quirk, Barton and Burn's Lead Works was the scene of a very lively encounter in the early hours of Monday morning. At ten minutes past twelve a man named James Finnon was arrested for lodging there. Some time after the manager telephoned to the police office that two other men had taken possession of the furnace, Constables Henry Gleave, Mather, and Bramwell went and found Michael Finnon and William Golding in the furnace. There was only a small opening, and when the police came in they were assailed by half-bricks and Finnon threatened to rip Gleave with a knife. half-bricks, then gotithe hosepipe, but the water did not shift them. The manager then got a small bottle of ammonia,which was thrown inside and broken. This brought Finnon out but did not shift Golding. Some het cinders were thrown in to help the ammonia fumes, and when Police- constable Bramwell put his head down to watch the effect, he got a severe blow on the jaw with a brick, cutting it badly.—The men were sentenced to three months' imprisonment forassault and twenty-eight days for lodging out.</p>
        <p>IS THE BEST AND</p>
        <p>SAFEST REACD.</p>
        <p>For Thm 1,1 01.10 nistant Relief. y</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>Examiner Saturday, April 2 1904.</head>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday, April 2 1904.</p>
        <p>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY. ;</p>
        <p>Englishwomen are much slower than their French sisters to adopt any new fashion, especially one that is at all pronounced, as they do not lightly relinquish what they have once accepted. In the all-important matter of headgear this is particularly noticeable, and although the lace "curtain" which drapes the back of most of the new picture hats of this season was occasionally seen a year ago it is only now that it is much worn on this side of the channel. It is a style well suited to set off to advantage the charms of a pretty face but it is rather too pronounced for every- day wear by everyday people. There is something very " stagey " about the style of some of these large picturesque hats of chiffon, tulle, or fanciful straw, made in the palest colours, and wreathed with flowers round the crown, or feathers curling over the brim. A happy medium for ordinary daily wear on the days when the world is bathed in</p>
        <p>spring sunshine, is a French Breton sailor hat worn well over the eyes with the crown surrounded by a soft scarf of coloured silk and a lace fall draping the velvet - bound edge, while a cluster of flowers, or a smart velvet bow, conceals the wide bandeau at the back which gives the necessary tilt forwards. These hats are made in all sorts of materials from coarse straw and sailcloth to black Chantilly and cream guipere lace. The former are plainly and simply trimmed, while the latter are generally completed by a wreath of small shaded roses with their buds and leaves. My sketch shows one of the picture hats which have at last attained a degree of popularity. It is of pale blue straw veiled with lace, while a wreath of small pale pink roses surround the crown and mingle with the lace fall at the back. For waim days and future garden parties the style is sure to find favour amongst those who affect an artistic style of dress, but those women who cling lovingly to the tine veil which enhances while it conceals any blemishes of the complexion will not find this style suit their requirements as it is impossible to arrange a veil round the wide brim unless the 1830 fall is adopted. The Breton sailor hatismore suited to the arrangements of a veil, but it must be borne in mind that everything in connection with a hat of this style must be</p>
        <p>neat, as any tendency to untidiness is bad styles The elongated - pointed narrow toque is one of this season's novelties, but although charming, especially when made entirely in hyacinths or some small delicately-coloured flower, should only be worn by the owner of a small face. White and mauve parma violets are made into beautiful floral toques as like- wise are small roses and Roman hyacinths. These dainty toques look well on a bright spring day worn with a black cloth costume, or [one whose colour scheme is echoed in the tint of the flowers.</p>
        <p>The smart coat depicted in my second sketch boasts the basque or skirt which is so prominent a feature this season on the coats of many of the smartest toilettes. It is made also ,with one of the modish cape-collars which develop into stole-like ends finishing a few inches below the waist-line. It is of brown cloth and is 'trimmed by bands of velvet of the same colour but a deeper shade dotted with small groups of small gold spots. The waist-band and cuffs are also of the same velvet. The skirt accompanying the coat is trimmed round the hem by three graduated velvet bands finished by rosettes, the latter being the revival of an old but quaint fashion</p>
        <p>which will be quite the rage this summer on al sorts and conditions of dresses. A smartly shaped velvet toque lined in with pleated cream chiffon and finished by a handsome osprey completes this useful and elegant afternoon toilette. Many of the new coats are left open but finished by a tight-fitting waistcoat of cloth, kid, or pique, which keeps the coat in place and looks very trim and neat besides. A navy blue costume I admired had a white cloth waistcoat dotted with black and piped with red, while the fanciful and ornamental buttons which secured it down the front were also tinged with red. It looked very Tmart with a canvas Breton sailor hat bound with red velvet, a cluster of red geraniums nestling under the brim at the back.</p>
        <p>The pretty tea-gown which forms the sub- ject of my last sketch is made of pale pink crepe de chine trimmed with cream lace arranged in bands over pink silk. The sleeves are completed by pleated frills of pink silk, and a small frill of the same silk appears on the shoulders. The style is loose and flow- ing, which is characteristic of the best gowns, for comfort as well as elegance must be aimed at in the construction of this kind of garment. Those that are tight-fitting may be equally comfortable to wear but are not so to look at and are more suited to matronly figures for the fronts are always left loose though the outlines of the figure are preserved at the side; and back, COQUETTEd ,</p>
