      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>KING OF DENMARK.</head>
        <p>THE KING OF DENMARK.</p>
        <p>The visit of the King and Queen to the King of Denmark, Queen Alexandra's old father, will be pleasing not only to him, but to the people of both nations. King Chris- tian has always been very popular in Eng- land, though of late years owing to his advanced age we have seen but little of the doyen of European rulers. The Queen, how- ever, has always made a point, especially since her father' became a widower, of visit- ing him in the spring and in the autumn of each year, and King Edward has always, as far as circumstances permitted, accompanied her upon at least one visit in the year. His duties at home have, however, often pre- vented him from going more than once a year, but it is a well-known fact that a deep affectiorl exists between King Edward and his father-in-law, whom he has always revered since he was first brought into per- sonal contact with him. For the size of his</p>
        <p>DENMARK.</p>
        <p>country and its importance, commercial and geographical, King Christian has always had a disproportionate amount of influence in European affairs, and the Court at Copen- hagen has for many years been the neutral ground for diplomacy on the Continent. This is due firstly to his wide-spread con- nections by marriage and the dose connec- tion always maintained by his family with the parental roof, and, secondry, to the very fact of Denmark's actual lack of national importance in any European questions. The life led at the Danish Court is sim- plicity itself, and even in the season there are as few functions as possible. This has especially been the case since the Queen of Denmark's death, as the Crown Princess is a very religious woman with strict views, and objects to dances and all similar gaieties. And, above all, King Christian is well over eighty and, though hale and hearty with all his years, nevertheless a very old man.</p>
        <p>RUMOURED ATTEMPT ON THE LIFE OF THE-AMEER. There was current at the e-nd of last week a rumour from St. Petersburg, via Ash:- kabad, that the Ameer of Afghanistan had been poisoned; but no details were given. It was, however, not taken very seriously, for several reasons and caused no alarm. To begin with, trustworthy news of events in Kabul always reaches the In- dian Govern- ment some weeks before they eould pos- sibly be known in Ashkabad, which is up- wards of six hundred miles from the Afghan capital. There- fore, in the 'ab- sence of any news from In- dia, it did not seem likely that there was any foundation for .E AMEER . such a rumour. ISTAN. The latest ac- tion. of the Ameer, according to the most recent Indian papers, was to summon a number of tribal representatives to Kabul to hold a consultation. He, it is stated, is following the Russo-Japanese war with the utmost interest, having the Indian papers despatched to him each day. It is only two and a half years since the present Ameer, Hahbibullah, succeeded his father and our ally, Abdur Rahman. The new Ameer was born in 1872, and is the most capable of all Abdur Rahman's sons. His full brother, Nasrullah, is well-known in England and was over here not long ago. His half-brother, Sardar Umar Jan, togother with his step- mother, the Bibi Halima, Abdur Rahman's widow, is kept a state prisoner, to prevent any plotting from that quarter, but his cause was greatly weakened by the death of Ghulam Haidar Khan, the late Ameer's Commander-in-Chief, who was the Bibi's chief supporter.</p>
        <p>THE NEW PROVOST OF TRINITY COLLEGE, DUBLIN. Pretty general satisfaction is felt in Irish educational circles at the appointment of Dr. Anthony Traill to the important position of Provost of Trinity College, Dublin. The new Provost comes of an old County Antrim family and of well-known Irish stock on both sides, his mother</p>
        <p>haying been a Galw a y</p>
        <p>Ffrench. H i s father, Mr.</p>
        <p>William</p>
        <p>of Ballylough</p>
