      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>DEATH OF SIR EDWIN ARNOLD.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF SIR EDWIN ARNOLD.</p>
        <p>The death of Sir Edwin Arnold, although it has been known for some time that he was in failing health, will be very widely regretted. Few Englishmen have ever ob- tained greater popularity with their poetry than he did with "The Light of Asia," which ran into seventy editions in this country and over twenty in the United States, and, in a lesser degree with "The Light of the World." Sir Edwin Arnold was impregnated with things Eastern and Budd- hist lore, and never has an Englishman so absolutely absorbed the spirit of the Easl</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, April 2, 1904.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April 2, 1904.</p>
        <p>ne was a prolific writer and has now for many years turned out a book of prose or verse practically every year. Born in 1832, the dead poet was educated at King's School, Rochester, King's College School, London, and University College, Oxford, where he won the Newdigate Prize for Eng- lish verse in 1853. After two years as a</p>
