      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>TEE LATE SIR EDWIN ARNOLD:</head>
        <p>TEE LATE SIR EDWIN ARNOLD:</p>
        <p>schoolmaster, he was chosen to be Principal of the Government Deccan College in Poona, where he stayed from 1856-1861. In that year he returned to England on leave, and, in answer to an advertisement, joined tilt staff of the " Daily Telegraph" as leader-writer, acting, also, as special correspondent at times and he became one of the ruling spirits on the paper. It is computed that Sir Edwin Arnold wrote something like ten thousand leaders for the " Daily Telegraph," but this did not prevent him from travelling widely and doing other literary work. It was during this time that he carried on his profound Oriental studies and wroth his two great poems, which won him serious con- sideration for the post of Poet Laureate. In 1888 he was created a and nine years later he married, as his third wife, a Japanese lady, Miss Tama Kurokawa. His death is a great loss not only to the "Daily Telegraph," but to all his fellow country. men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>FAMOUS VOCALIST.</head>
        <p>A FAMOUS VOCALIST.</p>
        <p>In Mass Louisa Pyne (or, rather, Madame Bodda-Pyne), who died last week at her residence in town, 85, Cambridge Gardens, there passed away a link with the older generation of musicians. Born in 1828, the daughter of a well-known vocalist of his day, she made her debut at New- castle - on-Tyne</p>
        <p>as far back- as</p>
        <p>1838, and after-</p>
        <p>wards sang with</p>
        <p>great success in</p>
        <p>Paris, and in 1849 made her</p>
        <p>first appearance</p>
        <p>in grand opera. Two years later</p>
        <p>she Came over</p>
        <p>to the Royal Italian Opera __</p>
        <p>House and</p>
        <p>scored a very</p>
        <p>big hit, af t e-r which she went to the United</p>
        <p>States for a</p>
        <p>years'</p>
        <p>LATE MISS LOUISA PYNE.</p>
        <p>visit and-carried</p>
        <p>all before her.</p>
        <p>at once. By the time she returned to London, she had established her position as a first-class vocalist and straightway joined herself for business purposes with Mr. William Harrison, starting the Pyne and Harrison Opera Company, which held seasons at the Lyceum, Drury Lane, and Covent Garden. But it was fated not to become permanent, and Miss Pyne went to Her Majesty's, and from thence to the concert platform. She was always a very great favourite with Queen Victoria and the Prince Consort, and was often commanded to Windsor. Amongst her greatest triumphs was her appearance at Covent Garden as Queen of the Night in "The Magic Flute." A good story is told of one of her outlying concerts in the States. She sang "Home, Sweet Home" and took the back woodsmen off their feet, so much so that one jumped up and shouted, "That's worth full price by itself. Let's all go out and Pay again, boys." This unusual sug- gestion was promptly acted on, and Miss Pyne had the unique experience of an audien which had voluntarily paid two entrance fees.</p>
        <p>-------- e• ',.., , ,ts I" .i I. ,---\ •-•,. ,-, ............ ~-t-,.,._ ,.,._ 1:(' • / THE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>rHE KING AND THE INCORPORATED LAW SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>rHE KING AND THE INCORPORATED LAW SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>One of the King's last public acts before his departure for Denmark was the opening of the new wing of the Law Society's Hall in Chancery Lane. It took place on •the day after the funeral of the Duke of Cam- bridge, and, of course, despite the fine day, both the King and the Queen were in the deepest mourning, the latter carrying a parasol for the first time this year, a black one with a very long handle, and a diamond spray at the end. The new building is a remarkably fine one, and over a thousand lawyers were present to receive their Majesties. On the ground floor there is a splendid restaurant with a fine smoking-room at- tached and a common-room containing read- ing matter of all sorts. To the beauty of these rooms the wife of the President of the Incorporated Law Society, Mr. Gray Hill, has greatly contributed by some charmingly painted panels for the smoking-room and a large picture, "Dawn on the Dead Sea," for the dining-room. Her work was much ad- mired on every hand and evoked special</p>
        <p>attention from the King and Queen. Theil Majesties arrived punctually to their scheduled iime, a quarter past three, and were re- ceived by Mr. Gray tHill and the Lord Chancellor, supported hy the Lord Chief Justice, the Master of the Rolls, Lord Esher, Sir George Lewis, and other leading lawyers. In the common-room the President of the Incorporated Law Society read an address of welcome to the King, and then an amusing misunderstanding occurred, Mr. Gray Hill endeavouring, owing to some mistake, to give the parchment to Colonel Legge and the King on his side with a smile making counter efforts to gain possession of it, which he eventually did amidst loud ap- plause. Then King Edward replied in a vigorous, straightforward, complimentary speech, which was most enthusiastically re- ceived. This ceremony concluded, the Royal party made a tour of the building,lleaving just before four o'clock.</p>
