      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>'appearance of that unique little " Diary and panion " issued by GRO. G. BUSBY &amp; (30.. for ths use' of Cricketers (which may be obtained from Sports Dealers, Booksellers, or Railway Bookstalls for 6d.), reminds usof the coming season. in anticipation of whiati Gmo. G. BUBSEY &amp; Co., have prepared a record" stock of Bats. Weare told that the timber they hold for Bat making alone extleeds 1,000 tone. " Glorious Cricket follows the Britisu Flag everywhere." (1)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>-NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>THE -NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>THE FIRST DIVISION.</p>
        <p>%ARRINGTON BEATEN BY BRADFORD.</p>
        <p>Warrington were at home to Bradford on Saturday, and in view of the strong position in the League held by the Yorkshiremen, there was a fair amoupt of interest in the match. The homesters did not, however, play a full team, owing to the important Cup-tie of the following week and injuries to players. No less than four of the regular backs and three forwards were absent, the men being Hallam, Isherwood, Dickenson, Bate, Naylor, Fairhurst and Harmer. Bradford came with a full team, and the result seemed a foregone con- clusion, but Warrington's smart play kept the issue in doubt to the last minute. There wereabout 4,000 people present, and the turf was in excellent condition. Teams and officials:—</p>
        <p>Warrington—Knight, back IFish (captain), Hocken- hall, Mahoney, and Harris, three-quarter backs; Davies and Duckworth, half backs; Thomas, Jolley, Swift, Cook, Boardman, Morrison, Lunt, and Edmondson, for- wards.</p>
        <p>I Bradford—Gunn, back; Sinton, Eagers, Mosby, and I Dunbavin, three-quarter backs; Marsden and Surman, half backs; Broadley (captain), Feather, Laidlaw, Shar- T ratt, Greenwood, Rees, Upton, and Turner, forwards. Referee, Mr. A. Smith, Halifax. Touch judges,Messrs. F. Little, St. Helens, and H. Davies, Flixton. Marsden kicked off for Bradford, and landed the ball I well inside the home quarters. Here some excellent defensive work by Knight and Davies eased the pressure slightly, but Eagers speedily ran the ball back, and War- rington were hard put to to keep the visitors out.A fine movement between Marsden and Surman was spoiled by Eagers, and Duckworth and Davies changed the venue. The pace was very fast, and Warrington again raised the siege, and a round of passing witnessed•Sinton pull up Fish at the corner. Harris was next prorainent,but Eagers relieved, and following this came a direct raid on the home goal, and a fine breakaway by Marsden made a fine opening for Mosby,who unmistakably failed. Marsden again made an opening for Sinton, but Fish ran the ball out to half-way. Another renewal of the attack culminated in Eagers dropping a goal from nearly half-way, which point represented the score at half- time.</p>
        <p>Davies restarted for Warrington, and Bradford again took up the attack, Eagers and Dunbavin being kept out with difficulty. The visitors' combination was rather poor, otherwise they must have scored. Warrington soon took the ball to the other end,and Harris following Davies's kick smartly, had hard luck in not beating Mosby and Gunn on the line. Bradford retaliated, Marsden making a smart run to the Warrington quarter. Fish soon changed the venue by running grandly from his own half and punting past the Bradford fullback; unfortunately the ball travelled sideways and Eagers secured possession first. For a time Warrington kept up a hot attack, Davies and Mahoney both being held close to the line. Though the visitors finally cleared, they had little advantage in subsequent play and must he accounted lucky to retire winners by a goal to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>LEIGH v. WIGAN.</head>
        <p>LEIGH v. WIGAN.</p>
        <p>At Leigh, before 7,000 spectators. In the first half play ruled in favour of Leigh, but Wigan tackled finely, and prevented any score. Wi gan played up strongly in the second half, and pressed Leigh severely, Hector and Davies missing chances. In the last few minutes iJohnson had a golden chance of scoring for Leigh, but utilised it badly. Result.—Leigh, nil ; Wigan,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>BROL7GHTON RANGERS v. OLDHAM.</head>
        <p>BROL7GHTON RANGERS v. OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>On the former's ground. Oldham commenced to press Widdowson, however, broke away, and scored an easy try for the Rangers, this being the only score in the first half. On resumina,the home team had all the play and eventually won, after a one-sided game, by 2 goals and 3 tries (13 points) to nil. HULL v. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At Hull, before 5000 spectators. Hull started, and through Jackson promptly scored two tries, these being the only points recorded np to half-time. On resuming, Cook dodged over nicely, and Jackson placed a goal. Runcorn had lost Richardson in the first half,and Jolley also retired through a broken collarbone. Butterworth scored, Price failing. Butterworth had his collarbone broken and a rib fractured. Result—Hull, 11 points ; Runcorn, 3 points. WIDNES v. SWINTON.</p>
        <p>At Widnes, in fine weather, before 2000 spectators. In the first half Hughes and Ike Taylor scored tries for Widnes, and Alf Taylor kicked three goals. In the second half Gates scored a try for Swinton. Preston failed at the goal kick. Result—Widnes,3 goals 2 tries (12 points) ; Swinton, 1 try (3 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>KEIGHLEY v. HALIFAX.</head>
        <p>KEIGHLEY v. HALIFAX.</p>
        <p>At Keighley. The visitors invaded, but found strong opposition, and Keighley returned the compliment. Hardwick scored a try, Walker converting. Then Little landed a penalty goal. On changing ends Halifax showed great persistency, but a clever dribble by Stacey let Hardwick over. Walker failed at goal. Cooper did not play for Keighley. Result—Keighley. 1 goal 2 tries (8 points) ; Halifax, 1 goal 1 try (five points.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>SALFOR D v. LEEDS.</head>
        <p>SALFOR D v. LEEDS.</p>
        <p>At Salford, before 15,000 spectators. Salford had all the best of matters, and early on scored through Buckler. The Reds also got over again before the interval. In the second half Leeds registered a try through Grace, but Littlewood failed. Bedford and White, however, added tries for Salford, but Lomas failed withall the goal kicks. Result—Salford, 12 points; Leeds, 3 points.</p>
        <p>HUNSLET v. HULL KINGSTON ROVER.</p>
        <p>At Hunslet, before 9,000 persons. The opening half was fairly evenly fought. By a sharp pass AlberGold- thorpe scored an easy try. Hunslet led by three points to nil at the interval. Afterwards Davies dropped a pretty goal for Hunslet, while Burnett executed a similar feat. Gordon scored for the Rovers and Everson for Hunslet. Result—Hunslet, 3 goals 2 tries (12 points) ; Hull Rovers, 1 try (3 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>BATLEY v. HUDD ERSFIELD.</head>
        <p>BATLEY v. HUDD ERSFIELD.</p>
        <p>At Batley. The home team attacked from the start. Wolstenholme scored a good try, Bogg failing to con- vert. Will Davies shortly after scored, Oakland being successful at goal. Bogg scored a third try, Oakland again converting. Half-time—Batley, 13 points ; Huddersfield, nil. Oakland, Turton, and Bogg, all scored tries, Autv converting the last. Lewis scored for Huddersfteid and converted. Result—Batley, 24 points; Huddersfield,s points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>WARRING TON CUP. SEMI-FINAL.--REPLAYED TIE.</p>
        <p>THELWALL V. ST. HELENS RECS REISERVE.—This re- played tie took place on the Warrington United ground before a moderate gate. The first half was keenly con- tested, Howard registering the first goal for St. Helens. Half-time; St. Helens Recs. one goal; Thelwall nil. On the resumption Thelwall were seen to advantage and showed good combination. S. Woods obtaining possession from loose work passed to Green who beat Porter with a good shot. A further attack on the Saints' goal resulted in S. Wood scoring, thus giving his side the lead. The game continued to rule in Thel- wall's favour, but there was no further score. Final : Thelwall, two goals; St. Helens Recs Reserve, one goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCEI.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCEI.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD ATHLETIC V. LEGH-STREET ALBIONS.— At Arpley. Latchford won the toss, and after a well contested game, half-time arrived with a clean sheet. Play had hardly resumed before` J. Burns scored, two more goals by B. Taylor and S. Dawson soon following. After this Legh-street pressed,but the back defence was soand and Latchford won by three goals to Legh-street .Albions nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>TILE MAN WITIteTriE PISTOL.</head>
        <p>TILE MAN WITIteTriE PISTOL.</p>
        <p>Dennis James Wood was charged at St. Helens on Monday with disorderly conduct. Police-constable Alexander said the man was drunk and disorderly in Park-road on Saturday night. He had a pistol in his possession, and a woman complained that he had threatened to shoot her. The prisoner said the pistol was not loaded, and it would not work. Chief Constable Wood said the prisoner had been convicted six times. In 1890 he was sent to penal servitude for five years for wounding with intent. ThP other convictions were for drunkenness. He was fined 9s. 6d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>"NOTHING CHEAPER OR BETTER THAN VI-COCOA."</head>
        <p>"NOTHING CHEAPER OR BETTER THAN VI-COCOA."</p>
        <p>Thousands of men and women say this. Experience has told them that a 6d. packet or 9d, or Is. Gd. tin of Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa is the best value in the world. Experience also tells them that Vi-Cocoa affects beneficially both the old and the young, hnd possesses properties equally valuable to the weary brain worker and to the man or woman whose labours are more par- ticularly manual. In fact there is no cocoa like Vi-Cocoa, and if you have not tried it the Proprietors will willingly send you a dainty sample tin free upon receipt of a post card addressed to 60, Bunhill Row, London, B.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>YELLOW LABOUR.</head>
        <p>YELLOW LABOUR.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKINGMEN'S PRO- TEST AGAINST THE ORDINANCE. Speeches by Mr. iimes Sexton and Mr. Fred Bromley.</p>
        <p>THE LABOUR REPRESENTATION MOVEMENT.</p>
        <p>The Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, was well filled on Tuesday evening when a meeting, convened by the Trades and Labour Council. was held to protest against the importation of indentured Chinese labour into the Transvaal. The audience consisted almost entirely of workingmen who manifested the liveliest interest in the important question under consideration. Mr. J. Podmore, jun., president of the Trades Council presided, and he was supported on the platform by Mr. James Sexton (general secretary of the National Union of Dock Labourers and a member of the Parliamentary Committee of the Trade Union Congress), Mr. Fred Bramley, a well-known Socialist lecturer, of Bradford, Councillor Stott, Messrs. 0. Buckley, A. Jolley, Randles and others. Mr. C. E. Parker was present in the audience.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>SOUTH AFRICAN "EL DORADO."</head>
        <p>THE SOUTH AFRICAN "EL DORADO."</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said each one of them ought to pro- test very strongly against the Chinese labour ordinance. He remembered the time when it was said that South Africa was to be an El Dorado for British working men, a land where there would be plenty of labour, plenty of money, and, to use the Biblical phrase, "a land flowing with milk and honey." That was held out to them for the purpose of obtaining a certain result, to make them mad upon a certain subject, and to-day they saw one of the results—the spectacle of a horde of cosmopolitan financiers, mostly with foreign names, wanting to get ; cheap labour into South Africa, thus giving the British labourer the go-by. He thought he was putting his finger on the spot in this matter, when ;he said that the whole of the trouble caused in South Africa during the past few years had been caused by capitalism. (Applause). It would ;be a very bad day for England if the workingmen did not take care how far capitalism got control. /n his opinion the capitalists as represented in South Africa , would, if they had the power, import Chinese labour in- to England. When capitalism ran rampant and got ab- solute power, it lost everything—heart,raind,patriotism honour, and justice,—for the sake of making money. , They knew that in days gone by,trade unionists had had r; to fight for the positionthey now occupied. The intro- duction of cheap labour into a district acted directly or indirectly against the general standard of living of the • workers. It was to the interest of a man who was well paid to see that his brother in another district,however far away, was also well paid. (Applause). It was an act full of humiliation that they had had to wait until the twentieth century for a band of so-called statesmen to commence what was virtually slavery. (Hear,hear). The spectacle was humiliating and d grading to the British nation. It VMS surprising that there were men who could make excuses even for that ordinance. He often thought that if the children or relations of those men were to be subjected to the conditions laid down for Chinese labour, they would be the first to protest. It was also surprising to find that people who attended meetings of Bible Societies which ; existed for the purpose of sending Bibles to foreign countries and converting the heathen to a knowledge of Christianity, could support the Government which was giving them such a taste of glorious Christian civiliza- tion. In conclusion he urged his hearers to sink all miserable partyism, and to unite for what was good and right. (Applause). At this stage a letter regretting inability to attend was read from Mr. E. Plinston, who alluded to "this monstrous and iniquitous intneduction of slavery into South Africa." THE RESOLUTION OF PROTEST. Mr. OSWALD BUCKLEY (a member of the Trades and Labour Council) moved the following resolution : "That this mass meeting of Warrington citizens em- phatically protests against the action of the Govern- ment in granting permission to import Chinese labour into South Africa upon conditions of slavery, and calls upon them to protect this new colony from the greed of capitalists, and the Empire from degradation." (Ap- plause.) He said the people of England believed they were prosecuting the South African campaign for the good of the country. They were told that South Africa was to be the white man's country, and that it would take the surplus labour from our markets. Had they got the white man in South Africa? No. Was this 1 what British soldiers sacrificed their lives for? Was it for this that they left their wives and children to the ; care of their friends in England? Was this what they had fought for? Would Warrington working men stand such a state of things? Were they going to sit down quietly and allow the Government to carry out such an infamous ordinance? Our colonies had protested against the introduction of Chinese labour, and they ; had had experience of it. This was, he contended, the 1 question upon which the next general election would ' be fought to a great extent, and when it arrived he asked those present to remember that their representa- tive in the House of Commons voted in favour of ; Chinese labour. ("Shame" and applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>MR. E. DRAPER'S VIEWS CRITICISED.</head>
        <p>MR. E. DRAPER'S VIEWS CRITICISED.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. JOLLEY, a member of the Trades Council, seconded. He alluded to a speech delivered at the Junior Conservative Club last week by Mr. E. Draper. The audience Mr. Draper was addressing must have been very junior, for it would have bean an insult to school boys to have been talked to in such a way. One argument adduced by Mr. Draper was that the British workman would not be able to do the work in the mines owing to his constitution and to the climatic conditions. They had always been led to understand that for physique and endurance, the British working- man was secondto none in the world. MrD raper told them that Sir Henry Campbell-Bannerman's vote of censure was the most disgraceful that had ever been brought into the House of Commons. He (Mr. Jolley) had an idea that it was one of the most glorious resolutions ever submitted. (Applause.) All honour to Sir Henry Campbell-Bannerman and the 242 members who supported him. (Applause.) It was a disgraceful thing that Major Seely—(applause)—who was elected to Parliament when he was fighting in South Africa should, when he had the courage to state his opinions, be howled downs by the mob of so-called English country gentlemen behind him. Mr. Draper had informed his hearers that the time would come when the British workman would see the matter in its true light, and wou'd know how he had been misled and duped, and then retribution would come upon those who had misled and duped him. Mr. Draper was behind the times. They already knew who had misled and duped them. It was the present Government which bad misled them throughout that miserable business. Granted the opportunity, the time of retribution would come. (Applause.) •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>MR. SEXTON APPORTIONS THE BLAME.</head>
        <p>MR. SEXTON APPORTIONS THE BLAME.</p>
        <p>Mr. SEXTON, who had a hearty reception, said the question before them was one which everybody ought to exercise their brains upon. While identifying him- self with the resolution he wanted to evenly distribute the responsibility. He was equally divided in his mind whether to condemn the Government or to condernnthe workingmen who male such a Government passible. (Hear, hear, and laughter.) In reviewing the situation Mr. Sexton said the Jameson raid in his opinion CVLS the beginning of the whole infamous business. The result of that raid was the Boer war, and they were told by the then Prime Minister (the late Lord Salis- bury) that it was not the intention of the Government to annex any territory or any gold-fields. It was not long before the good intentions of the Government were falsified. The country was annexed, and as a sop to the workingman, he was told that South Africa was going to be an El Dorado for the British workingman under the glorious flag of our empire, upon which the sun never sets—and in which, added Mr. Sexton the tax- gatherer never goes to sleep. (Loud laughter.) How many workingmen, he would like to know, gave up their jobs under a promise that they would be kept open for them until their return from the war in South Africa, and who on their return were unable to find any work. He knew many dockers who were unable to get a job, whilst at the same time Chinamen were being "shovelled" into South Africa. Where they had an " industrial system like the present, with so many men out of work, the conditions and circum- stances made freedom of conduct absolutely impossible. No man was free except he had the freedom to produce, and no man could have freedom to produce without there was free trade in the land.(Hear,hear.)The British workingman by his action at the last election must accept some responsibility for the present condition of things. Mr. Chamberlain ha.d now raised the cry of pro- tection to cover up the iniquities of the South African war, and at a recent meeting in Liverpool he spoke of protection as a panacea for raising wages and finding plenty of employment. (Laughter.) Mr. Chamberlain told them their industries were vanishing. The manu- facture of pearl buttons had nearly gone. What a calamity! (Laughter.) What was the use of a button to a man who had not a shirt upon which to stitch it. (Laughter.) British workingmen, they were now told, were not able to do the work connected with the mines in South Africa. Quite a different tale was told when the British workingman was fighting the Boers on the veldt for the Jew mineowners. It was now suddenly discovered that the Britisher's physique .to work the mines was not as good as it was to -fight the battles against the Boers. A hope had been expressed by a local gentleman that the time would come when the workingman would see this matter in its true light. (Laughter.) He (Mr. Sexton) wondered if it ever Would. There was a time when he despaired of the workingman seeing any- thing in its true light. (Hear, hear.) There was a glimmer now and it was getting brighter ever 7 day. He hoped the next election would prove that the working man was beginning to see matters affecting himself and</p>
        <p>his wife and children in their true light before every other consideration. (Hear, hear.) In his capacity as a member of the Parliamentary Committee of the Trades Union Congress he happened to be present in the House of Commons during the memorable discus- sion on the Chinese labour 'question. It would be a liberal education to some of them if they saw some of thp specimens of legislators seat to the House of Commons There were men representing British industrial consti- tuencies whose very faces gave the show away. Tlte Jew financier was written on every feature of their countenances and they conspired to defeat the vote of censure on the Government because they were directly interested in keeping up the present condition of things (Hear, hear). The specious argument was used that the white man would not work with the black man and that the work of the black man was degrading to the white man. There were white men compelled to do work in local chemical works which even the Kaffir would, not do. work that would kill the constitution of a horse, and which was so dangerous as to imperil life and limb There was no work too degrading if the wage was pro- portionate to the work. (Hear, hear.) That was the crux of the whole question in South Africa. Before the war the Kaffir got 35. 6d., and now it was proposed to reduce the wages list by theimportation of Chinese. In the Government return, it was stated that the negro population in South Africa was increasing 50 per cent. Mr. GRIMSFIAW : Question. Mr. SEXTON : There is no question about it, or else the Government return is wrong. Mr. PARKER: What period are you referring to? Mr. SEXTON: From the psriorl before the war up to the present. The native population is three times larger than the white population and the native population continues to increase more rapidly than the white population. The natives are only too willing to work but they will not work for Is. 6d. a day like a China- man. Thousands presented themselves weekly and were • willing to work if they got the s tme pay they roceived prior to the war.</p>
        <p>Mr. GRIMSTIA.W ; No, no. Nothing like it. The CHAIR.MAN : You will have an opportunity of refuting it later on.</p>
        <p>Mr. SimToN said he was quoting from the reports of the Johannesburg Trades Council, the Cape Town Trades Council, and other authorities who had made a study of the question from a labour point of view. Un- employment in South Africa was never greater than at the present time. What a happy hunting ground for the capitalist! It-was a most disgraceful thing for a responsible Government to allow the importation of Chinese after the promises which were made to the workingmen of this country during the war. What an example to the civilised world! The British Govern- ment were introducing a bill to keep undesirable aliens out of this country. What an irony on the situation in South Africa! The Government did not mean it. They were only gulling the workingmen again. How could a Movernment come with tears in their voices asking them to keep out undesirables when they were 'shovelling "them into a British pos.eseion when they could have kept them out. This was essentially a ques- tion for labouring men. He was not concerned who took up this fight so low, as it suited their ends. At present it suited them that the Liberal party had taken the matter up. He recognised their philanthrophy, but no further. He had no quarrel with them any more than with the Tory party. He was a labour man and he wanted labour men sent to the House of Commons. (Hear, 'sear.) But he was not going to be led away by those who wanted to make political capital out of the Chinese labour qnestion. Where there was a possibility of a labour man being run he ought to be run. (Hear, hear.) Where there was no possibility of a labour '.inan being run then it ought to be the duty of British workingmen to use any weapon against the present Government. (Applause.) That was a matter for those to consider where a labour man was not seeking their suffrages. There were men on both sides of the Housewho were disclaiming loudly against the introduction of coolie labour in South Africa, who were themselves employers of coolie labour. There were shipning owners who manned their ships by the very same kind of labour as was being denounced in South Africa. (Shame.) Ha was not going to be led off the track and he hoped British working- men would not. One object they had in view was to send their own men to the House of Commons to do their own work, and until they did that they would never get security of tenure in the House of Commons or anywhere else. The only way to change the laws was to change the men who had made them. At the next election the Labour party would have 78 men in the field. For the first time in their histoty, workingmen were paying for their politics and they were going to see that they got the true article. Fifty thousand pounds were being contributed by trade unions' to run their representatives. 'Nese men would not b 3 individual representatives of different branches of trade unTonism but w-ild do the whole work of the labour movement. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>MR. BRAULEY AND TEIS RA.ND LOWS.</head>
        <p>MR. BRAULEY AND TEIS RA.ND LOWS.</p>
        <p>Mr. BRA.MLEY, of Bradford, said their opposition to this latest decision of the British House of Commons was not based upon 'racial antagonism. They did not object to Chinese Labour because it happened to be yellow. The mineownera of South Africa were not par- ticularly anxious about the colour it wLs so long as it was cheap. (Hear, hear.) It was the conditions of labour they sought to impose upon the "heathen Chines" that constitnted the greatest portion of the objection of labour men in this country. (Hear, hear.) The British worker was told that the more black labour there was and the more yellow labour there was, the more white labour there would be in South Africa. He hoped they would see through that transparency. There were almost as many black workers in South Africa to-day as there were before the war. Before the war there were 98,000 black workers and ar, the end of 1903 the estimated number at work was 80,000 and that with- out taking into consideration the disturbing influences of the country. If there was a scarcity of labour, the mineowners were more responsible for it than any other section of the community. (Hear. hear.) The demoralising conditions in the mines and the low rates of wages ware responsible for the refusal of some black chiefs to allow their black men to work in the mines. They had it on the authority of Sir George Harwood, M.P., that he was told by the mineowners during his visit to South Africa that one result of the war had been to reduce the wages in the mines by fifty per cent. (Shame.) When some speakers pointed out during the Boer war that the war was in the interests of the specu- lators on the Rand, they were met with cries of" Rot," and " You are lacking in patriotism." The latest success of the mineowners was that thelfad been able to do something under the British Goternment that they never!woull have been able to do so long as President Kruger controlled affairs. (Hear, hear.) Not only were the mining lords able to control Lord Milner and the labour conditions in South Africa, but they were also able to control the political situa- ion in the British House of Commons. (Hear, hear.) Such was the power of gold; such was the power of the commercial interest In South Africa. Sir George Harwood was told by one of the influential magnates in South Africa (Sir George Farrar) that he hoped the British House of Commons would allow the introduc- tion of yellow labour into South Africa, and that they would have the sense to see that it was inevitable. Unless they did, Sir George Farrar added, the mining lords would not hesitate to go over to theßoers and make trouble in the country. These were • the men who had no patriotism. (Hear, hear.) The mine owners did not object to any kind of labour so long as it was cheap. What they wanted was a supply of cheap labour which would enable them to make larger profits. When the "victims" arrived in South Africa they were deprived of all the elementary principles of liberty which were conceded to human beings. When they got into compounds they could not get out with- out special permission, and if they were found outside the compounds without a signed permit they would be treated like criminals. They had to be denied all political privileges and rights of citizenship. They were not allowed to own land, and when the three years' term had expired they had to return to China. They were supposed to have saved sufficient to help them to live in comfort to the end of their days. (Laughter.) Mr. Bramley proceeded to deal with the reasons why the mine owners objected to the develop- ment of one of our colonies by our own people. They knew, he said, that if the ..orkers af England went there they would demand the realization of that privilege that we fought for, or that we were supposed to fight for, in South Africa.—equal political rights for all I white men. They knew the British workers would have the sense, at any rate, to demand a vote and that, they considered, would be a danger. The white workers in Australia had been successful in securing substantial concessions by their collective power in trade union organizations, and the mine owners did not want the conditions that prevailed in Australia to obtain in South Africa. It had:only been by the overtures of the Chinese minister and through his efforts that the mine lords were not permitted to flog their workers. Mr. GRIMSRAM : They dare not do it. Mr. BRAMLEY : Our friend either does not know anything about the question— Mr. GRINISHAIV : I know from the spot. Derisive shouts greeted Mr. Grimshaw's interruption but the Chairman restored order.</p>
        <p>Mr. BR A. AILEY pr o c neied with his speech and alluded to the argument that Chinese labourers would be "civilized" by their work in South Africa. To compel men to work under the destructive conditions that obtained in the compounds, to compel them to work for such miser- able wageseand at the same tune to tell them that they were civilizing the labourers was, to his mind,a misuse of the term civilization. (Applause.) They were told that it would not pay to employ white labour. He had a list of the mining companies and the percentages they were paying, and he found that the average percentage of interest on the money invested was over 64. That did not satisfy the men who had their money invested in the mines, and they de- mande&amp;that the honour and reputation of this country should be sunk in order to gratify their insatiable greed for increased dividends. (Applause.) • He was there to protest against the introduction of Chinese labour as a trade unionist.He recognized in it an attempt to reduce the standard of comfort of the workers of the world. It was part of the great conflict between capital and -labour. The capitalists monopolised our British House of Commons at the present time, and it was because the vapitalist conscience was so much in evidence that they had such decisions made possible. "What we want "continued Mr. Bramley "is a new national conscience. We want in the House</p>
        <p>of Commons men who believe in the brotherhood of men, whether they be black, yellow, or any colour, who believe that the first duty of English statesmen is to secure proper conditions of lifeand labour not only for those who live in our colonies, but for those who live at home. (Applause). I protest not only as a trade unionist but on the grounds of humanity. To me there is something more than increasing the dividends of Jewish speculators. There is a more important principle thas. to secure the increased output of gold, and that is t Protect the rights of humanity. (Applause.) I standher almost on a higher platform. I stand here as a patriot to jealously guard all there is left of the reputation of my country. (Applause.) We object to this system of slavery in the interests of the trade unionist workers of the world, and in the interests of the reputation of our country, and we protest in the name of true patriotism!' (Loud applause.) Before putting the resolution, the CHAIRMAN invited anyone present to move an amendment. There was no response, and on a vote being taken,the resolution was carried, Mr. Grimshaw being the only dissentient.</p>
        <p>Some of the members of the audience did not hesitate to express their candid opinions on Mr. Grimshaw's attitude, but the Chairman appealed to them not to "make a butt of one man, as he was entitled to his own opinion." The meeting then proceeded without further interruption.</p>
        <p>Councillor STOTT proposed a vote of thanks to the speakers. He pointed out that if Chinese labour were allowed in the mines, he thought it would be expected in the docks at Capetown. The Trades Council which he considered, was the most representative body of workingmen in Warrington, had unanimously passed a resolution of protest against the ordinance. That meeting appeared to show conclusively that the working- men, almost as a body, agreed with the 'Trades Council's protest. (Applause). Mr. HANDLES seconded the motion which was heartily carried.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN expressed the hope that they would have more meetings of.that description in Warrington,as they were needed. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>FISH AS AN ARTICLE OF DIET, Its Great Importance.</head>
        <p>FISH AS AN ARTICLE OF DIET, Its Great Importance.</p>
        <p>All doctors agree as to the great importance of fish as an article of diet, but, unfortunately, there is a same- ness in the way of serving fish that prevents its wider use. We must have our pllttes tickled, or we do not relish what we take. What is wanted is a greater variety in serving the commoner sorts of fish. To obtain this, fish should be served with a variety of simple yet tasty sauces. Such sauces are now within the reach of all, and in Brown &amp; Poison's booklet, "Simple Fish Sauces," written for ordinary households, by a leading expert, a number of excellent recipes for such sauces are given. These are quickly and easil3, made, and give snap, flavour and richness to the commonest fish. The thickening for such sauces is Brown &amp; Poison's" Patent " Corn Flour. Application for this booklet should be made at once, to Brown &amp; Poison, Paisley, enclosing id. stamp.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>GOLF AT CANNES.</head>
        <p>GOLF AT CANNES.</p>
        <p>Messrs Crosfield and Ball to the Fore.</p>
        <p>A writer in the Paris edition of "The---lew York - Herald," of March 22nd says:—</p>
        <p>"I have received the following interesting com- ments upon the recent play ot some well-known golfers : "On Friday afternoon Mr. J. Graham, jun., and Mr. G. NV. Hillyard played Mr. J. Ball. jun., and Mr. A. H. Crosfield, giving them three up. The match was closely fought from start to finish, and in the end was halved. The score of the best ball is worth recording. It is as follows : Out: 3,4, 4,3, 4,3, 3, 4. 4.-32 In: 4,3, 3,4, 4,4, 4,3, 3.-32. "As they were playing from the back tees, which makes the course much longer and more difficult I think it will be agreed that it is a record no, likely to be beaten in a hurry. "Of course, it is mainly due to the fact that Mr. Ball was in grand form and played a splendid game all through, while Mr. Graham's performance was magnificent. The lattar's individual score was 74, only one stroke more than his round in the morn- ing, which won him the two prizes. His driving was stupendous ; for instance, at the third hole, where any ordinary mortal is content to get home in tw ) with the aid of the wind, and well enough pleased to be within an approach of the green with- out it, Mr. Graham, with the wind three parts against him, was actually pasts the green in two shots. Better work than this we have never seen. At the tenth hole again, a terrible teeshot left him within an easy iron of the green, which most of us are only too glad to reach in two drives and an approach. There were at least three, if not four, of Mr. Graham's drives, out of which neither James Braid nor even Ted Blackwell would have been able to take any change. "In the team match of Wednesday and Thursday, although Mr. C. F. Hutton and Mr. Crosfield each got a handsome beating in the singles, they com- bined well in the doubles, and beat their opponents by two and one. For half of the round INlr.Hutton played a really first-class game, and in the other half his partner came to the rescue and showed something of his true formand combative qualities. "The captain of the visiting team, however, has complained lately that his form has suffered through lack of practice. Except during holidays at St. Moritz and Cannes, he scarcely ever touches a club nowadays, and, in fact, since last summer has not played a single round over the links at Hoylake where he lives. He was heard to remark that this was the result of meddling with politics, and that he had little hope of picking up any more 'pots' at the Royal and Ancient game, because golf was a most exacting mistress, and was sure to be revenged on him for lack of attention to his liege lady. "Yesterday morning Countess Torby and Mr. H. H. Hilton, played Miss Hakim and Mr. Ball, and Mr. Ball, and the former won a really capital match by two up and one to play. Mr. Hilton, though playing well at times, has not been quite at the top of his game this week, but Mr. Ball, save for one or two rounds, has played splendid golf, which it was a treat to watch. "The most brilliant game of all, however, was produced by Mr. Graham, and his play on Friday was equal, Mr. Hillyard and Mr. Crosfield say, to the finest form of the best professionals. If he will only produce the same game during the champion- ship, I shall be very sorry for whoever has to meet him, for he will make rings around his opponents. "Yesterday afternoon Messrs. Hillyard and Cros- field played an interesting fourball match against Dr. Eaves and Mr. H. L. Doherty, giving them odds and just winning of the last, arePn. '</p>
      </div>
