      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>ME EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>ME EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>APRIL 2, 1904.</p>
        <p>To an astonished HIPPY SCOTIA! House of Common the Secretary for Scotland on Monday introduced a Scottish Edu- cation Bill. The Llonse was astonished because the Bill was almost blameless, and most of the Scottish members fell upon Mr.GRAHAM MURRAY'S neck in the warmth of their appreciation, while English members looked on with wonder and envy. For Scotland is a part of the United Kingdom where education is not a contentious subject. In England, Wales, and Ireland it cannot be men- tioned without making politicians fly at one another's throats. The very word " edu- cation" has such echoes of Parliamentary strife that when it fell from Mr. MURRA.Y's lips without provoking the um tl uproar, English Welsh and Irish memb 3rs sat stricken with amazement. They must have been still more amazed as they listened to the provisions of the new Bill. Hare was a INlinister blandly assuring the House that the SJhool Bard wis a most valuable institution. As an educational agency, it was beyond price. The idea of sitting County Councils to administer education was too absurd to be entertained for a tn 3m3nt. Was IVIc• GRAErAm nUaßly greeted with indignant inter- ruptions a -id derisive cries? Did anybody remind him of the E 1 ucation Act of 1902 ? No. The Scottish members listened with approval,and when he ended they overwhelmed him with cougratula - tions ; and the English, Irish, and Welsh members seemed to think that they had strayed by acci- dent into some paradise where the wicked cease from troubling and th 3 weary are at rest. " Toere is no religious difficulty in Scotland," said Mr. GRAHAM MURRAy, with a glee that was almost indecent. In Scotland the people and their representatives think about educa- tion for its own weir,. In our pir of the island, whatever the people think, their representatives are apt to forget the interests of edit !ation in a furious clamour about sectarian dttfarea Hippy Scotland! We ware under the improii,Ja that the English School Board had done so great a work that they might still carry it on for the common good. Mr. B.ILFOUR and his colleagues thought differently. But for the benefit of Scot- land they were good enough to think as so many of us thought for England. Here the County Council, which knows nothing by experience of education, which was already overburdened w ith work,has been set upas the educational authority. This blunder is good enough for us, but not for Scotland. Why the Scots may have School Boards and why the English:•may not have them it was no part of Mr. MURRAy's business to explain, it has nothing to do with the religious difficulty, at any rate. The Scottish School Board is to be elected on the same day with the Scottish County Council. This is a notable tribute to the power of the Caledon ian members to keep two important things well apart, although they demand attention at the same moment. But the object of the Government in England seems to have been to take care that whatever the English electors might think about on election day, it should not be education. County Coun- cils are elected for a variety of purposes, and by throwing in education it was doubtless anticipated that this would get the least attention of all. That is the delightfully rational manner in which English Tories deal with by far the most impor- tant branch of the national welfare. It would never do in Scotland, where the people are notori- ously better educated than they are in the South. Perhaps the reflections inspired by Mr. GRAHAM MURRAY'S Bill may have a chastening effect upon English opinion.</p>
        <p>We are glad to note that Mr. Whitham's recent weighty words in our columns with regard to Friendly Society and Trades Union meetings being held in publiz-houses have already borne fruit. At the annual meeting an Monday of the oldest , local Foresters Court, Councillor A. Peacock said he should like to see the Courts united in a grand effort to provide themselves with a proper meeting place ot their own, and altogether apart from the public-house. It is to be sincerely hoped that the suggestion will ultimately materialise,for there is little doubt that, if the Foresters led the way, other Friendly Societies would follow the move- ment for dissociating themselves from licensed premises. Temperance men, as we have previously pointed out, can accomplish real and lasting good by bringing about such a reform as this. It is not a big thing, but it is one of those practical steps in the right direction which guarantees a satisfactory return. Unfortunately one cannot say the same of all temperance movements. By striving for an ideal many opportunities for doing good by the way are missed.</p>
        <p>Week by week the indignation at the much dis- cussed Chinese labour ordinance grows apace, and the latest local protest comes from s representa- tive gathering of trade unionists. The working- men who assembled io strong force in the Co-opera- tive Hall on Tuesday evening were perfectly unanimous in their condemnation of the Govern- ment's action. The protest will lose nothing by its non-political character. On the contrary it serves to emphasize the strong hostility which has been aroused against the ordinance in all quarters. Mr. Robert Pierpoint must surely not have been acquainted with the feeling ef his constituents when he answered to the party whip and helped ' the Government to pass the measure with the de grading conditions attaching to it.</p>
        <p>The ancient Festival of Spring is returning, Parliament has risen, in a March wind, the last touches of paint are being added at the seaside and the morning papers publish lists of excur- sions ußder the inspiring heading, " Where to Spend Easter." " Hope springs eternal in the human breast," and traders and others, after a fortnight of bright weather, are sanguine of a better season than the last. London Lancashire, the Midlands, Wales, the south coast—spirits are everywhere rising, like the sap in the tree, at the promise of Spring and the coming holidays. Brighton has just finished one season and is about to commence another. London offers attractions for cousins from the country. Blackpool, " the Brighton of the North," with its wires of promenade, is, with a great show of energy, completing another one; and the operatives of Lancast ire and the Midlands, with that relish for "having their fling" which is said to provide them with contented reminiscence in the workhouse, are preparing for the spring exodus to the coasts of east and west. The weather, however, at the time of writing is apparently susceptible of all the changes of April. Whatever it is for Easter, the invincible cheerfulness of .the Britisher may be relied on to make the best of it. The month of showers and blossoms is upon us,the ascending year is getting clear of the ruts, larks are rising out of every meadow, hot cross buns are being advertised, and the world and his wife are betaking themselves to the seaside. As the search is for some of life's little compensations, we may hope the quest will be a satisfactory one.</p>
        <p>Lord Grey's Public House Trust Association though only three years old, is a thriving aud lusty organisation. Warrington temperance workers have reason to be speclally interested in the work of the Assoch.tion, for in the Feathers Hotel the town possesses one of the half-dozen " Trust " houses in Lancashire. Tney will, therefore, be glad to learn from the annual report of the Asso- ciation—a compendious volume of nearly 200 pages—that" progress during 1903 has been steady and satisfactory." Twenty-nine Trust companies are now working in England, one in *Wales, eleven in Scotland, and one in Ireland. These companies, with a subscribed capital of over £300,000, have brought under their manage- ment no less than 130 public-houses, while the movement is also spreading to the colonies, to Germany and to America. The fear expressed in some quarters that Trust management might tend to increase the national consumption of drink is shown to be unwarranted by facts,and evidence is given of the influence exercised in favour of the higher temperance by properly conducted Trust houses. "There is no doubt" writes the secretary, " that,were the methods of the ordinary publican adopted, the sale of drink a': most of our inns would go up with a bound."</p>
        <p>As the result of the recent election of Guardians, Warrington has three lady representatives on the Board, and this fact must be especially gratifying to those who are desirous of seeing a fair propor- tion of women assisting in the administration of the Poor Laws. There are many details connected with the cAre and oversight of women and children which women Guarditins are more fitted by nature and training to take in hand than men. It is therefore eminently desirable that every Board of Guardians should include members of both - Miss Constance Broadbent has succeeded in con- y in ing the electors of Latchford Ward of this fact, if we are to judge by the polling on Monday. It is, perhaps, unfortunate that the Board is to lose the services of such a well-tried servant as Mr. George Pilling, but he is himself largely to blame, for leaving Fairfield Ward, where his seat was practically safe, in order to seek the suffrages of the Latchford electors. The public career of the two new lady representatives on the Board will be followed with interest, but like Mrs. Clay, they will no doubt fully justify the confidence of the ratepayers.</p>
        <p>The report presented to the governors of the Warrington Infirmary and Dispensary last week is satisfactory, so far as it goes. The debit balance ha l been redaced by £l5O during the twelve months under review, notwithstanding the fact that extra expenditure necessitated by the intro duction of electric lighting, an electric lift, and the Rontgen ray apparatus, has been met out of current revenue. A more serious difficulty now confronts the Board of Management in the impera- tively necessary work of extension. Tne growth of the town and the steady increase in population calls for the provision of extra beds, and this necessitates additions to the existing premises. The subject has been already under consideration, and the President (&amp;Ir. P4rr) makes the wise sug- gestion that steps should be immediately taken. to raise the necessary funds, and then consider how best the money might be laid out. Despite the depression in trade we do not think such a press- ing and urgent appeal on behalf of the town's premier charity will be made in vain.</p>
        <p>However hard it may be to realize the fact, the " merrie- month of May" will soon be upon us. Active preparations are already being made for the parade of horses which we in Warrington are quite contsnt should take the place of old time revels. Given that all-important condition—fine weather--the event promises to in every way worthy of the town. There will be several addi • tional classes and, as usual, inducements in the shape of handsome prizes are being offers(' to drivers to compete. It is gratifying to learn that the committee is meeting with success in its efforts to foster kindly treatment of horses. If only to encourage such a commendable object, the parade is worthy of cordial support. The need for funds this year is more urgent than usual, but we are sure the subscribers will rise to the occasion and see that the work is not hampered through stinted resources.</p>
        <p>Rational Association Friendly Society. The annual supper and smoking concert in connec- tion with the Warrington branch were held at the Waggon and Horses Inn, Buttermarket-street. There was a good attendance of members and friends. After supper, Mr. James Everett presided, and after the usual loyal toasts had been submitted, Mr. C. W. Hibbert proposed "The Rational Association." Mr. James Everett replied, and spoke of the great pro- gress of the society. At the present time they had a membership of 120,000, and a capital of £482,000. (Applause.) The Warrington branch had a member- ship of 150 adults and 50 juveniles. He ought to say that result was in a very large measure due to their excellent Secretary (Mr. A. Lmgard). (Applause). Other toasts follow- ed, including "Kindred Orders," given by Mr. A. Ellis,and acknowledged by Mr. George Acourt. During the evening a capital programme of music was rendered, Mr. Thomas Jones presiding at the piano. The vocalists were:—Messrs. J. E. Poole Moore, Robert Grounds, A. Wilde (concertina solo,) Mr. Sanders supplied the humour of the evening. —At the close the Secretary (Mr. A. Lingard)proposed , a hearty vote of thanks to all who had helped to make the gathering a success, and said he was quite sure they had met together that evening, with the best intentions of furthering the interests of the branch and the Society, and scattering abroad a knowledge of i their principles to get new blood n their ranks.</p>
        <p>" All the money I get I want for myself "was the candid avowal of Alfred Pitt, when accused of allowing his wife and three children to become chargeable to the workhouse. On being sentenced,at Southwark on Wed- nesday, to a month's imprisonment he complained bitterly that a month vvas not long paougn to allow him to have a good look round the prison.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Personal. Mr. Cyril A. Spencer, who is at present a student at the Lichfield Theological College, has accepted a curacy at the Parish Church, and will commence work i n Warrington in Advent next.</p>
        <p>Musketry Training. It is announced that the depot troops of the Man- chester Regiment from Ashton-under-Lyne, and the depots from Warrington, will carry out their annual course of musketry at Altcar, while the troops from Bury, Lancaster, and Preston, will go to Fleetwood. Warrington Bowling League.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the newly-form-edWarrington Bowling League was held at the Horse and Jockey Inn, oa Tuesday evening. A code of rules was approved,an4 titi was decided to play the first round of the compel on on April 16th. Mr. W. J. Shaw was elected a vce-pr esident.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Young People's Society. The closing social in connection with this societi was held on Monday evening when the annual election of officers took place. Mr. William Brown presided The reports presented by the hon. secretary (Mr. James Gauld) and the hon. treasurer (Mr. Walter Booth) were considered very satisfactory, notwith- standing a slight diminution in the membership. The Rev. John Yonge was re-elected president; Rev. Harry Wall, Messrs. J. Ball, C. Eccles, E. Pickavance G. Wallington, and R. Wyllie, vice-presidents; and Messrs. James Gauld and Walter Booth were re-elected to their respective posts. The committee was voted for by ballot and the result was the re-election of the existing committee. Refreshments were handed round, and the remainder of the evening was agreeably whiled away with gam es and musical items.</p>
        <p>BaCE BOOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER THE CLOCIL—AdVt • Latchford Drowning Mystery. Coroner Brighouse held an inquest at the Police Station on Tuesday, on the body of a man unknown, which was recovered from the Ship Canal near Latch- ford Swing Bridge on Saturday afternoon.—P.C. Hardy stated that the body was in a very decomposed state, and was minus an arm. There was a heavy hob- nailed boot on one foot. One of the pockets of a pair of corduroy trousers contained a luggage voucher which referred to a travelling-bag and bundle at Bank Quay Station. On examination of these it was found that they contained clothing and linen, and had been deposited under the name of J. Connolly on JanuarY 13 of this year. Amongst the luggage was also part of a copy of the "Cork Weekly Examiner," under date December 19, 1903- Deceased appeared to be from 35 to 40 years of age. The Coroner stated that descriptions of the body had been sent to 44 different places, but it had not been identified.—A verdict of Found drowned" was returned.</p>
        <p>The Dispute In the Bedstead Trade. The dispute in the bedstead trade, which a week ago threatened to involve 5,000 workneople, has collapsed with remarkable suddenness. The employers, at a special meeting, decided that in view of the number of firms who had given way, it was hopeless to insist on the notices. On Tuesday morn- ing they were all withdrawn, and work is now pro- ceeding as usual. A strong hope is expressed that now the slack season hafi passed the attempt to abolish the men's 15 per cent. bonus will not be revived. The workmen employed at Messrs. Monks, Hall and Company's Standard Bedstead works and the Mersey Bedstead Company's works in Knutsford-road came out on strike at noon on Saturday. Shortly afterwards, representatives of the employers and the men conferred together, and the firms decided to with- draw the notice withholding the bonus. The whole of the men resumed work on Monday, so that for- tunately, there has been no cessation of work in coir sequence of the dispute.</p>
        <p>H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the Krsci oF ToNICS.-63, SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Legh-street Ptimitive Methodist Church. In connection with the Band of Hope a grand con- cert was given in the above schoolroom. Mr. N. 11. Matthews ably presided, and gave an interesting and instructive address to the children, urging them 5, become staunch teetotalers. Songs were given admirable style by Miss Lavinia Hewitt and Mr. Walter Perry ; Mr. Whitfield contributed a recitation; Miss G-regson and Miss Furnival recited and Mr. Sher burn gave phonographic selections. A feature of the programme was the first appearance of the Legli- street Brass Quartette (Messrs. Landor, Brooks, Sutton and Smith.) The admirable manner in which they played " Murmuring Breezes" and Clouds and Sunshine "reflects great credit on the conductor, Mr. Fred Podmore, who has carefully trained them. Recitations were given by the children. Miss Fanny Leicester ably accompanied. Mr. Henry Billinghata was the musical director.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse and Inquests In Public Houses. At an inquest held at Abram, Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner, referred to an inquest which had been held in a local church vestry, lie said that some persons imbued with deep temperance principles bad memoralised the Lancashire County Council to the effect that he was holding inquests in public houses, and that it was not in the interest or welfare of the community that they should be contaminated by go- ing to public-houses. The memorialists further though t fit to offer what he regarded as a gross insult to himself by insinuating that the fact that he happened to be the solicitor for the Southport Licensing Association accentuated the situation. When he first came into office in 1884 nearly all inquests were held in public-houses. Since then he had transferred them as far as possible to courtrooms and other places; and, roughly speaking, out of 500 inquests a year he only held 160 in public houses. He did not want the licensed victuallers to think that he had a finger in the pie. He was simply a servant to the County Council,and the main movers in the whole thing were the British Women's Temperance Associa- tion and a well-known temperance solicitor. Personally he had no objection to holding inquests in public houses, and certainly he did not feel that he was con- taminated. If they could get the same accommoda- tion elsewhere that they could get in a public house he had no objection to use it. Cheshire Cheese.</p>
        <p>An editor of " Punch," when told by a lady that his famous journal was not as good as it used to be, replied: "No, madam, it never was." We frepaently hear that Cheshire cheese is not as good as it used to be; and various explanations are given for the falling-off, and remedies suggested. It is satisfactory to find, however, says a writer in the "Manchester Evening News," from the last annual report of the Council of the Cheshire Dairy Farmers' Association, that the best Cheshire cheese is worth from 18s. to 20s. per cwt. more than the best imported cheese. This is but one instance of what is so often urged, that, if we only do our best—and improve on our best as we readily can do—we can raise better produce at home than we can import from abroad. The prosper- fty of our agriculture is in our own hands to make or mar. On the average, soil and climate are good enough. It is our science and economy that are at fault. The output of Che shire cheese last year,owing to the wet season, was below the average, but there was a good demand at satisfactory prices, owing to the high prices asked for American and Cauadiaa cheese.</p>
      </div>
