      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <p>Sale of Ground Rents. At the Blue Bell Inn on Wednesday,Messrs.Thomas Sutton and Sons conducted a sale by auction of ground rents amounting in the aggregate to £lOl 4s. 3d. per annum, secured by dwelling-houses and shops in or near Folly-lane, Crosfield-street and Latchford,of a gross total annual value in the aggregate of upwards of £975. The rents were offered in three lots and were purchased by Messrs. Faller, land agents, Manchester, for £1,267 10s., £9lO and £377 respectively.</p>
        <p>ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLA.SS MESTRA. WATERS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Fire Brigade Practice. An interesting fire brigade practice took place at Bewsey Locks on Saturday: afternoon. The Corpora- tion Brigade under Superintendent Turner and eight Imen turned out with engine "Major " at 3.17 p.m., and Crosfields' engine " Perfection " with Superin- tendent Gallon and eight men left the 'works at 3.20 p.m. Both brigades got to work smartly, and they seemed to be equal in point of time &amp;c., in steam raising, pumping and playing with the water out of the canal. Messrs.Crosfields' appliances were up-to date in every respe,ct,and a special display was given and, was appreciated by the Corporation Fire Engine Committee which was represented by Alderman Smethurst, .Councillors Arthur Peacock, Peter Peacock, 13 rough, Burton, Wilson, and Ashcroft. After an hour's good work, the committee conveyed their thanks to Dr. Ma rkel, (managing director of Messrs. Crosfields'), whr was present, for the friendly practice which had beel arranged between b.)th brig idas,and at the same time pointed out that with such an eMcient brigade near at hand to render assistamce to the town when necessary it should give the public conffience in the equipment p rovided in the district.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>GOOD FRIDAY.</head>
        <p>GOOD FRIDAY.</p>
        <p>HOW THE DAY WAS SPENL</p>
        <p>Though cold and boisterous, with several smart showers, the weather daring the greater part of yester- day was fine, and at times there was bright sunshine. The high wind made cycling a hard task rather than a pleasure, and there does not appe4r to have been any great exodus of holiday-makers. Most people who desired the benefits of fresh air c-mtented them- selves with walks in the town and neighbourhood. The football match between Warrington and Halifax drew a crowd of 5,090 persons. In the Anglican and Roman Catholic Churches the day was observed with great solonanitv and there were numerous services. At several Anglican Churches the Three Hours' Service, commencing at noon, was well attended, addresses being delivered on "The Seven Sayings from the Cross." The Rev. G. Longridge, of the Community of the Resurrection, was the preacher at the Parish Church. Services were also held in Wycliffe Congregational Church and Bold-street Wes- leyan Church. The service at Bold-street was in con- nection with the missionary anniversary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Latchford Baptist Chapel Tea Meeting.</head>
        <p>Latchford Baptist Chapel Tea Meeting.</p>
        <p>It has been a long-standing custom to hold the annua tea meeting in connection with Latchford Baptist Chapel on Good Friday, and the gathering which was held yesterday was even more successful than its pre- decessors have been. The small schoolroom was crowded to excess, and an excellent tea was served by-the lady members of the congregation. The subsequent proceedings were held in the chapel. The pastor (Rev. J. Fl. Matthews) occupied the chair, and he was accompanied on the rostrum by his father, Mr. Mathews, who is engaged in Christian work in London, the Rev. J. S. Hughes (Hill Cliffe) and the Rev. J. C. Elder (Acton Bridge). The Chairman briefly reviewed the past year's work, and stated that the various institutions were in a prosperous condition. The membership had now risen to 110, and there were two candidates for baptism. The finances, too, were never in a more satisfactory position.—Encouraging addresses were delivered by Mr. Matthews, the Rev. J. C. Elder, and the Rev. J. S. Hughes. The choir, under the con- ductorship of Mr. Wilkinson sang several anthems. Miss A. Rennison recited and solos were contributed by Miss Sarah Leigh, Miss Eva Grounds, Mrs. Fairhurst Mr. Appleton, Mr. James Hardy and Mr. Isaac Wilkin- son. Mr. Harrison presided at the organ. A vote of thanks was tendered to those who had taken part, and the proceedings terminated with the pronouncing of the Benediction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. HALIFAX. Exactly a year ago, Warrington and Halifax met on the Wilderspool enclosure, and the home team's victory over the then leaders of the league by a goal to nil was a notable one. Yesterday, a change had come o'er the scene. Both Halifax and Warrington are fighting their way in the cup-ties and it was mutually agreed between them not to make the contest a battle royal, but to play practically "A" teams, so that the first team players might not be unduly exerted before their important engagements of to-day. On the Warrington side, the only player appearing against Halifax who will play again to-day was Fish, who came on in place of Evans. The teams lined up as follows : Warrington :—Knight, back ;Fish,Mahoney Brooks and Kenyon three-quarters; Duckworth and Hockenhall, half-hacks; Fairhurst, Foster,Harmer. Gallop, Coleman, Jolley Antrobus and Hewitt, forwards. Halifax Albun back; Howarth, Ward, Jones and Morley, three-quarters; Barnett and Rigg, half-backs; Hammond, Brierley, Long, Longbottom, Slinger, Horbury, Lockhart and Wedgwood forwards. Mr. Renton officiated as referee. • Antrobus started operations against the wind and for a time the run of the play was of an even character. The first movement of note was a fine break away by Hockenhall who after beating all opposition gave the ball to Foster but that player knocked on and spoiled a glorious chance of scoring. Ward took up a dribbling move- ment on the other side and matters looked dangerous until Fish saved cleverly.' Warring- ton were penalised near their own 25 and Albon had little difficulty, with the strong wind behind hirn,in kickinga goal. Fish restarted and Coleman charged down the return kick. Immediately afterwards the home team was again penalised and Albon tried once more to lower the Warrington goal but failed on this occasion. A mark by Lockhart gave Albon another opportunity and this time he succeeded in placing a magnificent goal. A passing movement between Rigg, Jones and Howarth, almost ended in another score for Halifax. The home team were unable to make any headway against the wind and at half-time they were four points in arrear. Rigg re-started, and after some loose play Fish kicked across, and Kenyon taking the ball at his toes kicked over the line and scored a fine try. Fish failed to give Warrington the lead with the goal kick. The hpme team continued to make good use of the wind. and Antrobus unsuccessfully attempted to drop a goal. erude passing by the Warrington backs spoiled good openings and Halifax getting into the Warrington 25 gave some trouble until Duckworth kicked into the open, and Fish getting possession screwed across to Kenyon's end. The ball rolled over the line and Kenyon by a lightning run grounded the ball amid a host of oppon- ents. Fish added a goal. Warrington now pressed almost continuously and from a penalty Fish adrled another goal. The final iirrived with the score: War- rington ten points, Halifax four points.</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S LIM ax.r. LuA.GUE.—On Tuesday afternoon at Mr. Joseph Mountleld's house, Walton-road, a good number of ladies assembled and it was decided to form a Women's Liberal Leagne and to ask Mrs. Henry Roberts to become president. Mrs. E. England was elected secretary, Miss M. Bouchier, assistant secretary and Mrs. E. Lloyd Jones, treasurer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <p>CRAWFORD'S</p>
        <p>OVAL</p>
