      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, April 2, 1904</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April 2, 1904</p>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.]</p>
        <p>young gentlemen with wealth and ability and courage would fight some of our Tory strongholds as Mr. Acland and Mr. Briggs are fighting the Richmond and Thirsk and Mahon Divisions, they would have their reward. The mischief of it is that so many of our agricultural constituencies are allowed to go uncontested. The expense of a contest is so great and the work so arduous and thankless that the Tories are often allowed a walk over and some voters never record their votes in a life time. From what I have seen and heard in my rambles I would not let a siligle Tory seat go unchallenged at the next election. If our educational work is only carried on vigorously right up to the polling day there will be. plenty of surprise packets like Mid- Hertfordshire falling into our hands. There will be plenty of safe Tory seats captured, and the leaders of many of our forlorn hopes will find themselves at the head of the poll.</p>
        <p>former played with characteristic pluck. Davies was the best man on the side, and there is no questioning the fact that the ex-Salfordian is proving himself a valuable acquisition to the Warrington ranks. He seems to be improving weekly, and for resourcefulness would take a bit of beating.</p>
        <p>The League table up-to-date is as follows : For. Against. P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. Pts Salford 29...21... 6... 2..49 69-305...12 21-87...44 Bradford Hunslet 29...20... 8... L.,42 45-219...17 29-121..41 Br'ghton R 28...18... 6... 4...27 71-267...23 19-103..40 Leeds 27...17... 6... 4...22 39-161...12 25-99...38 Oldham 29 16. 10 3...28 40-176...13 20-85...35 Warrington 28...14...11... 3...24 40-168...20 23-10..31 Hull K't'n R 29-13...14 2...21 29-129...19 37-149..28 Halifax 29 .12 .14. 3..22 21-107...24 24-120..27 Swinton Runcorn ... 29...11...17... 1...28 28-140...34 45-203..23 Widnes 3O. 9..16. 5...17 21— 97...26 53-211..23 Wigan Batley) 29. 9..17. 3...23 25-121...43 48-230..21 Hull 2B. 9..17. 2...18 29--123-45 48-234..20 Leigh Keighley 28... 7...16... 5...27 15— 99...37 55-239..19 Hudderstrld2B... 6...22... 0...13 31-119...45 62-276..12</p>
        <p>Wigan and Leigh played a pointless draw on the ground of the latter on Saturday, but yesterday Wigan won at home against Leigh by 3 goals tl nil. These results have some bear- ing on the West Lancashire Competition. War- rington still possess the best chance, and should improve their record at the expense of St. Helens on Monday, when four points will be at stake. This match has only been re-arranged this week, and should prove an interesting Easter Monday fixture. The West Lancashire table to date is as follows : Pts.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. for agst.Pts. Wigan Warrington 5 ... 4 ... 0 ... 1 42-14 ... 9 Widnes 7 ... 1 ... 3 ... 3 18-31 ... 5 *St. Helens 2 ... 1 ... 1 ... 0 8-14 ... 4 Runcorn Leigh *St Helens matches, not being home and away engagements, each count four points.</p>
        <p>Warrington successfully brought off their League game with Halifax yesterday and managed to win by 10 points to 4. Both clubs, being interested in to- day's cup-ties, played reserve teams. Warrington had however, the valuable assistance of Fish, who turned out in place of Evans and won the match by twice kicking judiciously across for Kennyon to score, and placing a couple of goals. Halifax were immeasur- ably superior in the scrummage, and thus again em- phasized the fact that Warrington must have bigg and better forwards from somewhere if they are ever to be a successful club. The backs got few opportuni- ties but Kenyon and Mahoney played good football. Archie Rigg, who captained the visitors, was the best man on his side.</p>
        <p>The Northern Union International game will take place on Tuesday at Wigan, the following tiams hav- ing been definitely picked: England.—W. Little (Yorkshire County) ; F. Spottis- woode (Cumberland County), R. Wilson (Lancashire County), J. Lomas (Lancashire County), and J. Fish (Lancashire County) ; J. Baxter (Lancashire County) and G. H. Marsden (Yorkshire County) ; A. Starks (captain) (Yorkshire County), P. Tunney (Lanoashire County), Jack Riley (Yorkshire County), S. Walker (Cheshire County), and J. Ferguson (Lancashire County). Other Nationalities.—Dan Smith (Wales) ; A Hogg (Scotland), D. Harris (W-ales),T. D. Llewellyn (Wales), and J. Dechan (Scotland) ; Sam and Will James (Wales) ; J. Rhapps (Wales), A. Laidlaw (Scotland), J. G. Moffatt (Scotland), " Dai " Rees (Wales), and G. Frater (captain),(Wales).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>SOCIAL SERMONETTES</head>
        <p>SOCIAL SERMONETTES</p>
        <p>FOR WORKING MEN,</p>
        <p>BY JOHN TAYLOR. Author of " Search Lights on Land, Labour,</p>
        <p>ancl Social Problems."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>POLITICAL WORK IN THE</head>
        <p>POLITICAL WORK IN THE</p>
        <p>~VILLAGES.</p>
        <p>"To speak his mind is every freeman's right, In peace, in war, in council,or in fight."—Pope. . For the last six months, or more, I have spent most of my time lecturing and addressing political meet- ings in our agricultural villages, and doing what Lord Rosebery calls "spade work" among the rural electors. The sphere of my operations extended from Darlington in the North, to Weymouth in the South, and from the Humber to the Irish Sea, so I have had variety enough in all conscience. I have come into contact with all sorts and conditions of men, and I have had exceptional opportunities of seeing and hearing for myself what is going on in our villages, and how the rural electors regard the political outlook. I have spent a month in Yorkshire, lecturing every night in the Thirsk, and Mahon, and Richmond Divisions, with several nights at Hull and Normanton. I had three weeks in Dorsetshire, and a fortnight in rural Cheshire, and two or three weeks in the Eastern and Midland Counties, so lam getting about seasoned to that kind of political mission work.</p>
        <p>1.-EVERYWEIERE I FIND A SPIRIT OF UNREST AND DISSATISFACTION WITH THINGS AS THEY ARE.</p>
        <p>There has come a marvellous change over the tone and temper of the people. Three years ago they were like a wilful steed, that gets the bit between its teeth, and bolts, and no man living can guide it. To-day they are docile enough, and they will listen eagerly, aad cheer to the echo, sentiments for which they would have mobbed me,and half murdered me in those days. The after-math of the war has chastened them, and subdued them, and the war fever is as dead as Queen Anne._ _ _ . .</p>
        <p>The old-fashioned Tories who have rsligioqs convic- tions, and pious opinions, have had their faith rudely shaken by their own leaders, who have" no settled con- victions, and who can airily affirm any principle to-day, and deny it to-morrow, and who boast that they keep "an open mind "about all things. Thefiscal controversy has unsettled men's political opinions, and shaken their faith, and beclouded their vision, and opened the floodgates of 'controversy, till the average elector does not know where he is, or what he believes, or what course he ought t. steer. The spirit of unrest is to be found everywaere, and it begets in men a spirit of enquiry that makes them willing to lend their ears to any man who has convictions of his own, and who knows how to state them clearly.</p>
        <p>IL-THE RIIRAL_ELECTORS APPRECIATE THIS</p>
        <p>POLITICAL SPADE WORK.</p>
        <p>My latest campaign in Yorkshire has been cbnfined almost exclusively to the villages in the Richmond and the Thirsk and Mahon Divisions. Mr. F. D. Acland and Mr. J. J. Briggs, the two prospective Liberal candi- dates, are working like Trojans, and winning golden opinions everywhere. They are holding two, and some: times three, meetings every night, and they stand the racket well. In the majority of cases I had a meeting all to myself, but occasionally the candidate would pay us a brief visit, and give us a short address, and drive off to another meeting several miles away. It is no joke carryinc, on these campaigns in the depth of winter with a fresh home every day, and a strange bed every night, and your meals whenever you are lucky enough to catch them. I have slept in lordly mansions, and in creepy ghost-haunted bedrooms, and in comfortable hotels, and 1 have often been thankful for a roosting place in some lowly cottage, or the village inn. I have driven across wild moorlands, where we lost our way, and we had to strike our last match to read the name 9n some lonely sign post at the parting of the ways: I have taken charge of a meeting with orders to talk tili the candidate and his friends came from another meet- ing to relieve me. On one occasion the whole party got lost,and never turned up till I had talked two mortal hours by the clock.</p>
        <p>But all these inconveniences are forgotten when there is such a spirit of enquiry abroad as I found in my Yorkshire campaign. I was sent one night to Aysgarth in Wensleydale, to lecture for Mr. Acland,in the village schoolroom. It was a pitch dark night, and I had .to tramp a mile or more, through strange roads, ankle deep with snow and slush. A friendly policeman took charge of me, and piloted me to the school, or I never should have found my way at all.</p>
        <p>When we arrived, the room was lighted with two feeble oil lamps that made darkness visible. I was re- ceived with a rousing cheer of welcome, and when my eyes got accustomed to the gloom I found out that this place was packed with an ever crowd of farmers and labourers and shepherds,who had come miles to hear me over the wild fells and the lonely moorlands. They sat in breathless silence for more than an hour, while I showed them what Protection did for our fathers, and what it inevitably would do for them. Then they gave me acordial vote of thanks, and trudged home to talk these matters over with their friends and neighbours. On another occasion I was sent to Easingwold, and told I had to lecture in the Town Hall, on "Eight Years of Tory Rule.". When I landed there I found hundreds of people in that large hall, eagerly waiting to hear what I had got to say. As I denounced the Fiscal Policy and the destruction of our School Boards, and the muddle in South Africa, and the proposal to corn- ' pensate the publicans, and the introduction of John Chinaman to South Africa, they cheered me to the echo, and when they got outside they held a dozen little meet- ings of their own, and discussed what I had said for an hour. _</p>
        <p>Now I want to know what it was that brought all these village electors out on dark, dirty nights to these political lectures. It certainly was not my name and personality that draw theca. It was that eager earnest spirit of enquiry that has come over men who have been fooled and blinded and misled so long, that they are determined to find the truth, if they have to seek it diligently. lII.—THE RURAL ELECTOR Is .OPPOSED TO PROTECTION.</p>
        <p>I have met many prosperous well-to-do farmers who have been dazzled by the promise of high prices for their corn, and who would vote for Protection if they were quite sure that it would put money into their pockets. But it is a striking fact that the fiscal policy has not captured the agricultural labourer,and it will not win his vote at the next general election. Some of the strongest arguments against it have come from the lips of workmen who remember the days of Protection. In one of my meetings at Hull an old man told us that he had paid five shillings and five- pence for a stone of flour when he was a tanner's labourer earning ten shillings a week. The same kind of workman earns a pound now, and flour is selling at sixteen pence a stone. So that under Protection a man could not earn two stones of flour with a week's wages. Under Free Trade he can earn fifteen stones of flour in a week. In other words, Free Trade doubled that man's wages, and gave him more than seven times as much food for his labour.</p>
        <p>Such an object lesson as this made a profound impression on that meeting. it was one of those hard, telling facts that cannot be denied, and I found it good business to appeal to those living witnesses as often as I had the chance.</p>
        <p>In some of the villages I visited they have not had I a Liberal meeting for fifteen or twenty years, and I got a very freezing chilly reception every now and then. ' The parson and the squire do not organise mobs now to pelt us with rotten eggs or put us under the pump for presuming to teach them anything, But they sometimes refuse to let us have the use of the schoolroom, or they charge us three times as much for it as they would ask of anybody else. The old spirit of bigotry and intolerance is not dead, and it is hardly safe in many places for any man to avow himself a Liberal. I am treated as a sort of chartered libertine, and our friends the enemy will let me say what nobody else must say, but I get into some warm corners sometimes, when I go single handed, and alone, into the Tory strongholds. I went to one village where there was not a known Liberal in the place. My friends had hired the village school room, and announced me to lecture on "Free , Trade." I was alone, and I could not raise a chairman because no local man dared to be seen in my company. The parson and the squire turned up to hear me, and brought a big contingent of Tory farmers and labourers at their heels. They made no demonstration of any kind. They did not even give me a cheer of wel- come. They sat like waxwork images, or frigid corpses while I cracked my jokes, and warmed up to my work. Presently I said something that made them roar with laughter, and then I knew they were human beings. So I plied them with facts and arguments that they had evidently never heard before, for they leaned forward in their seats, and looked me through and through, as if they were afraid of missinr, a single word. Theni took Mr. Chamberlain's facts and fallacies • one by one, an I mercilessly knocked the sawdust out of them " till I raised a protest from some angry farmer who shouted, "That's a lie." T was devoutly thankful for this interruption,because it gave me the chance of giving that unlucky farmer a very bad quarter of an hour, and of saying ever so many things that I never should have said if he had let me alone. The meeting got excited over this little duel, and rewarded me at the finish with a rousing cheer. But it forgot to give me a vote of thanks. It broke up into three or four smaller meetings of " whole- hoggers," and" little piggers," and, " Protectionists," and" Free Traders," but it was evicient that the over- whelming majority of them were on my side. Wherever I go I find that the agricultural labourer knows the difference between a big loaf and a little one, and he is not going to vote for a little loaf at the next election. If I rightly read the signs of the times there is a great awakening among our agricultural labourers, :and if</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>Another infectious disease (though not notifiable) has sprung up in Warrington and bids fair to run the measles epidemic pretty closely. There have been occasional outbreaks in the past, a severe one being experienced in the year 1901, but it was effectually dealt with by a Batley physician. Those of my readers who are sufferers, will doubtless have recognised the complaint ere this,as Cup-tie fever, which attacks old and young alike, but usually spares the gentle sex. Unfortunately the same physician is not available on this occasion, but it is possible some skilful prac- titioner may be found in the wilds of Pontefract, or a liberal dose of the native product, liquorice, may do the trick. Somehow, however, I think the fever will have to run its course this time. It appears to have got too firm a hold to be cured by liquorice.</p>
        <p>The above medical disquisition really means that Warrington are playing Pontefract in the third round of the Northern Union cup-ties to-day and mean to win. They have not tried the liquorice town before, but as the football exponents of the neighbourhood are only in the second division, Warrington expect to secure the spoils of victory and thus, for ths third time, enter the semi-finals. I hear that the Pontefrac- tors are somewhat refractory, and do not intend to give in without a struggle. They settled Batley and why not Warrington? Just so ! But logic does not count for much in cup-tie football. Neither side is entitled to an overwhelming amount of confidence in my opinion, though Warrington's superior class should turn the scale.</p>
        <p>Excitement—or rather the fever—has been steadily growing in Warrington during the week, and a couple of special trains will convey several hundred War- ringtonians to the scene of action. They are leaving nice and early for the long journey and should reach their destination early enough on this occasion to give their champions the necessary encouragement. The two Headingley fiascos are being guarded against. . One is indicated above, and the other was concersed with the preparation of the players before the match. It will be well remembered that by a ridiculous lack of judgment on somebody's part, the team reached Headingley so early that the men had to sit and twiddle their thumbs for about an hour, during which time they got themselves into such a state of nervous- ness that they never made a decent show throughout that memorable match. This year, weather per- mitting, the players are going to walk to the ground and give themselves just time to prepare and dash in- to the fray.</p>
        <p>Pontefract are a much heavier team than Warring- ton, and this fact is sure to have an influence on the game. They defeated Batley forward, and will prob- ably do the same with Warrington, but I have no fear that they will bottle up the Warrington scrummages in the way Wigan did. I don't believe the colliers themselves could do that again. Still, with a set of forwards averaging about 13 stone the liquorice men are not to be held cheaply. The Warrington for- wards have only to show themselves possessed of that dash-up they exhibited last Saturday against Brad- ford, and the primrose and blue colours should be in the ascendant. The speed of the backs will have to be manifested in the style it was against Swinton.</p>
        <p>A. couple of Warrington emissaries were in Ponte- fract on Saturday where they carried on a high game of bluff. The home officials were sure Warrington would win easily, whereas the Warrington men were just as confident that it was three to one on Ponte- fract. They found their opponents in good trim when opposed to Normanton, but did not come away scared. By the way they are expecting great things of Fish in the Yorkshire town. His fame has preceded him and if he does not get about half-a-dozen tries and goals the liquorice lickers will be as much disappointed as Warrington—so they say! I sincerely hope they will not be disappointed in this respect, and Fish will doubtless do his best to oblige.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>Notes train Pontefract.</head>
        <p>Notes train Pontefract.</p>
        <p>Pontefract supporters are looking keenly forward to a good gams on the Park Avenue ground this after- noon; and the feeling is growing that the Warrington boys will not have the " safe thing" which perhaps they would naturally be excased from anticipating. True, it is a first division team versus a second.; it is true that Pontefract has not figured in the class of football in which Warrington has been moving for Ihe past six months; and it is true that the " liquorice boys" stand only low down in the Coin petition table. But all the same, Pontefract are on "top form" just now; they have played a consist- ently sound game all the season through, and the present comparatively low position is due solely to repeated bad luck at the beginning of the season. Since, however, the inclusion of Brunyard and Booth at half some eight or ten weeks ago. they have not lost more than one or two matches, and this pair will need all the careful watching that can be bes towed upon them.</p>
        <p>It is generally conceded at Pontefraet that the Warrington three-quarters will be smarter in most departments of the game than the Pontefract quartette but the latter are keen tusslers. The liquorice hopes are chiefly centred in the front rank, who are a sturdy lot, and in the pink of condition, to whom chiefly the present position in the ties is due. If Warrington forwards can hold their opponents, there is little doubt they will win, but Pontefract folks think that where Batley signally failed, Warrington will not do much better. It is true, however, that Auty the Batley centre, had one splendid opening which, unlike him, he did not make the most of: and if Warrington have a similar opening they are not likely to Ao that, but still,Pontefract were not lucky in beating Batley so thoroughly: they deserved the victory, and the score about indicated the run of the play.</p>
        <p>The Pontefract team ill be the same as defeated Brighouse and Batley. The following particulars of the weights and heights of the playing members of the Pontefract team may be interesting reading to-day.</p>
        <p>Position Full-back</p>
        <p>Name Weight Height st. lb. ft. in. T. Smith ... 12 5 510</p>
        <p>Lt-Wing three-quarter Connell ... 11 13 510 Rt-Wing ” Davison ... 11 9 5 8% Centre /t Driscoll ... 10 2 571</p>
        <p>T. Ward ... 11 3 5 9- falf-back ~ ' BW. rnßnoyaortdh::: 191 27 55 67 ” Forward J. Debney... 15 1 511 ” j.yre ... 13 12 5 9i ” Cole ... 13 12 5 8 ” J. Sims ... 13 5 5 7 ” Foster ... 13 7 511 ~ Parkinson... 13 0 5 10 ” , Cookson ... 12 6 5 8 ” Robinson ... 11 6 5 8 ” Johnson ... 12 1 5 9i</p>
        <p>Two Warrington officials visited the Pontefract v. Normanton match on Saturday last, but they had better not place too much reliance upon their observ- ations, as the match was not treated seriously by the home players throughout. They just left enough marffin in hand to win for the sake of the two points —arid that was all.</p>
        <p>The Bradford match last Saturday seemed a very tame affair after the cup-ties, but it was rather too generous of Warrington to present their opponents with a couple of points by playing only half the first team. It is doubtless advisable to give one or two men a rest, but for seven to stand down was rather too much in the way of caution. As events turned out Bradford could only scrape home by a goal to ml, and if either Isherwood or Dickenson had been playing I think Warrington would have won. They had as much of the game as their opponents, but want of finish at threequarter lost them many an opportunity of scoring. None of the substituted backs, Knight, Mahoney, Hockenhall, or Duckworth distinguished themselves, though the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>DRAUGHTS.</head>
        <p>DRAUGHTS.</p>
        <p>• MONKS, HALL V. FLETCHER, RCSSELL'S.—PIayed at the rooms of Monks, Hall last Friday evening. FLETCHERS' MONKS, HALL. Wins. Draws. Wins. 2 J. Hamblett .... ..... 0 H. Powell 0 1 J. Connolly 0 J. Blackburn° 0 T. Norbury 0 W. Talbot 0 W. Wych 1 W. Talbot, Sen 1 2 A. Spencer 0 E. Pritchard 2 C. Johnson 0 Absentee</p>
        <p>CROSFIELD'S V. MONKS, HALL AND Co.—Played on Monday. Result : CROSFIELD'S. MONKS'S. Wins. Wins.</p>
        <p>Draws.</p>
        <p>*J Robinson 2 S. Dent 0 W. E.Clegg 0 G. Bowman 2 1 H. Bell 0 W. Talbot, jun. 2 W. Tynan 0 F. Ireland 0 2 S. Thompson 0 Absentee</p>
        <p>* One ;Itto nit oltya I. WARRINGTON LEAGUE._</p>
        <p>The following m‘tches were played during the past week at the rooms of the first named clubs. By theii victory over Richmonds on Monday' night Cairo-street have ensured their position at the head of this league, and winners of the "Naylor "Cup. RICIIMONDS. CAIRO-STREET. Wins Draws - Wins 0 M. Gorman • 1 W. Tomkinson 1 1 W. Rigby 0 G. Rowe 1 H. Richardson 1 1 W. Loftus 0 W. Potter 1 1 W. O'Brien 0 J. Green 0 F. Kenny 0 P. Hankinson</p>
        <p>_ PIDGATE. CROSFIELDS. Wins Draws Wins 2 J. Roberts 0 S. Dent 0 0 J. Pomfrey 0 J Robinson ' 2 G. Holcroft 1 C. Roberts 1 A. Williams 1 T, Smith 0 W. Tynan 1 2 .T. Smith 2 H. Holcroft</p>
        <p>FLETCHERS.</p>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>-- -----. Wins. Draws. Wins. 0 J. Hamblett 1 J. Connolly .0 NV. Trigger 0 M. Royle 0 J. Blackburne 1 J. Jghnson 0 T Smith 0 T. Norbury</p>
        <p>The return match between Cairo-street and Warring- ton, which should have been played on Thursday; resulted in a "walk-over" for the former, as the War- rington Club was unable to raise a team, several of the members being prevented from attending by business engagements. Ihe League Competition being now oompleted, Cairo- street came out victors by a Majority of five points,with Richmonds a good second, while Fletchers and Warring- ton "tie" for third place. The competition has aroused great interest ; and it speaks well for the clubs that they have all kept their engagements, in spite of adverse results, as in the case of Crosfield's. Such perseverance will ensure eventual success; and there is no doubt that the standard of play is improved all round.:since the start ; and that future seasons will bring about a still further advancement in the standard of local draughts playing, and enable Warrington to enter a team in the County Competition for the " Love " trophy, with some hopes of success. LEAGUE TABLE. FEIAL RESULTS. Matches. Gaines. P. W. L. D. W. L. D. Pts. Cairo-street Richmonds Fletcherss l2 6 6 0 ... 66 82 28 ... 12 Warrington Padgate Monks Hall Crosfields ENGLISH CHAMPIONSHIP TOURNEY'. The seventh (fourth biennial) tourney for the English championship will commence to-day (Saturday) at the Coal Exchange, Market Place, Manchester. 'Proceed- ings will be opened at 10 p.m. by Mr. Jos. Townsend, of Rochdale, president of the English Draughts Associa- tion. Following is the official list of entries, which closed last Saturday :—A. Hynd. J. Hynd. junr., W. BlakeleY, T. Worthington, W. T. Jones, R. Parry, and J. Lang- ford, of Manchester; J. Roberts and F'. Dunne, of War- rington: J. A. Milne, Eccles • H. Morrall, Liverpool ; W. H. Waller, Blackburn; T. Dean, Padiham ; J. Alexander, Birmingham ; W. J. Wray, Halifax , T. Goldsboro, Bradford ; A. Jordan, London; and R. T. Ward, Nottingham. As regards num.bers,the present tourney constitutes a record. As will be seen from the above list,Warrington is well represented. F. Dunne is an old contestant in these tournies, having played in the first o id at Manchester in 1885, and has twice .reached the prize list. His old pupil, Mr. J. Roberts, now makes his first attempt, and will no doubt give a good account of him- self. Play will be continued every day at the same place until the conclusion of the tourney, excepting on Tuesday, when the large Hall of the Exchanse Hotel .Fennel-street, has been engaged for the occasion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>WESLEYAN FOREIGN MISSIONS.</head>
        <p>WESLEYAN FOREIGN MISSIONS.</p>
        <p>Services and Meetings at Bold-street.</p>
        <p>This week the congregation at Bold-street Wesleyan Church have been celebrating the foreign missionary anniversary. There were good attendances at Sunday's services when sermons urging the importance of mis- sionary work among the heathen were preached in the morning by the Rev. G. H. Carnson and in the evening by the Rev. R. Wilkins Rees. On Monday evening the Rev. R. Wilkins Rees lectured on "Japan and the Japanese" in the school- room. Yesterday morning a special service was held, the Rev. Henry A. H. Barley, a missionary from Calcutta, preaching. The annual public meeting took place last evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Japan and the Japanese.</head>
        <p>Japan and the Japanese.</p>
        <p>LECTURE BY THE REV. R. WILKINS REES.</p>
        <p>The Rev. R. Wilkins Rees delivered an able lecture on" Japan and the Japanese "in the schoolroom on Monday evening, but although the subject should possess particular interest at the present moment, the attendance was not large. Mr. J. C. Holland presided. The lecture was illustrated by a series of beautiful I views which helped one to realise the" wealth of natural beauty and the wonders of the strange and ancient civilzation of Japan"— as the lecturer aptly put it. Mr. Rees alluded to the rapidity of the development of modern ideas and institutions in the country. Things which seemed to be centuries old in other countries were only thirty years old in Japan. Almost on the same page one might find the names of Prime Ministers and feudal lords. Changes which had taken hundreds of years to accom- plish in England and other lands had been effected in Japan in a comparatively short period. The inhabit- ants now had all the machinery of that Government which required so many years to build up lin England. The great feudal system in Japan only expired on coming into contact with modern thought in 1868. Proceeding to describe a series of charming scenes, the lecturer pointed out that a delightful trait in the character of the Japanese was their love of gardens. The poorest people managed to grow something in the way of flowers. Another pleas- ing feature was the readiness with which those who possessed fine grounds threw them open to the public, and the privilege was never abused as it sometimes was in England. Some of the amusing contrasts in the Japanese mode of life as compared with Western ideas were briefly touched upon. The audience next beard with some surprise that a hard-working car- penter in Japan could earn the munificent sum of is. per day. It was even said that some men would set fire to buildings in order to obtain work. It was a rare sight to find drunken men in the streets in Japan. A remarkable fact WAS that each year about 200 earthquake shocks were felt in the capital. There were practically three religions in Japan, and they were so accommodating in some respects that a man could pro- fess all the three at the same time. He could be either a Shintoist, a Buddhist or a Confucian. There was no religion in Shintoism: There was no moral code and no idea of God in it. It was simply worship of ancestors and of certain natural forces. Buddhism was introduced into the country from China. The strange thing about it was that in the country where it was founded—lndia —it had practically no followers. Buddhism came into Japan in about the sixth century and practically banished Shintoism. About a hundred years ago ShintOism again began to come to the fore, and to-day it was really the State religion of Japan. The rev. gentleman urged his hearers to make themselves acquainted with a few elementary facts connected with that religion which must have a marvellous interest for them all.</p>
        <p>The lantern was manipulated by the Rev. G. H. Cam- son. The usual votes of thanks concluded a pleasant meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Public Meeting Last Evening.</head>
        <p>Public Meeting Last Evening.</p>
        <p>Lsst evening the annual public meeting was held in the church, Mr. George Weaver (senior circuit steward) presiding overa fair attendance. He was supported on the rostrum by the Revs. Henry A. H. Barley (from Calcutta), G. H. Carnson, R. Wilkins Rees and W. C. J. Neville, Messrs. W. P. Silcock and J. G. Withinshaw.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>AN INTERESTING REPORT.</head>
        <p>AN INTERESTING REPORT.</p>
        <p>In his annual report the Rev. R. Wrixiss REES said: —I am glad to be able to state from information received from headquarters that the Wesleyan Methodist Church will declare an increase in its foreign missionary income for 1903 of be- tween £3,000 and £4,000, so that the total raised during the past year will be about £160.000. The returns, how- ever, are not yet quite completed. This increase of something like 24.000 (last year the increase was over £7,000) is less than had been honed for,but it is believed that the unfavourable weather of the autumn when most of our meetings are held, and the state of trade in the country—Lancashire especially—must have had no small influence upon the returns. A few points worth noting, apart from the income, are these : The West Indies are now once more part of our mission field. For eighteen years the West Indies had had their own conferences, but it has now been found necessary, in order to give the assistance whieh the Methodism of those islands re- quires, that the constitutions under which the West Indies were formed into separate conferences should be surrendered,and that Methodism there should return to its former position when it was under the direct con- trol of the conference in this country and the Foreign Missionary Society. This step has been found neces- sary mainly by the impoverished condition into which so many of the people of the West Indies have fallen during the recent years of commercial and agricultural depression in that part of our empire. In bvegone days the churches of the West Indies were the glory of our -Missionary Society. The glamourassociated with recent converts and a people newly passed from slavery has passed away; but we have there now fairly organized churches with a large proportion of men and women whose intelligence and piety are a foundation of hope for advancing Christian usefulness and power in the life of those islands, and perhaps in those,. the oldest missions of our church may be witnessed in the future, as in the past, some of the noblest triumphs of missionary Methodism. When the general report is issued one cheering item of intelligence will be a large increase of native members in the Transvaal. It will be further seen that the new mission in the province of Hunan, China, is making good pro- gress. Developments are also being made in the medi- cal work in connection with our missions, and it is hoped to make that a considerable feature in the pro- gramme of the immediate future. As to the amount raised in the Liverpool District, it:might suffice to say that the President of the Conference congratulated the District last Tuesday evening upon the honour of con- tributing a tenth part of the missionary income, as con- tributed in this country, and also a tenth part of the year's increase. The gross amount for 1903 from the Warrington circuit is £266 15s Bd, the largest amount ever'yet raised in the circuit. The amounts for the various places are :—Bold street, £l3 4s 4d, a decrease of 28 14s 8d; Bevisey road, £42 6s sd, an increase of £3 2s id; Sankey Bridges, £9 8s 4d, increase £1 9s 10d; Latchford, £lO .2.s 2d, increase £2 4s 4d ; Ellesmere street, £3 16s 3d, increase Os 9d; Lymm, £l5 Gs Oci, in- crease £4 3s 7d ; Penketh, £5O 5s Bd, increase £ll us Od Moore, £l7 16s 7d, decrease £5 7s 3d; Whitley,£lo 5s id increase, £1 14s ; Antrobus, £l2 19s 3d, increase 3s: , Appleton, £7 Os. 4d., increase 16s. 11d. ; Padgate, £32 Os. 3d., increase, £3 4s. 7d.; Martinscroft, £l, increase, 117 s. 9d.; Stockton Heath, £8 55., increase £2 3s. Bd. It is thus to be seen that only two places in the circuit reported a decrease. The decrease at Moore is more than fully accounted for by the death of Mr. John High- field, whose foreign missionary subscriptions alone amounted to over £6. The net increase in foreign mis- sionary income for the circuit during the past year is £l9 7s 9d, and it should be borne in mind that the income for 1902 was £l4 net more than t4at of the preceding year. If there had not been certain losses, almost wholly unavoidable, during thepast two years, the cir- cuit foreign missionary income for last year would be about £6O in advance of that of two years ago. The amount-213 4s. 4d.—from Bold-street was comprised of the following sums :—Public collections, £25 18s.; collected by Miss Ashdown, £2 ; collected by Miss Marion Weaver, £8 2s. 6d ; Juvenile Association (one third of the amount collected), £7 3s. 10d. Mr. Rees strongly urged the people generally throughout the circnigto send in their canvass papers without the least further delay so that the results of the recent canvass might be at once tabulated. This was reported to be of extreme importance as the canvass had been directed by Conference and the returns had to be arranged and forwarded to the Mission House at an early date. THE ADDRESSES. _</p>
        <p>The CRAIRMAN said the society which claimed their support was doing a splendid work. In his opinion it was thermost important of their connexional works,for there was no higher duty than to help forward the Christianising of the heathen. The society had been in existence about ninety-one years, and though it com- menced in a small way, and the missionaries had had to encounter many difficulties and privations, it had held on persistently, and was in a stronger position to-day than ever. To realize what foreign missions had done, theyl had only to look at places like the Fiji Islands where cannibalism was once rampant, but which were now civilized, and possessed a Government akin to our own.</p>
        <p>AThe Rev.HEsEtv A. H.BARLEY gave a highly interesting account of his six years' missionary work in the Province of Bengal, which, he said, was twice the size of Great Britain and had a population of 80 millions. The people were highly educated and very progressive. In 1902 there were 628 native Christians in the Calcutta Dis- trict, an increase in ten years of 363. He believed that had been greatly improved upon during the past yew. The speaker referred to the general progress of Christi- anitv in India and pointed out that Hindooism was not holding its ground or keeping pace with the increase in the population.</p>
        <p>The Usual votes of thanks concluded the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>CHINESE LABOUR.</head>
        <p>CHINESE LABOUR.</p>
        <p>THE PEOPLE'S IDOL.</p>
        <p>Steele said of Lady Elizabeth Hastings that "t know her was a liberal education;"to know Mr.Joseptt Chamberlain would be something more. It would tax the efforts of his dearest friend to really under- stand him; and if to-day he arrived at that happy estate he would on the morrow be flung into despair again. Clever, surprisingly clever, smart, up-to-date, he has no equal.A hard hitter,a wondertul wheedler he captivates all those with whom he comes into contact. There are many who would lay down their lives for him, who would go through another South African Campaign for him if only he appealed to them and their patriotism. It would not matter what the cause or the cost so long as" he "commanded them. Such is human nature. But, surely, that kind of slavery cannot go on for ever. And what a man—even Mr. Joseph Chamberlain—states one day cannot be contradicted by him another day without arousing the suspicions of those who would follow him implicitly. And this question of cheap Chinese Labour is so wrapt up with the late Colonial Secretary that to discuss it without introducing into the arena- "the people's idol" is altogetherimpossible. We regret that, on account of the condition of his health, he has been compelled to leave the country while such momentous questions are being discussed. We do not remember having heard his voice during the Education Controversy. He condemned the introduction of Asiatic Labour in South Africa once, but he has slept since then and may have had another" dream." He will probably wake up soon and find his friends his bitterest foes. THE BOER WAR. To understand the matter clearly it will be necessary to revert to the Boer War—even though it does offend the sensitive natures of those who clamoured for it. Let us first of all see what Mr. Chamberlain thought of the Boers. When a Minister of Mr. Gladstone's Cabinet, Mr. Chamberlain said :—" The Boers are not naturally a warlike race ; they are a homely, indus- trious, but somewhat rude and uncivilised nation of farmers living on the produce of the soil ; they are animated by a deep and even stern religious senti- ment, and they inherit from their ancestors, the men who won the independence of Holland from the ep- pressive rule of Philip 11. of Spain, their unconquer- able love of freedom and liberty. Are not these qualities which commend themselves to men of the English race; are they not virtues which we afe proud to believe form the best characteristics of the English people?'' Such are the people with whom we—or rather shall I say the inhuman Capitalists—sought a quarrel and succeeded eventually in snatching from them the country they loved so much. What a contemptible gang of thieves we must be. Land, Gold, Slavery, three words only, and yet they are to-day making English history repeat itself. British soldiers en- forcing the eviction of a homely, industrious nation of farmers to satisfy the cravings of a set of scoun- drels. Such Boer bravery against enormous odds- -256 to I—is sufficient to make the blood tingle in our veins and to knock all the conceit and hypocrisy out of us. But no! The Capitalists have not yet got all they require, so we must bow down to theni and be bribed ;into submission, singing the same old song "Britons never, never shall be slaves."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>LABOUR FOR THE MINES.</head>
        <p>LABOUR FOR THE MINES.</p>
        <p>The country is ours. What shall we do with it? If we were not inveigled into the war by powerful trading companies, controlled by clever, rich influential men then there ought to be work for those who fought and won it. But so far as I can learn the sympathy and support of the British public, hoodwinked by exaggeration and misrepre- sentation, have been prostituted, and now that the fighters are no longer required, the workers are to be chosen from other ranks. But what was the real object of the war ? Let us see. "A meeting of the 'Consolidated Gold Fields Company of South Africa' was held at the Cannon-street Hotel, 'ondon, Novem- ber 14th, 1899, and is fully reported in the "Financial News " of November 21st. Lord Harris (the chair- man) stated that upon the working capital of £2,147,000 the profit for the year had been £1,006,000! But this enormous profit does not satisfy these people. They want more, and they mean to have more. Mr. John Hays Hammond, engineer of the ;company, said in his speech that he estimated. that after the war they would increase their profit to £2,199,000. So this single company stands to make a million a year by the war. This is the real reason why the war was inevitable. Do not think that this means a splendid opening for British labour; it means nothing of the kind. The plan is to apply as little British labour as possible and to reduce the wages of native labour to the smallest pittance. These men do not conceal it. Mr. Hammond said: "There ought to be no difficulty in obtaining 80,000 Kaffirs to work the mines." Where does the British working man come in? Mr. Hammond further said: "With a good Government there should be an abund- ance of labour, and with an abundance of labour there will be no difficulty in cutting down wages, because it is preposterous to pay a Kaffir the present wages. (The native earned about 50s. or 60s. a month.) He would be quite as well satisfied—in fact, he would. work longer—if you gave him half . the amount."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>A-SIA'ITIC LABOUR</head>
        <p>A-SIA'ITIC LABOUR</p>
        <p>Earl Grey, in addressing the Chartered Company's shareholders (reported in "The Times," December 15th, 1899) said: "We must dismiss from our minds any idea of developing our mines with white labour. . . . . It is obvious that the black labour of the aboriginal inhabitants of South Africa must be our first line of defence An incentive of labour must be provided, and it can only be provided by imposition of taxation. I look forward to the imposition of a hut tax of £1 per hut in conformity with the practice that prevails in Basutoland, and I also I've that we may, with the permission of the imperial authorities, be able to establish a lab Dur tax which those able-bodied natives should be required to pay who are unable to show a certificate of four months' work. I may add that the directors are already making inquiries on their own account as to the possibility of obtaining 'Asiatic • labour." So we see now what ouglit to have been apparent to us before what was the real incentive in the minds of Philanthropists alias Capitalists. But let us continue. From the " Daily Telegraph" of September 14th, 1900, I take the following: "The alarm at the pros- pect of the introduction of Chinese cheap labour into Cape Colony and Rhodesia, a scheme which, it is understood, is favoured by Mr. Rhodes, is spreading among all classes of colonists. The mercantile com- munity have already protested in the strongest manner, and to-clay the Cape Town Corporation decided to send a deputation :to Sir Alfred Milner to offer an equally emphatic protest. Kim- -I?erley is quite hostile to the project, which is re- garded as likely to be altogether injurious to the welfare of British artizans and workmen who may settle in the country after the war, besides introducing the immorality and uncleanness of the lowest class of IChinese. There will certainly be vehement opposi- tion to the dumping of the Yellow trouble in South Africa, not stopping short of forcible resistance." That was said in 1900. What do we say in 1904?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>MODERN CHRISTIANITY.</head>
        <p>MODERN CHRISTIANITY.</p>
        <p>Let me say that with the Chinese, immoral and un- clean as the lowest class undoubtedly is, for the moment we have no quarrel. What we, however, demand is that a country, which has been won by the Britisher with his own hands and his own money, shall first of all belong to the whole people and not to a few self-elected individuals. We must insist that slavery shall no longer be tolerated. Labour is after , all wealth and happiness, and without it Capitalists can do nothing. It may be modern Christianity "to knock a man into a ditch,and then to tell him to remain content in the position in which Providence has placed him," but it is not the Christianity which is going to win the world for Christ and humanity. You say—" We did not knock him into the ditch." 'We shall never know what you have done, or left undone, until the question with us every morning is, not how to do the gainful thing, but to do the just thing during the day; nor until we are at least so far on the way to being Christian as to acknowledge that maxim of the poor half-way Mahometan, ' One hour in the execution of justice is worth seventy years of prayer! ' " Let me appeal to ' the young men, those with ideals, to unite in protest- ing against such an infamous outrage on Christianity as the introduction of slavery into South African mines. If you take away a man's liberty, thought, and action and altogether treat him as a beast of burden 1 life becomes odious to him and the only relief which can come to him and free him from bondage in his , premature old age is death. Surely, we ought not to Isit at our ease whilst such an un-Christlike action is premeditated. Let us not waste all our energy in religious sentiment which finds its only outlet in words 1 and grimaces but prepare ourselves for the Death to I come by earnestly living the Life that is.</p>
        <p>EDNIUND OSBORNE.</p>
      </div>
