      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>NOW IS THE TIME.</head>
        <p>NOW IS THE TIME.</p>
        <p>Do you enjoy perfect health? If net. NV hy not? The i!implest way foil man to secure immunity from disease is to follow, as far as practicable, the laws of nature, and if illness overtakes him, to take as remedies only such preparations as are supplied by nature. The only natural remedies are vegetables, and the chief vegetable remedy of the present age is Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, the Vegetable Tonic. GWILYM EVANS' QUININE BITTERS. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is a preparation now extensively taken throughout the country by patients suffsring from debility, nervousness, and general ex- haustion, and if any value be attached to human testi- mony, the efficacy of this medicine is established. Its elaims have been tested and proved by the medical pro- fession, and others, and corroborated by the written testimonials of eminent men. OUGHT TO BE IN EVF,RY HoUSE. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters contains not only a suitable quantity of quinine in each dose, but theactive principles of the following well known herbs:—sarsapar- illa, saffron, gentian, lavender, burdock, and dandelion. At this season of the year no one should be without Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, the Best Remedy of the Age for various ailments. GWILYM EVA.NS' QUININE BITTERS. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is recommended for indigestioa in its different forms, such as sick hbad- aches, heartburn, cramp, flatulency, sense of fulness and oppression after eating, drowsiness, and pains in the region of the heart. It is, without exception, the best remedy for depression of spirits and melancholia. Being a vegetable" Pick-me-up," it is strongly recom- mended for nervous diseases, such as undue anxiety, despondency, fainting fits, neuralgia, and nerve pains generally.</p>
        <p>STRONGLY RECOMMENDED. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters has smcessfully treated (after all other known preparations have failed) severe oases of affectiorts of the chest, such as common colds, bronchitis, asthmatic colds, shortness of breath.influenza, c. It purifies and enriches the blood. It is a healthy and safe tonic. Should be taken by all who are delicate, as it strengthens the system and promotes good health. People suffering great weakness, either after an illness, or through long confinement in ill-ventilated rooms, or any other cause, find Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters most valuable as a great strength reviver. ONVILYM Ev.k.Ns' QUININE BITTERS. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is sold everywhere in bottles 2s. 9d. and 4s. 6d. each or will be sent, on re- ceipt of stamps, carriage free direct from the Sole Pro- prietors :—Quinine Bitters 'Manufacturing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales. BEWARE OF IMITATIONS. When you ask for Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, see that you get it, with the name "Gwilym Evans" on the label, on the stamp, and on the bottle, without which none Is genuine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>SIX MONTHS FOR THEFT OF HARNESS. At the Warrington Police Court on Wednesday, John Rutter, of no fixed abode, .was charged with stealing five horse collars and a large number of articles of harness, of the value of £3, the property of Mr. Edward Connor, cab pronrietor.—Superintendent Monaghan said the fairness was left in the cab yard. On the 19th March prisoner went to Edwin Shaw, second-hand dealer, Battersby-lane, and offered some horse collars and other articles for sale. He stated that he was a horse and coal dealer, but was giving up business. Mr. Shaw gave him 103. for the harness. The following morning prisoner again went to Mr. Shaw with more harness and a manure fork. Mr. Shaw was suspicious and questioned the prisoner who said" It's all right. I've found some more." Mr. Saw purchased the fork, but having made inquiries reported the matter to the police. During the day prisoner sold a bridle, collar and pair of reins to Joseph Tavlin. Longford-street, for Is., telling the same story. Mr. Connor identified the harness as his property and the police commenced a search for the prisoner. Nothing was seen of him until he was arrested at Patricroft on Tuesday. Prisoner -would not work and his wife and family were in the Workhouse. He had served three months for neglect- ing his family and had also been convicted of stealing -clothing and tools from workmen. Prisoner was sent to goal for six months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>Minor Cases.</head>
        <p>Minor Cases.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>MONDAY—Bef-ore the Mayor (in the chair), Alderman Smethurst, Councillor Thornton, Messrs. Birchall and</p>
        <p>Wright. COUNTY COURT BAILIFF ASSAULTED.—AIfred Hoyles, 36, Turner-street, was charged with having been -drunk and disorderly in Bridge-street on Saturday evening and with assaulting John Knight, a County Court bailiff.—Mr. Knight said he was going along Bridge-street when he met the prisoner who, because witness refused to treat him, struck him in the mouth with his clenched fist.—Prisoner also made use of abusive language.:--A fine of 103. and costs was imposed. ONE OF THE PERENNIALS.—James Leanardy, a well- known character, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Lyme-street on Saturday night.—The 'Chief Constable said prisoner made his 32nd appearance and was" one of the perennials."—A fine of 10s. and costs was inflicted,Mr.Birchall remarking that prisoner was one of those people whom the Bench despaired of doing any good with. Wirt SHE SWOONED.—Margaret Stephens, 15, Old. road, was charged with stealing from the door of the Public Benefit ,Boot Company's establishment on Satur- .dity evening two pairs of men's boots, value 13s. 10d.— The Chief Constable said prisoner was evidently en- gaged in wholesale shop-door thefts on Saturday. At 9.30 P.C. Burrows was on duty at the corner of Rylands- street and saw the primmer carrying something bulky under her shawl. He went towards her and asked her what she had got, and she immediately pretended to faint, falling down in the roadas though insensible. It was found that the cause of her sudden swoon was a pair of new boots which were concealed under her shawl. It was subsequently ascertained that they had been stolen from the Public Benefit Boot Company's -shop a few minutes previously. After the prisoner had been locked up, her husband came to see what had become of her. It was noticed that he was wearing a pair of shoes con- spicuous for their newness. On being asked where he had got them from, he said his wife had bought them that night. It appeared, however, that prisoner had bought them on the same terms as the other pair—that was they had been stolen from the Public Benefit Boot Coinp my's shop. The police did not allege that the husband knew anything about the theft, because his story seemed straightforward enough.—Prisoner, who had a bad record, was sentenced to two months' hard labour. BOYS' DINGER.OUS PRA.CTICK.—Thomas Brookes,23B, Winwick-ruad, and .Thomas Summerset, Kerfoot-street, were summoned for trespassing on the London and North- Western Railway on February 21th in such a manner as to expose themselves to danger or risk of danger.—Mr. Fenn). prosecuted and pointed out that the defendants might have been charged with larceny. Neither the company nor himself desired to prosecute such youngsters for offences of that kind. The object of the proceedings was to stop people from going on the Dallam sidings where a vast amount of trespassing and a great deal of theft took place. Men were constantly watch- ing there and or the date in question they found the defendants in a waggon. There was nothing in the waggon but straw, and this the lads were binding up into bundles and throwing it down on the sidings, for the purpose of taking it away. When the detectives appeared, Summerset ran away and climbed over some sleeper fencings. Brooks was caught, and on being asked what he was doing on the sidings, he said he wanted the straw for some puppies at home. He gave a false name and address.—ln the course of the evidence, it was pointed out that the boys exposed themselves to great risk, as shunting operations were going on.—Summerset was fined 2s. 6d. and Brooks, for giving a false name and address, had to pay ss.</p>
        <p>TUESDA.Y.—Before Aldermen Sutton (in the chair) and Hutchinson, and Mr. J. R. Young. ANOTHER THEFT OF BOOTS.—Laura Lawless, Heaton- street, was charged with stealing from the pawnshop of Benedict Tyrer, Winwick-street, on Monday a pair of boots of the value of 9s.—Superintendent 'Monaghan said the prisoner went to the shop to pledge some goods. Shortly after she lei% the boots were missed, and it was found that prisoner had pledged them at Tunstall's pawnshop for 4s.—P.C. Cooper arrested the prisoner. She was a married woman and her husband was in regular employment.—Prisoner, who made her first appearance, was ordered to pay the costs (65.) THEFT FROM A COAL YA.RD.—Henry Faulkner, 15, Beswick-street, was charged with stealing from the coal yard of Messrs. W. W. Westwell and Company, Factory-Lane, 42 lbs of scrap lead, of the value of 4s. Superintendent Monaghan said that for the past eight •or nine months prisoner had been employed at the yard.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Original Cocos, and a Speciality,</head>
        <p>The Original Cocos, and a Speciality,</p>
        <p>I , •</p>
        <p>being distinguished from all others by its invigorating nutritious qualities and its delicious flavour: This Cocoa, con- taining as it does all the substance of the Cocoa Nib, maintains its leading position after three-quarters of a Century as</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>C • COA</head>
        <p>C • COA</p>
        <p>the best form of Cocoa for every-day use.</p>
        <p>Instead of attending to his work on Monday morning he was drinking, but in the afternoon he came to the yard. As he was drunk and abusive Mr. West- well sent for a policeman and prisoner went away. Between five and six o'clock, P.C. Hewitt met him in Factory-lane, carrying a bag containing scrap lead. He could not give any satisfactory account of how he came into possession of it, and he was taken to the Police Station. There he stated that he did not steal the lead but took it from som4 scrap in the yard. He had no right to take any lead.—A fine of 20s. and costs, with .the option of a month's imprisonment, was ' imposed. WZDNESDA.Y.—Before Mr. Birchall (in the chair) Alderman Hutchinson, and Mr. Geddes. A NIVAL DESERTER.—Sydnay Bullock was charged with being a deserter from H.M.S. " St. George" stationed at Portsmouth.—P.C. Dennett gave evidence of arrest and prisoner was remanded in order that the authorities might be communicated with.—On Thursday the Bench recommended the Constable for the usual reward.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the char), Alder-</p>
        <p>man Smethurst and Mr.' F. W. Monlai.</p>
        <p>HUSBAND A-ND WM.—James Owen, 27, Lockett- street, was summoned for assaulting his, wife, Annie Owen, on March 29th.—Complainant stated that her husband came home with 16s. for a week's wages. He asked for 6d. for beer, and she refused to give it to him. Thereupon he seized her by the neck. Afterwards he struck her in the face, inflicting a black eye. He deliberately burnt a two pound loaf rather than allow the children and herself to eat it. On other occasions defendant had taken pokers and knives upstairs and had threatened to murder his family.—Defendantsaid he had been unwell and his wife aggravated him. She challenged him to fight, and "put her props up." He admitted striking her, but denied throwing the loaf in the fire.—The magistrates thought it was a case of "sir of one and half-a-dozen of the other" and ordered defendant to pay the cost of tne summons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>......_-,1.,.._........ STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>......_-,1.,.._........ STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL BAND OF HOPE MEETING.—The tenth annual meeting and concert in connection with the Hill Cliff° and Stockton Heath United Band of Hope was held in the Providence Chapel Schoolroom, on Monday even- ing. There was a good attendance. The chair was occupied by the Rev. J. S. Hughes (the president of the society), who was supported by the Rev. H. Wall, of Warrington, Mr. W. A. Sandford, and Mr. G. Whitfield (secretary), who presented a very encouraging report of the work of the society, and also referred to the need for a senior temperance organization. A capital pro- gramme was given, commencing with a pianoforte duet by the Misses Jones and England. Miss May Isherwood sang very sweetly and was deservedly encored, as was also Miss Elsie Spruce for her excellent singing. Master Spruce contributed a humorous song, for which he re- ceivsd an encore. Miss Wood, of Penketh, gave a capital rendering of "The Gift," which was much enjoyed; whilst Miss Ethel Dunn con- tributed two songs in good style. Other songs were given by Master T. Richmond and Master Stanley Jones, their voices being heard to distinct advantage. The instrumental quartets, contributed by the Misses Pooley and Warren, were much enjoyed, each item being encored. Miss E. Pooley also gave a violin solo and was recalled. A couple of flute solo were supplied by Mr. Cook: selections by the children's choir, and recita- tions by Mary Earle and Joseph Whitfield. The Rev. H. Wall aripealed for more workers in the temperanos cause. During the evening medals were presented on behalf of the 'Lancashire and Cheshire B.H.U. for col- lections made on behalf of their new building scheme to Lawrence Kenworthy, Arthur Whitfield, Harold Jones, Stanley Bairsto, and Charles Goslin. Mrs. Hughes pre- sented the medals. The society by entertainments and collecting cards has now raised £lO towards their fund. Votes of thanks were passed to all who had taken part in the proceedings on the motion of Mr. Sandford, seconded by Mr. E. Lloyd-Jones. Mrs. Hughes, Miss Bouchier, Miss Jones and Master Whitfield acted as accompanists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>LIKE A KNIFE.</head>
        <p>LIKE A KNIFE.</p>
        <p>"Pains, caused by my Kidney Trouble, seemed to cut me like a knife," writes Mr. Owen T. Lewis, Station Road, Gowerton, near Swansea. "I was laid up in bed for five months, and during that period I scarcely slept more than half-an-hour at a time.g had two doctors who gave me a good deal of medicine. It cost nearly six pounds, but I derived no benefit from the treatment. It was while I lay in bed that I happened to read an advertisement of Dodd's Kidney Pills, and seeing so many cases, where cures had been effected, I resolved to give them a trial. From the first I began to mend, so dropped all other medicine and kept right on with the Pills. They have completely cured me, and I have not now an ache or pain in my body." The remady that has p3rintnently cured Mr. Lewis, and thousands of others, is surely worthy of a fair trial by all who suffer with any form of Kidney Trouble. Dodd's Kiduey Pills are obtainable wherever medicine s sold.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>MAY HORSE PARADE.</head>
        <p>THE MAY HORSE PARADE.</p>
        <p>Additional Gla3ses and Prins.</p>
        <p>In the hope of being favoured with congenial weather it has been decided to hold this year's May parade of ' horses on a later date than usual—May 21st. The ' arrangements are already being pushed forward, and a meeting of the Committee was held at the Lion Hotel on Monday afternoon under the presidency of Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart. There were also present : Messrs. Cohn Mclver, W. P. ttylands, W. ff. J. Jenkins, R. Spann, C. Jackson Holmes, S. E. Picton, R. M. Roberts, A. Thompson, J. Fulton Smith (treasurer), G. Venn and T. H. Pretty (joint secretaries). . The balance sheet wassubmitted by Mr. Fulton Smith It showed a balance carried forward from 194 of £33 10s, 6d., and receipts for the year, amounting to £lO6 14s Od. After paying all expenses there was a balance in the bank of £7l2s Bd. The balance sheet was adopted. It was reported that a meeting ofjthe sub-committee was held on March 4th to considerthe arrangement for this year's parade. Mr. Fulton Smith,on behalf of Messrs Peter Walker and Sons, Limited, said it was their wish not to enter for any cup or medal presented by the committee. It was decided " that any competitor winning outright a cup or paedal be disqualified from competing for a cup or meal in the same class the following year." The parade, it was suggested, should be held on Satur- day, May 21st ; entries to close on May 14th. A vote of condolence was passed with the family of the late Mr. Joseph Knowles, and it was resolved that Mr. Peter Knowles be asked to take his father's place as a judge. The amount received in subscriptions for 1903 £54 7s.—was far below that for the previous year. As the balance in hand this year was considerably less, and there were three more classes to provide for, it would •be necessary for united efforts to' be made to increase the income. The classes at the forthcoming parade would be as follows :-1, pair horse brewers; is, single, horse brewers; 2, railway and canal carriers; 3, builders merchants; 4, builders, contractors and carters; 5, tanners; 6, Corporation; 7, coal merchanta and dealers 8, corn dealers and millers; 9, butchers, bakers, and grocers; 10, mineral water dealers; 11, heavy mis- cellaneous; 12, light miscellaneous; 13, pair horse miscellaneous ; 14, fish dealers and milk dealers; 15, farmers; 16, covered furniture vans. On the proposition of Mr. FULTON SMITH the com- mittee decided to hold the parade on May 21st. Mr. Peter DA.VIES wrote consenting to act as referee, and offering to give a cup for the best horse on parade. The SECRETA.RY announced that the Shire Horse Society renewed their offer of silver medals for the best single and pair horses in the procession. Sir GILBERT GREENALL offered two silver cups,value £5 each, for the best light lorry or van home and the best light horse respective-ly. Mr. FULTON SMITH remarked that he thought the past parades had resulted in a marked improvement in the condition of horses in Warrington. He moved a vote of thanks to Sir Gilbert Greenall for presiding, and expressed the indebtedness of the committee to him for his offer of the two silver cups. It was very en- couraging to have a: the head of affairs a gentleman who took such a great interest in the objects of the parade. (Hear, hear). Mr. RYLA.NDS seconded the motion which was heartily agreed to, and Sir Gilbert briefly acknowledged the compliment. - -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>LAST YEAR'S PARADE.</head>
        <p>LAST YEAR'S PARADE.</p>
        <p>The report of last year's horse parade has just been issued by the committee. The event, it is stated,proved to be quite as successful as the one held the previous year. The number of entries was about the same, viz., 110; and the judges noticed a marked improvement in matters of detail compared with 1901. The weather was again unfavourable and caused great disappointment to all concerned.Fortmately it kept:flue or the assembling and marshalling of the various turnouts, for the judging and for the presentation of prizes and diplomas by Lady Greenall, a duty, it is needless to say, she per- formed with grace and dignity, and with a kind word of approval to each recipient which was much appreciated. Immediately the procession started the rain came down in• torrents and considerably marred the effect. The Committee tender their sincere thanks to Mr. Peter Davies, Heatley, for presenting a silver cup for the best horse in the procession; to the Shire Horse Society, for their contributhtn of a silver medal for the best horse in single harness; to the R.S.P.C.A., for giving a diploma to be awarded to the winner of the Ist prize in each class, conditionally that the winner had not been previously convicted of cruelty; and also to the Chief Constable and the men under his chargo for their valuable assistance in main- taining order and helping the committee to marshal the turnouts on the ground and along the line of route. Much credit is due to the horse-keepers for'the highly satisfactory manner in which the horses appeared on parade; the aim of the committee being to encourage regular care in the feeding and grooming of horses, and to foster kindly treatment of them at all times.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, April 2 1904.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April 2 1904.</p>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHEMES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHEMES.</p>
        <p>Cantata at St. Paul's Church.</p>
        <p>At this church on Sunday evening Maunder's cantata "Penitence, Pardon and Peace." was sung in the, presence of a large congregation by the full choir of about 40 voices. The soloists were Miss Nellie Armstrong, (soprano) and Mr. C. E. Gelderd (baritone) who aoquitted themselves very creditably. Mr. Ernest Wilkes, A.L.C.M., presided at the organ. " The Crucifixion." This cantata on the sacred passion of the Holy Redeemer written by the Rev. W. J. Sparrow Simpson, MA., and wedded to beautiful music by the late Sir J. Stainer, was impressively rendered by the members of the Wycliffe Choir on Sunday eveniag under the leadership of Mr. W. H. Payton, Mug. Bac. The two leading soloists, Mr. Arthur Griffiths (tenor), and Mr. W. H. Wallington (baritone), were both in good voice and they feelingly interpreted the solemn passages of the meditation. One of the most beautiful pieces was the duet in which Mr. Griffiths and Mr. Wallington were heard together in the verses commencing So Thou liftest Thy divine petition.' Shorter solo pass— ages were sung by Mr. A. Cotterill and Mr. B. Hibbert. The choruses were adequately sung by the choir, who may be said to have distinguished themselves.in their interpretation of the processional to Calvary Fling wide the gates." The congregation joined in singing several hymns, including the litany of the Passion. Mr. W. H. Payton,lMus Bac., presided at the organ, and was very effective in the des- criptive passages. The service was conducted by the Rev. John Yonge.—A collection was taken on behalf of the choir funds.</p>
        <p>Last evening, "The Crucifixion " was rendered with great impressivenessty the Parish Church choir at the close of evensong. Mr. F. M. Darby Mus.Bac., presided at the organ with marked ability. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. T. -Birchen and Chilstian Citizenship.</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance at the open meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A. Society on Sundayafternoon. Mr. T. Birchall, IP., occupied the chair, and in a brief address he said the last time he presided at that meet- ing, twelve months ago, he thought it would be the last one. Seventy-four years of age and East winds did not agree, and it was with some difficulty he was able to dis- charge the duty. He was pleased to be able to con- gratulate them on carrying on that excellent move- ment, for it was a good work, the Bishop of Manchester notwithstanding. He was sure the Bishop of Manchester did not thoroughly understand the movement, or he would not have said what he did. In speaking of our duties as citizens, Mr. Birchall said we were apt to take things too carelessly. It was very im- portant that we should carry our Christianity out of thechurch into the world. Christianity that was confined to a church or chapel was not of much use. If we took our Christianity into municipal and public matters it would have a grand purifying influence. We should Labour to do our duty as citizens fearlessly and try to influence for good all those with whom we came in contact, in fact to take Christianity into the workshop and office and all the affairs of life. If we did that we should exercise an influence which would help to bring about the time when the knowledge of God would cover the earth as the waters covered the sea.—An address was delivered by Mr. George Power,of Manchester. In alluding to criticisms which had been passed on the work of the P.S.A., he said he was able to prove from his own experience, that the movement had been instrumental in lifting up many a fallen brother. It was God's work, he said, because the homes had pro- claimed it, and because the lives of women and children had brightened and benefited by it. It was not in singing and praying only that a Christian life was to be judged,but in the lifeand character.—The Rev. John Yonge announced that the membership had been increased by nine that afternoon, making a total of 48 for the first month of the present year. He also stated the sick club intended to pay a dividend of 4s. to the members.—Miss A. Bridge sang with great effect two solos entitled "Entreat me not to leave thee," and "The children of the City."—Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus. Bac., presided at the organ:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Services for the People.</head>
        <p>Services for the People.</p>
        <p>The Parr Hall was crowded on Sunday evening when another of the" Services for the People" was con- ducted by the Rev. H. Dunnico. Mr. Frank Hickman gave an organ recital previous to the service, and a large choir, under the leadership of Mr. T. Brough rendered the anthem "When I can read my title clear.' The large congregation joined heartily in the hymns The Rev. H. Dunnico spoke on the relationship of the church to modern social problems, rand said that no league or society of men would be able to solve the social problem which did not at the very outset deal' with the drink problem. The onus of dealing with this question must not be left in the hands of total abstainers, but ought to be lifted above all narrow issues and made a citizen's question. The" Trade," as it was termed, .represented a capital of £300,000,000, 120,000 shareholders, 9,000 private brewers,jl26,ooo "on licences" and 30,090 " off licences." These people were all united and highly organized, and would resist the elec- tion of any candidate, be he Liberal, Labour, or Con- servative who sought in any way to restrict the national consumption of intoxicating liquors. A candidate might be in favour of an eight hours day, a living wage, old-age pensions, and other similar reforms, but if be did not meet with the approval of the "Trade" they would leave no stone unturned to secure his defeat. When they took into consideration that amongst those interested in the drink trade there were peers, titled persons,doctors, bishops, deans;archdeacons canons, vicars, and ministers of various denominations he thought they would agree that the "Trade" formed a great social and political menace to the country's welfare. Proceeding to discuss the problem of poverty the speaker said that it was a standing disgrace to the Christian churches that such a state of things should exist. The country was richer than ever it had been, yet more than one-quarter of the population was living on the verge of starvation. We had been accumulating long enough; it was time we thought more of distribu- tion. The speaker strongly condemned unlimited com- petition, and contended that it was the duty of the State to find every man an opportunity of labouring for his bread even if it meant pledging the national credit, and said that a man who had served his country should not be left in old age with nothing before him but poverty and starvation on the one hand and pauper- ization on the other.—To-morrow (Sunday) Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus. Ban., will give an organ recital at 6 p.m., and Mr. Dunnico will deliver his monthly sermon to young men and women, subject. "A young man's view of life."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>HOSPITAL SUNDAY.</head>
