        <p>Even a county court judge cannot be expected to know everything. "I am not an authority on washing and mangling," confessed Judge Edge to a lady at Clerkenwell on Wednesday, who procured a wringer in court, and complained that a roller bulged when a bed quilt was put through. Another dissatisfied person brought some coals wrapped in brown paper, and said he should like the judge to pass his opinion upon them. He bought them for the best silkstone, but they were a mixture of hard steam and rubbish. "They look like what is called in Lancashire 'roofers '—where the coal joins the stone," observed the judge, "that is all I can -saY-"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>LOCAL ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>POLLING FOR GUARDIANS AND PARISH COUNCILLORS.</p>
        <p>Return of Miss Constance Broadbent.</p>
        <p>There was only one contest in the Borough on Monday in connection with the triennial election of Guardians, but in the adjoining districts, matters took a more lively turn, and there was keen competition for positions of public trust. Not only sere there contests for the position of Guardians and Rural District Coun- cillor, but there were also elections at Burtonwood and Stockton Heath for Parish Councillors. THE CONTEST IN LATCHFORD WARD.</p>
        <p>The election in'Warrington was in Latchford Ward,and the candidates were :—Miss Constance Broadbent and McGeorge Pilting.Miss Broadbent pursued her electoral campaign with vigour, and held a public meeting in support of her candidature, whilst on the other hand, Mr. Pilling did not seem to throw any energy into the contest. There were two polling stations, one at Christ Church schools and th? other at St. James' School. At the former Mr. Lawrence was presiding officer and Mr. Alfred Wood, poll clerk. There were three polling places at St. Jamas' schools, in charge of the following (1) Mr. C. E. Sutton,presiding officer ; Mr. E. J. Sutton poll clerk; (2) Mr. E. H. Sutton, presiding officer ; Mr. P. Aukland, poll clerk ; (3) Mr. Stanley Wilson, pre- siding officer, Mr. A.Starkey,poll clerk. Polling proceeded quietly throughout the day,but became briskeraftecr tea when the men had finished their work. At eight o'clock, the ballot boxes were removed to Mr. Sutton's office, and the result was declared shortly before nine o'clock as follows : _ _</p>
        <p>Constance Broadbent 451 George Piling ll9</p>
        <p>332 THE GREAT SAN KEY CONTEST.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas Halton; who for nine years has repre- sented Great Sankey on the Board of Guardians and Rural Council, was opposed by Mr. George Thompson, of the Mersey White Lead Works. Neither candidate held a public meeting, but Mr. Halton issued circulars, calling the electors' attention to useful public work he had performed on behalf of the township. The election was keenly contested and conveyances were requisitioned to get the electors to the polling station which was situated at the schoolroom. Mr. F. W. Kerfoot was the presiding officer and Mr. George Clarke poll clerk. The resultiwas declared shortly before nine o'clock as follows :</p>
        <p>George Thompson Thomas Halton 64</p>
        <p>The result was received with cheers by a goodly number of voters, who had assembled in the schoolroom Mr. Thompson proposed a vote of thanks to the pre- siding officer. He remarked that Mr. Kerfoot had given satisfaction both to himself and Mr. Halton in the con- duct of the election. So far as he personally was con- cerned, he had tried to fight the election fairly and squarely, and he thanked them for returning him by such a majority. (Applause.) Mr. Halton seconded. He was sure, he said, the elec- tion had been fought on most friendly lines and he only regretted he was at the bottom ef the poll. The motion was heartily agreed to, and Mr. Kerfoot acknowledged the compliment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>CONTESTS AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>CONTESTS AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>There were two contests at Earlestown for seats on the Warrington Board of Guardians,one in the Viaduct Ward and the other in Wargrave Ward. Both of the retiring Guardians succeeded in retaining their seats. The result of the poll was as follows :</p>
        <p>VIADUCT WARD. John Clarke 343 Thomas Lythgoe 3lB</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>WARGRAVE WARD.</head>
        <p>WARGRAVE WARD.</p>
        <p>E. L. Bent 202 A. King</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>HAYDOCK.</head>
        <p>HAYDOCK.</p>
        <p>The candidates were Mr. J. Fh-Lnaghan and Mrs. Catherine Hayward. Mr. J. Dickenson, deputy return- ing officer deolared the result of the poll about 8.30., as follows : Flanaghan Hayward 125 • Majority for Mr. Flanaghan lB4 NEWTON URBAN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>On Monday, polling took place in two wards of Newton-in-Makerfleld, namely, Viaduct and Town Hall Wards to fill the vacancies in the Urban Council caused by the retirement of Mr. J. W. Emmett and Dr.Latham respectively. The last-named gentleman sought re- election. Considerable interest was taken in the con- tests and there was an exceptionally keen fight in Town Hall Ward. The results were declared as follow :</p>
        <p>TOWN HALL WARD. John Naylor 342 Dr. Latham vn Majority 8 --"rmol VIADUCT WARD. Mr.W. Beatty Mr. E. Worsley</p>
        <p>Majority STOCKTON HEATH ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>Interest in Monday's elections ran high at Stockton Heath where the ratepayers had the opportunity of vot- ing for representatives on the Runcorn Rural District Council and Board of Guardians,and the Parish Council. Stockton Heath's representation on the Runcorn authorities has recently been increased to two members. Mr. C. W. Davenport, the retiring councillor, did not seek re-election, and three candidates—Messrs. John Hallows, James Whitley, and John Pemberton— entered the field. For the twelve Parish Council seats there were no fewer than seventeen candidates. Polling proceeded briskly at the National Schools where the counting afterwards took place. Shortly beforfften o'clock Mr. W. E. Hough, deputy clerk to the District Council, declared the results as follows:</p>
        <p>RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL John Hallows James Whitley 197 John Pemberton PARISH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Frederick Pearson John Hallows Joseph Houghton Clarke William Mclntyre James Whitley 204 John Fairhurst 192 Herbert J. Westbrook Joseph W. Bouchier Frank Warburton. 169 Thomas R. Westbrook Samuel F. Welsby *Thomas Williams *Ebenezer England 157 John Pemberton John Lord ll7 William Henry Sherburn John Miller</p>
        <p>*As Messrs. Williams and England tied for the twelfth seat, they decided to draw for the honour and Mr. Williams was fortunate enough to obtain the winning number. The first twelve gentlemen in the above list are therefore elected. .</p>
        <p>It is interesting to note that the poll has resulted in the return of all the candidates elected by show of hands at the recent parish meeting, when Mr. John Lord, supported by several other ratepayers, demanded a poll.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>PARISH COUNCIL ELECTION AT BURTONWOOD.</head>
        <p>PARISH COUNCIL ELECTION AT BURTONWOOD.</p>
        <p>There were twelve caadidates for the seven seats on the Burtonwood Parish Council. The result of the poll was as follows: John T.-Addison, Pear Tree ll3 George Anders, Bridge Inn William Fairelough, Causeway Bridges William Fairelough, Brook Head Farm Thomas Forster, Ivy Farm Richard Forshaw, the Brewery Herbert Gawthorpe, School House William-Houghton, Mersey-street l3l John Thomas Mehers, Collins Green Alfred Mansfield Mitchell, the Vicarage 141 William Southern, Rose Villa John G. Thompson, Bank House The following were elected :—Messrs. R. Forshaw, J. G. Thompson, John Thomas Mehers, Rev. A. M. Mitchell, William Southern, William Houghton and Thomas Forster.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>SUSPICIOUS CHARACTER SENT TO PRISON.-At Newton Occasional Court on Monday, Michael Feeney, alias Thomas Feeney and Thomas Johnson, a native of Bold, was charged with having been found on enclosed premises at Penketh, for an unlawful purpose, on the 10th inst.—P.C. Houghton was on daty shortly after midnight on the 15th inst., when his attention was drawn to "The Elms," through the incessant barking of the dog. The constable went round to the rear of the house, and heard footsteps. He stood still, and the prisoner attempted to bound past him, but was seized. The only explanation prisoner could give was that he intended going to Widnes the next day, and he was looking for somewhere to sleep.—lnspector Foster pro- duced a record of 12 convictions against prisoner, for housebreaking and loitering at Knutsford. Chester, and Liverpool County Sessions. Prisoner was sent to gaol for a month with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>BEST MAN WINS.</head>
        <p>THE BEST MAN WINS.</p>
        <p>BY CECIL CLARE,</p>
        <p>Author of "The Pride of the County," "Drawn at a Venture," " The Beautiful Miss Smyth," "Coigns of 'Vantage," "Sacrificed in Vain," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>"No, I am not exactly a woman hater," said the Major, leaning back in his chair. "I am entirely indifferent to women," he continued, admiring the curve of a lady's neck in the near distance. ‘By the way, any of you fellows know the name of the lady opposite? She is rather pretty—from the back."</p>
        <p>But none of the fellows did, -and feeling the Major could be drawn into a story settled down for a smoke.</p>
        <p>"The reason I am indifferent to women," the Major went on, "is because I give up the possibility of understanding them. Sometimes they appear to me to be only irresponsible beings, at other times almost criminals." "Oh, Major, draw it mild I" ejaculated a youth on his left. "Have they no virtues ? " "Virtues ! " The Major lifted his hands, then on second thoughts—principally of his mother— " Well, the old ones have. I love old women. They are just women then, all love and heart, and all the sort of thing a woman is supposed to be by the poets when she is young. But, when a woman is young all her tenderness is crushed down by her love of love—of admiration, of power—her vanity and envy of other women." "Oh, Major, leave me an illusion," someone interrupted in a broken voice. But the Major turned upon him fiercely. "Fool away," he said, "but it's boys like you they suck the heart out of—you who think all these pretty ones sit at home and dust their china, and dream about birds and flowers or little innocent romances of princes and princesses while you men are away worrying over the ruling of the universe and the power you are to your party. But you mistake. They are as great plotters as your Ministers, and their rule of the few is as measured as their rule of the many. That's why women are content to be out of the great movements of the world, because they are always busy with some deep mischief of their own—conquering souls instead of countries. Their blushes are their army, their eyes their cannon, their touch captivity. And they will fight to the end, till their power is complete and their victims broken and at their feet. Then they will say: 'This is so sudden; I never thought of such a thing.' And if they drive their victim desperate they get hysterics or faint, and their lady friends come round to whisper: 'How distressed she is over that poor fellow who persecuted her with his intentions and: 'What horrid bad taste it was of him to cut his throat upon her doorstep,' all the time wishing he had done it upon theirs. "Oh goodness ! " said one of the listeners. what does all this lead up to ? " "Whether a woman is responsible if she brings about crime by her flirtations." His companions looked sharply at him. "And the one woman you generalise from ? " said a greybeard, seriously. "Do tell us the story, Major." "The story," said the Major, thoughtfully, "if there is a story, is about a woman I knew in India and two lads. They were as fine a pair of young fellows when I met them first as you would wish to see—handsome, frank, cheery boys, taking all the joy they could out of life, and full of young con- fidence that there would be no change. But they met this woman, and she set herself to capture them; there was no one better to amuse herself with just then. Ah I she had a good game. What was she like ? Soft, small, modest-eyed, of course; but those are the worst. The big, strong, healthy woman, fond of manly sports, who looks you straight in the face with a claim for comradeship and equality is safe enough. It is the little delicate- looking woman who sits quietly with downcast eyes that is the danger, and she was like that. I used to watch her sitting between the men with her long lashes cast down, and her colour coming and going, and the quick, innocent glance flying from one eager, face to the other as they leant over her. It was a study worth looking at. The men's faces you could read like a book—all their love, and passion, and jealousy; but hers, like a child's, expressing nothing but wonderment and a want to understand their desires.</p>
        <p>"And</p>
        <p>" I wish I could do something to help to keep you from loneliness, for you must be lonely coming from home,' she would say, plaintively; but I am lonely myself—so lonely, and the tears would stand in her baby eyes. But looking through them I could see the woman, and it seemed to me as if she were old and cunning and evil, as if she sat in that fair body pulling the strings, closing and unclosing the lovely windows, moving the white limbs, and pouring the red blood into the soft cheeks at command—an ugly hag, quick as a cat, watching from behind the drooping eyes every movement of the young men, ready to spring and draw them back if they strayed a moment from her power. And she played with them till the poor lads hardly knew what they were doing. They had only two thoughts in their minds sleeping and waking—love of her and hate of each other. Poor fellows! You would not have known them for the same lads after they had known her three weeks; they were sullen and desperate. It worried me to see them so wretched, and I asked her to let them alone. She turned her big, questioning eyes upon me, but finding it did nOt do laughed and said it was good for boys to learn, that youth is only made perfect when it has known how to love. And someone else coming up at the moment and hearing her laughed with her, pleased, I could see, that she was not in earnest with the lads. Those raw youths will be made men by a little suffering of the kind Mrs. Vartry so prettily tenders to both; man never lives till he loves. And they went off laughing together. "lialf-an-hour after I saw her sitting between the lads, her shoulder leaning towards one, her hand almost touching the sleeve of the other—she sitting consciously unconscious of the fiery glances fencing about her. The young men were tossing dice, and I heard Richard Ferris say: The best man wins,' looking his rival in the oyes. "‘ The best man wins,' Charles Boland answered, tossing the dice for the last time. But Mrs. Vartry leant back, to all appearance un- conscious of the double meaning implied by one young man and understood by the other. "‘ You have lost,' she said to Charlie as he bent over the dice counting them. 'Poor fellow 1' She touched his coat sleeve softly. "But I mean to win—another time,' he smiled into her startled eyes, and she blushed as if fol the first time she had a glimmer of his meaning. "'After a while she grew tired of the two being always with her, for neither would give way and allow the other a chance of being with her alone. Then she tried another plan and found it more exciting. She would drive one away and hardly speak to him for days, and the other would go about with her looking as happy as a king. Then she would suddenly take offence at some harmless remark of his for the sake of getting rid of him, and draw the other by a look back into Paradise. Thus the poor fellows became a prey to the most awful passions of jealousy and hatred. I came upon Charlie once leaning against a tree with a red gleam in his eye that I did not care to understand. He was staring across the grass at the other two, who were sitting with their backs to him evidently in deep, intimate conversation. I spoke to him then, but of course he would not hear a word against her. It was all that cad, Ferris,' and how he had told lies of him (Charlie)—said that he Wee a drunken lot and she had better have nothing to do with him. And you know, Major,' the poor fellow added, I seldom drink, and if last night I—l forgot myself and did take a little more than usual it was because I was so miserable.' “Ile spoke to me like the boy he was, blurting out his troubles. It was that very passionate in of themselves and taking themselves so seriously away that made me feel rather anxious about them both; one never knows what these hot- headed chaps will do in their mad moments. I told Charlie then that I was sure Ferris would never tell tales of him, but he answered: Someone told her, ' and he drew his conclusions from the way she spoke that it was Richard Ferris. From that I could not move him. "A week after he was back in favour and I came upon Ferris, the veins of his forehead standing out like whipcord and he muttering to himself like anything. I gave him a good talking to; I was so angry to see the two boys spoiling the best part of their lives over a woman who I saw meant to have nothing serious to do with either of them. Then, of course, I heard it was all the fault of that cad, Boland,' who had told her he (Richard) always carried a portrait of a girl in his watch. And though I told her it was my sister, Major, ' the poor fellow appealed to me, 'whatever he said to her about A she won't believe</p>
        <p>ms.' He openea the watch-case as he was speaking and shewed ma the likeness, and a sister so like a brother I have never seen. I laughed at the idea of the woman not knowing they were relations, and told him so. But it is as useless to talk reason to a man in love as it is to try and persuade a woman that her baby is exactly the same as any other baby of the same colour. "It was after this that the real trouble began. I saw the lads were bent on quarrelling; she had made the breach between them so wide that there was no use trying any longer to bring them together, I could only keep a strict eye upon them. Fortunately they seemed to avoid each other as much as possible, feeling, I suppose, they could not meet without some danger of breaking through all self-control and flying at each other's throat, Once, however, 1 turned a corner and came lima them, red as turkey-cocks, facing each other. " ' How dare you shoulder me ? ' cried one. " I didn't shoulder you, but I would if there were occasion,' shouted the other. I was upon them like lightning; I gave it to them up and down. I asked if they called themselves gentlemen, and what they meant by brawling like a couple of corner boys ejected from a public-house. I said if there were any quarrelling they should be punished, and they went off fully asham vl of themselves. After that I went straight to my lady and spoke fo her as if she were ray own daughter. I said she was playing a dangerous game and would be held responsible if anything happened, though all the time I knew of course the law would do nothing, not if they were lying dead at her feet, their hands at each other's throats. I told her how I had found them quarrel- ling, and begged her to prevent this bad blood between friends. I even said people were talking about it, and that they might have more to talk about if she did not take care.</p>
        <p>"Not a word I said affected her; Irsaw on the contrary that she was delighted. Her vanity waa touched, and she left me smiling to herself. "'Foolish boys,' she said, 'I will speak to them about it, Major.' "Speak to them about it! She went off that minute straight before my eyes and told them I hid been scolding her on account of them. And she repeated the story the same evening when I wirA listening to her, and she knew it. I was sitting in a conservatory at a friend's house at the time behind a little palm that did not half hide mee when she came in attended by her swains. The minute she knew I was there she planted herself on a couch a yard away and started—all about the trouble she was in and how I had spoken so sharply. I could hear the tremble in her voice, and coughed to let her know I was listening. But she did not pretend to notice, and the lads were too engrossed in themselves and her to have noticed an elephant if he had been trying to hide behind a geranium. So I had to listen to some strong criticisms on my interference from the young men. She told them a long story of how she had been ill-treated when a child; sent from India by her parents and reared by a cruel aunt who did not understand her; how she was blamed at school for what others had done; and, lastly, how her parents had died without her ever seeing them again, and she, an innocent girl, knowing nothing, had married an old man to escape the cruelty of her relative. It was a sad tale, well told, and one I thought I had heard before. I felt the tears come to my eyes as the little sweet voice rippled on. Of course I was very, very wicked to marry without love '—she paused humbly while the young men assured her that she was the best and most wronged woman in the world—' but I was so young and did not know anything—only wanted freedom, she sighed, leaving them to infer in that, too, she had known disappointment, when they paused out of breath. And then I gathered that the only good thing the old man did was to die and leave her just enough to live on. And on that she had come back to India to revisit the dear places she knew as a child, where her parents used to live, and where she was once, oh I so long ago, happy for a little time. "'So you see, , she half whispered, how greatly my life has needed love, and when you came to me like the two brothers I might have had, had I been a more fortunate woman, I could not turn away as I ought to have done from your affection.' "Here the young men assured her there was no reason for turning from them, that they were never so happy in their lives as they were since she came to know them, that Major Sullivan was am old fool, and that anyone could see they were in the seventh heaven so long as she would be friends with them and not get angry. "But she went on in that tone of self-pity one Iften hears when people have started a romance they believe to be their own. "Of course, if I had had more experience (I thought she dropped her voice on the word so that I should not hear it) I might have understood you did not care for me quite in the way a brother does for his sister.'</p>
        <p>"And now you do know,' Charlie said, gently, as if making up his mind, 'what are you going to do with us ? ' "What can I do,' she answered, sadly, 'excel). send you both away ? ' "At this Richard got angry. You will have to choose between us sooner or later—why not now ? ' " I couldn't,' she said, quickly, with a change of humour like a bold child, you are both equally horrid. You, Charlie, are so jealous, I feel likes prisoner when I am near you. And as for you, Richard,' she added, quelling the look of triumph that rose to his face, you never talk of anything but lo—yourself and your affairs. I am tired of you both—you may go away.' "The young men, sullen, passionate, humiliated, sat with their heads down unable to speak for rage. It was all so serious to them, though I laughed at her nonsense behind the palm and she heard me. I suppose she was angry at my taking her so lightly, for she gave me a glittering glance as the lads rose to leave her, and before they reached the door sent a sentence towards them that sang like a bullet through the air: "'Remember, the best man wins,' six fired after them. And they, beaming under her smile, would have returned, but she forbade them with a wave of her hand.</p>
        <p>„. "'You have done it flow,' I said, as she looked at me. 'Why did you not leave that last bit out? ' "'They can never prove which is the best man,' she laughed. I thought it a good joke. LYou see, they would not:be dismissed.' "‘lf you were only disagreeable enough ti” would go away and forget you,' I said, knows.. how unlikely my proposal was to be accepted, 'You drew them back with a hope just now because you thought they were going away too angry with you, and you prefer to keep them on, dangling or wasting their time, rather than lose the flattery of their misery. Women are all alike; if a man is wretched you may always cherchez la femme.' "But she turned upon me like a small whirl- wind; indeed, I wonder she stood go much from me since it was none of my bu- `q• I suppose It pleased her I should take her sei.,,usly. " Cherchez la femme ! ' she mocked. Cherches la femme! always blame the woman, from Adam upward. The man was tempted by the woman; the man ate the apple, then whined the blame upon the woman. Just as to-day they seek her out, then start to have what they call "a good time." They flirt and lie, and lie, and lie to the woman who sees through them and fights back with the same weapons. And if the man gets hurt it's "What mean creatures women are," and " Cherchez la femme," and all that. But there's a better saying than that,' she snapped at me, God made the women to match the men.' She rose to her feet, leaving me gasping an apology. " You might try and make these fellows happy,' I had courage to say as she got near the door. _</p>
        <p>Can I marry them both ? ' she chirped back, then vanished, but I could hear her laughing she went. "A week passed and the whole thing was as net; as ever; the young fellows were perfectly off their heads. Have you ever seep two sparrows fighting for a mate and the hen sitting looking on, every now and then rushing into the fray with a peck for both rivals and favour for none ? Well, it was just like that. The poor fools were snubbed and chidden by her, and at dagger's point one to the other. I was wondering how it would all end, and felt sure something must happen soon, when something did happen and end it. Charlie i vanished as completely as f the ground had opened and swallowed him. For three days and three nights we went through the dense groves that surrounded the little town where we were quartered, and Richard went along with us calling the loudest. Once I came upon him, his face lit by the dim light of his lantern, and was amazed and shocked by its expression. 'Charlie,' he would call, and then strain forward with an awful look i of horror upon his face as f listening for some- thing whose voice would have killed him if it had replied. All these days he never seemed to remember the woman or go near her. I thought she was forgotten, but did not know. He had no time to look for her, as the minute he got back from the search he flung himself upon the couch and was instantly asleep with the heavy exhaustion of a man whose brain is too weary to think, and</p>
        <p>whose body is too tired to be troubled by anything save brute weariness. For three days and three nights we sought Charlie. and I thought we should never find him. I was sure some wild beast had taken him away if he had lain dead, as I feared, in those wild bits of land where we sought him. And all the time a cruel suspicion crept around my heart. I looked at Richard growing more ghastly and hollow-eyed every day, and felt I could not speak to him; often we worked side by side without a word.</p>
        <p>"One day I took a path I had not seen before; it seemed freshly trampled down and gave me some hope as I stepped along it. But Richard made an effort to persuade me some other way, which I refused sharply. He sighed as he followed, his eyes upon the ground, no longer seeking from side to side as they had been. 'Don't let us be the ones to find him,' I heard him mutter, and I turned upon him quickly. Why ?' I demanded. He seemed distressed. "'He is sure to be found by the others soon. It would be awful to come upon him lying for three nights out here dead—all alone, poor lad.' He stopped with a half sob, but I pushed forward and came upon the corpse. He was lying behind a tuft of grass, his rifle to his shoulder, his hand upon the trigger—stone dead. I saw a little red mark under his hair, upon his forehead, and turned to where Richard was standing looking at me.</p>
        <p>" 6 You have killed this man,' I accused him, and he did not flinch.</p>
        <p>"In fair fight,' he answered, softly, all his fear and horror gone now he stood before the dead. You can understand how with that thing lying there he could not rest, but had to keep his brain from thinking by pretending to seek what only he could have found without seeking. 'lt was a duel. I lay here '—he pointed to a stone— 'and he lay there. It was a fair duel. It had come to that between us; there was no other way.' We said: "The best man wins," and we shook hands, and when we had counted three we fired. And now there will be no more quarrelling.' He whispered to the corpse, laying his hand upon it, 'You said, you know, "The beat man wins." ' "The movement of his hand must have shaken the body so that the stiff finger moved on the trigger. There was a loud report from the dead man's rifle, and Richard fell across the body with a gasping sentence unfinished in his throat—' The best man wins.'</p>
        <p>"It was an uncanny thing," the Major added after a long pause, during which the listeners filled their pipes in silence, "the duel between the living and the dead. I cannot get over the feeling of horror it has given me ever since." "And what did the woman do when she heard of it ?" a lad questioned. Death was a tale to him —he had never seen it.</p>
        <p>"Do? Oh, she went into hysterics, and wept and shrieked so it was impossible to comfort her for a week," said the Major, scornfully. "And who tried to ? " questioned the enfant terrible.</p>
        <p>"I think I will take a turn outside," eaid the Major. "It is getting very warm in here.' [THE END.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>CONFIRMATION AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday evening the Bishop of Chester held a. onfirmation service at Runcorn, there being 130 candi- dates from Runcorn parish, and about 100 from the parishes of Holy Trinity, Runcorn, Frodsham, Halton, and Weston Point. BOARD OF GUARDIANS ELECTION.</p>
        <p>There were contests in two wards only at Runcorn for the Board of Guardians on Saturday. In Central Ward the retiring members,Messrs. T. Leaand T. Wood, were opposed by the Rev. Father Donlevy, who ousted Mr. Wood. The figures were—Donlevy, 219 ; Lea 156 ; Wood. 146. In Newtown Ward Mr. J. W. Temperley was returned by 174 votes to Mr. M. Thomas 129. The following were returned unopposed ; Halton Ward Mr. S. Taylor, Mersey Ward, Mr, W. Morris ; Bridgewater Ward, Mr. J. Douglas ; and Heath Ward, Mr. A. R, Norman, J.P. BEYOND CONTROL.</p>
        <p>At Widnes on Monday, a young lad named John Mere- dith,aged twelve years, was ordered to the training ship Clio on the recommendation of Mr. G. H. Danhy,Director of Education. Last week he was charged with stealing a. pair of slippers, and after the case, in which his mother said he was beyond control, it was decided to leave the matter in the hands of Mr. Danby to find him a school. The boy had previously been in an industrial school,but according to the rules laid down by the Home Office leave to come out on licence could be claimed. The bench were advised to send him to the Clio, and the order was made for the boy to remain there for four years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>ST. HELENS TR IDESNIAN'S DEATH.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS TR IDESNIAN'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>Mr. Samuel Thompson Beckett, one of the most res- pected tradesmen of St. Helens, died early on Wedkes- day at his residence, 134, Prescot-road. Mr. Beckett was sixty-eight years of age. He belonged to a Shrop- shire family, and came to St. Helens in 1859 to the well-known building firm of Messrs. G. Harris and Son. About eighteen years ago Mr. Beckett established a building business and sawmills of his own in Tontine street. He had been in failing health for two or three years, and for the last fortnight had been suffering from a paralytic seizure. Mrs. Beckett died four years ago, and the family consists of four sons and six daughters. The funeral will take place at St. Thomas's Cemetery on Saturday, Mr. Beckett and his family having for many years been connected with St. Thomas's Church.</p>
        <p>For the next fortnight quietness will brood over the Law Courts, and his Majesty's Judges will seek in the country lanes fresh strength to grapple with arrears of work. The Lord Chief Justice has gone to his country' house at Guildford, Sir Francis Jeune to Newbury, Berks, Mr. Justice Grantham to Lewes, Mr. Justice Bruce to Berwick-on-Tweed, Mr. Justice Ridley to Crawley, Sussex, Mr. Justice Darling, to Brockenhurst, and Mr. Justice Buchnill to Littlestone, Kent.</p>
