      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>FREE CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>THE FREE CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>CHINESE LABOUR AND THE TEMPERANCE QUESTION. rjhe resolutions passed at the National Free ;-"furch Council Meetings at Newcastle-on- Yee on the Temperance and Chinese Labour Vuestions have been forwarded to Mr. Balfour tld leading members of the Government. hether these gentlemen will take any notice r, Free Church protests remains to be seen. 'ertainly, in the past, the Government has ab- Boa lutely ignored the opinion of Nonconformists, +lid there is no reason to believe that after their tleatmen „ tof the Free Churches in reilard to zducation Act, that they will treat with #l.lYthing like respect the opinions expressed by ""le half of the religious population of the ec'entry.</p>
        <p>lUPRESSIONS OF TILE COUNCIL MEETINGS. 8 The Free Church Chronicle for April is a 466,1 number' devoted to the Annual !etings at Newcastle - on - Tyne. In " General Impression," which the editor t"tributes, two points are noted as being 113:„Pical of the meetings. "In the first place,: ria-• SaYB2 " I was struck with the tone of pessi. rp,lBlll which pervaded many of the addresses. twEI very title of the President's address—' The ieatlutY of the Free Churches in an Age of ionof, to reveal that all things jiver() not as they should be; that God's in His vena„vi„en; but that all is not right with the 1,4"i1a:,'".' Another fact that impressed mil FPs arose from the first I have mentioned. conderlying all the deliberations of the assembly !Ild be noticed a strtng, deep longing for a e.lr.itual revival. The delegates present seemed th t 6 hungering and thirsting ' for spiritual gS• How eagerly and intensely they to Mr. Jowett's sermon treating of the et and the prophecy that come from God! tee Can survey with hope all the reactionary i_lidencies of our time if only there is present vutthe hearts of our people that deep hungering et the spiritual."</p>
        <p>MR. MEYER AS PRESIDENT. tblille same writer records his impression of 4e presidency of Mr. Meyer: "I think ivnbody was charmed with the way in p,le Mr. Meyer filled the high office of resident. His speech to the Mayor of New- ti 8"e was a model of dignity, and from that ocr" onwards, Mr. Meyer had ready for every weasien some appropriate word or flash of wit. rt 8 were struck by the fact that he was a I,:ister of assemblies, that he had a perfect °n the whole audience. His personal whnence seemed to sway that large body, and to en he on several occasions appealed to them e.od a session with becoming dignity, they re- lofted en bloc in spite of the attractions of the th-,,eu_ienii hour or the tea. Another thing which do"olegates noticed about our worthy Presi- as, his prayerfulness and personal piety. ih 'old not but feel that This is a good</p>
        <p>SIR GEORGE KEKEWICH'S VIEWS. A„Si.F George Kerewich, who was the only Itmg'jean speaker at the Free Church Council tings, gives his impressions in the Free Chronicle. "I think the gathering at e,lveastle " he says, "the most splendid "iencel ever addressed. Ido not mean in 2e.re numbers, though those were great, but in aluekness and keenness of appreciation, in fonestness, in unity of purpose, in enthusiasm Justice, and determination to obtain it. I tolt: especially struck by the absence of any Qi"" ef hostility to the Anglican Protestant for ureh• The prevailing spirit was that desire fivil and religious liberty which has always quguished the Free Churches, and to which, 'teed, they owe their very existence." FREE CHURCH YEAR BOOK. thUe "Free Church Year Book," containing official report of the proceedings at New- Z4le, together with the Federation Report, and Ohniass of information of interest to Free . nrchmen, will be published early in Arril. ;,1t work of reference this book is invaluable 13; tChurchmen. It forms, too, an interest- ary in itself. Such addresses as those !le e Rev. J. H. Jowett, for instance, will r,. to-perusal in verbatim report form. A 1111 OFFICIAL LIFE OF DR. CLIFFORD. beengl:?at event in the publishing world has he publication by the National Free forticu Council of the official life of Dr. • This is written from information sup- ed by the Doetor himself by Mr. C. T. Bate- ... Loin end is published at 6s. Undoubtedly the °f Writing the Doctor's life has been a (Mi.- `l(3ne, but Mr. Bateman is in evident sym- tPatilY with the work. Many of the incidents hecurded of the Doctor's.early I;fe are entirely ,2811, and the whole of his career is treated in -.I exhaustive way.</p>
        <p>TWO NEW SERIES. tOmother book which has just been issued by 0 Publication Department of the National • %ft:led deals with "The Lollards." It forms first of a series of "Eras of Nonconformity," h'lted by the Rev. C. Silvester Horne, M.A. It fkaB already met with a good reception, and are is every probability of the series being a " popular one. Another series to be pub. Lttued by the National Council, and entitled tiittle Books on the Devout Life," is to be inzenced by the volume of "Lessons from .076,, Cross," by the Rev. Charles Brown, the 411-known Baptist minister of Ferme-park, hicildon• The second volume in the series, h W . ja, • Is edited by the Rev. F. B. Meyer, 8.A., is understood, to be from the pen of the v. Alfred Rowland, D.D.</p>
        <p>FAREWELL TO GIPSY SMITH. l‘i(liPsY Smith, the well-known missioner of the Itti°l2al Free Church Council, has sailed to b,(111t11 Africa on a mission tour. Several im- kteuive farewells were arranged. At Waterloo thati°n, the Rev. Thomas Law and the staff of „e National Council, together with his fellow Zoner, Mr. W. R. Lane, and many others azt leave of the Gipsy, and at Southampton tr a thousand people, mostly connected with 14,!ii.ocal Free Church Council mustered °Tillie 47ing Stage. In view of the state of South fo,„„ell, Gipsy Smith's mission there is looked ward to with great interest. FREE CHURCH COUNCIL HOUSE. pOl4, h interest has been created in the pro- the Rev. Thomas Law to build a Free tremrch Council House. When the Federation lifstarted, a small office in Birmingham was Noswidient for its needs. Since 1896, however, tarn the headquarters of the Movement were cona,b;lhhed at the Memorial Hall, London, lin,"'3111„al progress has been noted all along the .tvery nook and corner available at the it 8-'°rial Ball has been secured, until to-day 161118 absolutely necessary to start building 0 "se for the purpose of the Free Church (311ncil.</p>
        <p>Tt. PROGRESS IN THE GIRLS' GUILD. ue -uuGirls' Guild in connection with the Free rch grilse Council is making very steady pro- -24 • At Cambridge, for instance, on March very successful Industrial Exhibition and be Was held. Twelve permanent homes have iaoee w° established in the country, and the Guild 411tabers 12,000 members. Th. APIECE OF AUTOBIOGRAPHY. Char„.l"l is an age for autobiography, and one •Pre--'eg fragtnent appears in this month's 1410 e Churchman. Mr. George Clarke, the well- inissioner—who, of course, is not a Free ate I.°h/tau—tells in a beautiful way "Why I 44 Christian." Those who have had the plea- clut °f listening to Mr. Clarke will not miss this riteteristic article.</p>
        <p>ItehA• GUARRANTEED CURE FOR PILES. sreautt_ng, blind bleeding or protruding. All chemists kerit tt,!orised by, the maiitifacturers of Pazo Pile Oint- cas, oi) refund the money where it fails to cure any ordiiin,yiles, no matter of bow long standing.. Cures resiJ e,ases in six days. One application gives ease 44.1nech,• Ibis is a new iliscovery, and is the only Pile hetVOid on apositive guarantee. Price 2s. 3d. of all 840:i or t irect from PARIS MEDICINE CO. Dept .S,) a neilendon, E.C.</p>
