      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>CANADA AND PREFERENTIAL</head>
        <p>CANADA AND PREFERENTIAL</p>
        <p>TARIFFS.</p>
        <p>The following remarks by Mr. Charles Lancaster, of Liverpool,on the attitude of Canada towards preferential tariffs, were made at the meeting of the Warrington Chamber of Commerce last week. Imagine, if you please, he said a typical specimen of a Britisher,brought up in this country of Free Trade, with no natural or in- herited Protectionist feelings, but who, in a few years. brings himself quite into line with the Canadian view ; what I myself heard, in fact, from my own friends of this kind in Canada last autumn. You will understand that in what follows I am reproducing the view held by the great majority of all Canadians, as well as by English people who have lived in both countries. From such persons as I have described we should hear some- thing like the following :—" The two countries, Great Britain and Canada, do not thoroughly understand one another in the matter of fiscal policy. Canadians do not understand the underlying principles which would make the adoption of a high protective policy in Eng- land as great a madness as the introduction of Free Trade in Canada would be, and the ordinary Englishman cannot understand the special conditions which render a full protective tariff necessary for Canada. We here in Canada see the absolute necessity for a different policy, owing to the difference of economic and industrial con- ditions, and particularly to the artificial competition to which Canada is subjected in the manner of her labour supply in the highly artificial system of her neighbours the Ifni ted States. It is very desirable that these differ- ences should be understood by the rank and file of the people, as it is a necessary preliminary to that sympathy and mutual support which we all wish to foster, in order to strengthen the united national life of the Empire. We Canadians therefore think that Canada's interest in the domestic change which has been pro- posed in England at the present time is very deep and very real, because we desire, above everything, to see this closer understanding promoted; in the main we are Imperialists to the back-bone, and on account of this Imperialism our loyalty is of the deepest possible character. As to the question of the imposition of a tariff upon stuffs, with a view ultimately to the pro- motion of Imperial preference, and to turning the streams of energy and development in Canada into such channels as shall result in the strengthening of the ties of Empire, instead of allowing them to develop along channels which might drag us. however unwilling, in some other direction, no one who understands us, and has any interest whatever in our welfare—even a senti- mental interest—could advocate such an arrangement as an</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>IMPERIAL FREE TRADE ZOLLVEREIN.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL FREE TRADE ZOLLVEREIN.</p>
        <p>This woui-cl-si;;;PIY-re-s-t;it- in arresting whatever progress the Colonies had made, absolutely crippling them in competition with their artificially. developed neigh- bours, and instead of their -growing into strong nations, bearing up the wealthy mother-country upon the strong arms of their own industry and natural resources, they would hang as heavy weights upon her at a time when she is feeling the competition of growing foreign States more keenly than ever she has done before, and at a time when, owing to many causes which would take too long to enumerate and explain, she must inevitably take a second place in certain of her interests in which she has hitherto held the pre-eminence. The scheme of an Imperial Free Trade Zollverein, therefore, to us in Canada is as impracticable as it is undesirable from a Colonial or Imperial point of view. With regard to this question of taxing food, we in Canada are striving from the Zollverein idea to find something more practic- able and equally efficacious in binding us together. Colonials as a whole believe, whatever is said to the contrary, that a slight duty on foodstuffs, with a PREFERENCE IN FAVOUR OF THE COLONIES, would just be sufficient at the present time to divert the e..ormous commercial development which is now taking place, say in Canada, from those lines which would tend to throw her continually more and more into the arms of an uncongenial neighbouring State, into her natural line of development which is along the lines of Imperial unity. We believe that this method is the only method practi- cable for producing this result. Then you English- men say, 'Well, if this is the case, why is the Colonial not more excited about it? Why is be not agitating in support of the preferential tariff? In reply to this, it may be pointed out that the reason the Colonies are not showing themselves more actively keen is that the Colonies feel themselves quite strong enough to build themselves up upon the foundations which they have al ready laid,and in the laying of which they have taxed- themselves to the uttermost and poured money out lavishly. We have now got to that point in which we are beginning to reap the results of our early trials and expenditure. Canada can to-day produce pig iron and produce her grain at a lower cost f.o.b. the Atlantic than any competing nation, and this is largely due to our grand transportation facilities. We know that we shall supply the consuming markets with our principal products; we know that this development, upon the eve of which we now stand, will inevitably—and in spite of any desire en the part of our own people—draw us into much more intimate relations with those markets.Weknow that the trend of our trade will govern the direction of our future evolution. At the same time, we know eqUally well that our evolution will be equally rapid and satisfactory in whichever direction the bulk of our trade goes. We, therefore, say that the great desire in the heart of every loyal Canadian at the present time is on this account, to see that trade development run in the direction of</p>
        <p>THE OLD COUNTRY AND HER COLONIES..</p>
        <p>We feel that there is between the British nations, in the matter of their institutions, in their conception of true liberty, in their social life, a common bond which would render a closer connection than a purely senti- mental one—absolutely in accord with the existing conditions' and it is the earnest gesir9 of every loyal Canadian to see progress wade in this direction. At the same time we feel that Great Britain, as the axle upon which the Imperial wheel turns is the proper source of any official overture which should be made. We feel further, and believe that Great Britain has more to gain from tne development of such a policy than Canada has. We therefore wait to see what (3reat Britain has to say upon the matter before we take any active steps to influence her. We have '.one as far as it is possible to go with any self- respect by our policy of the last few years, suggestiog fiscal development on the lines of an Imperial prefer- ence, and by many of the speeches of our public men, as well as the actions of our Government, have shewn the direction in which our thoughts are running. If Great Britain cannot see the wisdom of encouraging thi: idea and rejects it, we in Canada will still be loyal in sentiment, but she will not allow her progress to be stopped. We will turn to some other country which understands our value better, and our trade will thrive in other channels. England will lose an enormous source of strength and power, Canada will lose nothing except all that her British Citizenship holds dear. The result will be an increase in Canada of the mercenary spirit of the United States,and a great loss to her national life. A far reaching check will have been given to he efforts nov being made by all that is best among tCanadians to increase thelmperial pride and Patriotism of Canadian youth. "If it is asked 'WHAT IS CANADA GOING TO GIVE us?'</p>
        <p>_ —a more petty and trifling question could not be put in connection with so importantand grand a movement.' "It seems absurd to us that England should expect for one moment, or that she should think it to her advan- tage that Canada should cripple in any way her own manufacturing development, especially as 40 per cent. f the Canadian working population is connected with manufacturing industries. The whole matter, it seems to us, as it is argued by many Englishmen is away off the true line and the oply line which can lead to really statesmanlike and practically useful conclusions. "We cannot believe that Great Britain will turn aside from such a grand future as is opened out to her at the present time We do believe, however' from the experiences of the past, that she may be along time before she alters her present frame of mind. Not for one moment do we deprecate the high ideals which have influenced her in the past, nor do we think that these high ideals are any worse to-day than they were in Cobden's days, but we believe that such grand practical results as are now held out to the British Empire must not be sacrificed for any academic ideal, however practical it might be if surrounded by an ideal environment. We in Canada cannot see why the facing of patent facts and the adop- tion of what appears to be the policy, pointed out by every circumstance in our political growth, should be arrested on account of a fear lest England should, for the first time in its history, prove either too lazy or too stupid to deal withstle new conditions which are sure to arise as the rt.sult of the inauguration of anew policy." (Applause.)</p>
        <p>There are at present being built at the request of the British Admiralty by Messrs. Caramel', Laird, and Com- pany, at the Birkenhead shipbuilding yard, two new scouts intended to be the fastest ships in the world. In making this announcement at the annual meeting of the company at Sheffield on Wednesday, Mr. Laird, repre- senting the Birkenhead shipbuilders receotly amalga- mated with the Sheffield steel firm, said that Russia had hitherto laid claim to possessing the fastest ships of this class, but against the twenty-four knots of the Russian vessels the Canimell-Laird scouts were designed for twenty-five knots. They were to be of 3,000 tons displacement, and had been specially def...ignecl by the company.</p>
        <p>A marked plaice caught off Wincbelsea' Sussex, was placed on the Leman Ground, in the North Sea, on December 12. It was then 13in. in length. When re- captured at Winchelsea it was 15in. long. The fish had ravelled about 200 miles.</p>
      </div>
