      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDIT3R,I</p>
        <p>-1-10TY6E TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>Authors of letters intended for publication must inall cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith. Matter intended for publication must be written on one side only of the paper ad mat in, if possible,not later than Thursday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Ragged School Soup Kitchen.</head>
        <p>The Ragged School Soup Kitchen.</p>
        <p>- - The soup kitchen has been open daily this week, there being no falling off ia the number of applicants for food. The Committee thankfnlly acknowledge the following donations received during the week :—Miss Pierpoint (St. Austin's) 10s.•' J. Charlton Parr, Esq., £5 ; the Warrington (apportionment of Mrs. Wilkinson's donation) 255.; Mr. Alfred Hydrae' 2s. 6d. Letters of appreciation and encouragement have also been received from the donors. We are also again indebted to Messrs. G. Bowcock, J. Bowcock, Mynekeyne, Peat, Cooper, Owen' Tunstall, for meat; Mr. Harding, Miss Gill, for bread; Messrs. J. Hallwood, Reid, Roylance, for vegetables, bread, etc, The money received will about defray the expenses to date, and although we are implored by many of the recipients not 'to close the kitchen (being almost depen- dent on it) we shall be compelled to do so, unless further donations are forthcoming. On behalf of the Committee, JOHN W. HOWARTH. March 31.</p>
        <p>Class Legislation. Sir,--Olass legislation, that is legislation by dukes, lords, squires, bishops, and other men in high social position, has reigned in this country from time im- memorial. And the results are strikingly visible where- ever we turn. On the one hand we see that most of the land of England is in the hands of a few privileged families ; that the banks are full of money; that millions are passing through the Stock Exchange daily; that capital is going about seeking new fields for pro- fitable investment ; while the press supplies us every morning with lists of men and women who have died aid left thousands and tens of thousands behind them. All this must be very satisfactory and encouraging to the wealthy people who are interested in it, and they will, no doubt, live on in the com- fortable hope that this state of things will continue and still further develop for their especial benefit. But there are other results of class legislation which are quite as deserving of notice as the ones enumerated above. It is said on fairly reliable testi- mony that over thirty per cent of the inhabitants of this country are so poor and depressed that, they are , neither housed, fed,nor clothed as human beings should be. And in addition to this distressing state of things it may be added that the workhouses, prisons and asylums are full of inmates, and that vast numbers of people able and willing to work can find no work to do. Surely this abundance of wealth and luxury on the one hand, and privation and poverty on the other, cannot be the arrangement of God who is no respecter of perso as. He is loving unto every man, and His mercies are over all His works. It is, as Robert Burns says, "Man's inhumanity to man that makes countless thousands mourn." God has provided more than abund- ance for the wants of all his creatures, and all are equally entitled to his free gifts, the only stipulation being that all should labour for their bread. lie that will not work neither should he eat. But never, per- haps, in the world's history were men more greedy of gain than they are now, and never more inclined to shirk work. Such men are not content with their lot, but must needs oppress and defraud their fellow creatures in order to live at ease and accumulate wealth. This is the secret which underlies the land laws, and accounts for the "sweating" that is carried on in so many departments of labour. Ejpenditure in wages which must be sternly kept down in order that profits may rise, seems to be the general rule in business, whoever may suffer thereby. And as it has been under the regime of class legis- lation that these ithings have been brought, about, it would surely be foolish to expect that a continuation of the same system would produce any better fruit in the future. Impossible! The Leopard cannot change his spots, nor the Ethiopian his skin. If the conditions and surroundings of life in this country are ever to be changed for the better as regards the great toiling world of men and women, class legislation must be abolished for ever, and political factions driven from all our local and im- perial councils. There is no serious difference between Tory and Liberal politicians in matters of policy. Both represent classism—both desire to conserve a monopoly of wealth and power' and both are lavish and extrava- gant in spending the hard earnings of the people. And he would be a simple minded man indeed who could believe that our political parliamentarians expended large sums of money to get into Parliament for the sole benefit of their constituents. Experience teaches us a different tale. Whatever fair promisgs a candidate may make when he is before the electors,he is only too prone to let them slip from his memory in the con- genial atmosphere of the House of polished gentlemen. In truth it is not uncharitable to say that the average member goes into Parliament on his own account first, and afterwards for the people, when he can spare a speech or a nod for them. And all this need not sur- prise us when we recollect that the member is only human, and quite as wide awake to his own interests as are more plebian people. Great honour is attached to a seat in the House, and man; valuable sinecures Are in the gift of the Crown. And, of course, the voting generally proceeds upon party lines, the Tory supporting his shibboleth and the Liberal his, only too often indifferent to the justice or injustice of the measure voted upon. How long will the patient people who have the nation's debt upon their shoulders lend them- selves to this mischievous quackery of Government. How long will they permittheirmanhood to be degraded by being pitted into hostile camps for the sport of political philistines? By a happy, and opportune turn of Providence they can now make and unmake Parlia- ments at will. And surely the time is fully ripe for a drastic change in our legislature. The people have been too long befooled by Tory and Liberal alike in regard to reforms which hardly touch the fringe of the evils which oppress and impoverish them. The huge monopolies of land, wages, and liquor must be abolished and the National expenditure reduced to the minimum before real prosperity and happinsss can prevail in this country. The classes having failed to exercise righteousness and mercy in dealing with the people, it is time that the people themselves should try their hand at legislation for the common good. They could not possibly make a greater mess than their predecessors have done. But, to come nearerhome, will the working men of Warrington put their shoulders to the wheel to help forward a reform of Parliament? They need not go to Bank Quay or St. Austin's for a man to lead them. In a town of sixty-seven thousand inhabitants surely at least one intelligent, fdirly educated and able working man could be found to voice his fellow workers of al classes in the House of Commons. Yours truly, TONA. March, 1901.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Chinese Indentured . Labour in South</head>
        <p>Chinese Indentured . Labour in South</p>
        <p>Africa.</p>
        <p>The letter of Mr. F. B. Grimshaw in your last week's issue fails to convince methatthe introduction of Chinese Labour in South Africa under restrictive conditions is either wise or right. I would put this question. Seeing that goldmines in different parts of the world are pro- fitably worked by white labour, what is there in the condition of the Transvaal mines to make white labour impossible if similar conditions as to surroundings and wages are created? Mr. Grimshaw quotes from a letter from South Africa the words "the people are not desirous of seeitv, white men do natives' work, and perhaps working alongside them." Who are these people whose desires are to govern the situation? Is gold mining a degrading form of labour in South Africa, and i an honourable one n Australia,Klondyke and California, and if so,why ? Does not the following words of one of the mining directors give the true key to the situation? "We have settled the question of native labour, and have reduced the wages by one third without any diffi- culty. We have now the more difficult problem of white labour before us. I do not at all approve of suddenly reducing wages, and so producing a strike. That is In every way undesirable. What we have to do is to shut down some of our poorer mines, and let their men walk about the streets for two or three months until their feet are on the pavement. They'll be glad enough then to take whatever we may choose to offer them." Now as to profits, and the ability to pay a white man's wage. Fourteen of the leading gold mines have for the last ten years paid an average dividend of 60 per cent. much of the stock being watered. In that period therefore the shareholders have received 600 per cent., and as a result el ery El share can be sold at £7. Those who have visited the Transvaal Mines tell us that the conditions of the compounds are degrading to humanity, but there is nothing in the work itself that the white man cannot do, and nothing he would not do if the conditions of life were made respectable, and the remuneration made adequate. In WO of the shortage of labour, largely created by the raineowners cutting the wages, the output of gold is now 141 millions, against 16 millions before the war, and the number seeking employment is increasing every month. Mr. Grimshaw quotes a passage from a correspondent, wnich is rather unfortunate from his standpoint. His friend writes: "It is simply disheartening to walk: round the Post Office in Johannesburg, and see the hun- dreds of fellows with nothing to do, and nothing to eat, right from the lowest class of individuals to the best class of gentlemen." The only way of bringing the work, and the out of work g together, is for the mineowners to abandon the degrading compound system,to employ labour that does not require guarding by a ring fence, and to give such remuneration as will enable white men to earn a decent living; in a word to let this industry stand on its merits in turn with all natural industries, and without preference. But this would mean the reduction of dividends, or which the mineowners and their absent shareholders</p>
        <p>will fight to the last. Of course lam well aware that at Johannesburg the population is largely composed of artizans and therefore cannot at once adapt itself to mining. Thisis the result of the false economic con- ditions, which have been created by wrong methods in the past. I am ready to grant, that onthe ground of expediency the introduction of Chinese Labour is a way out of the difficulty, but I do not believe that it is as Mr. Grimshaw puts it "at present the only way out of the difficulty." Let the mineowners do for the mines in the Colonies, and the Mother Country, what they are ready to do for the Chinese, omitting the restrictions as regards their freedom when not at work, but let the wage be governed by the market value of white labour. If the wage that can be offered in competition with other natural indus- tries is such that it will not enlist workers, then the industry has no right to exist under dishonouring and unnatural conditions, and the conditions are such if it is necessary for the legislature to interfere, and intro- duce indentured Asiatic low priced labour. Archdeacon Wilson goes to the root of the higher aspects of the question when he says. "If he were asked on what ground at bottom he objected to Chinese importation into South Africa, his answer would be that it was always wrong and unchristian to regard human beings as a means and not as an end." Mr. Grimshaw says " One must remember the class of people. The ordinance has been drafted for the pro- tection and safety of the white popnlation." Exactly, but if these people are of such a character that the in- habitants of South Africa have to be protected against them by keeping them in enclosures, why introduce them at alt? But says the mineowner, we must have the gold, and we must have it cheap. 'The gold is the dominating factor' the means to get it is secondary. Human beings are the means, the gold is the end. Good may come out of evil and it may be that the raisirg of the Chinese aspect of the question may turn the fierce searchlight of public inquiry on to the gold mining rand monopoly and all its doings. Under such a light how will the compound system for native labour appear apart altogether from the Asiatic praposal ? I do not mean the dividend aspect but the humane. Does it help to raise the black or any other race, for these men to be separated from their homes and families, and brought in touch with civilization uneer the conditions which exist in the coMpounds. The general effect of any attempt to civilize lower races by taking them out of their natural environments and bringing them into touch with EurNpean races under conditions unnatural to them is that the lower race appropriates the vices of the so-called higher race and fails to assimilate the virtues. They are debased instead of being raised. And there is much reason to fear this has already_been the result in South Africa. CHARLES E. PARKER. Penketh, March 31st, 1901.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>FORESTERS' ANNUAL SUPPER. The Question of Meeting on Licensed Premises.</head>
        <p>FORESTERS' ANNUAL SUPPER. The Question of Meeting on Licensed Premises.</p>
        <p>On Monday last, the annual supper and smoking con- cert in connection with Court " Jaaziel " Nunber 138 of Ancient Order of Foresters, wm held at the Court house, the White Bull Inn, Horsemarket street. The supper was well served by the host and hostess Bro. and Mrs. Newall. After supper the district chief ranger,Bro. R. Thornley presided over a good attendance of mem'. bers and friends, and was supported by P.D.C.R.G. Maybury, P.D.C.R. W. Bullock, and Bro. Councillor Peacobk, district auditor. The chairman said he was sure that members had every confidence in their court which was the oldest in town and also the pioneer of Forestry in the District Bro. Maybury, in proposing the toast "The Ancient Order of Foresters and Warrington District" gave an interesting account of the workino, of the Order and said that their District was one of the oldest in conec- tion with the Order, being an offshoot of the Royal Order of Foresters which ceased to exist in the latter part of the 18th century. Bro. Peacock, in responding, said the officers had of late devoted considerable time to the improvement of the District, and he was very hopeful of success. Referring to their meeting houses he would like to see the Courts united in a grand effort to provide themselves with a proper meeting place of their own, altogether apart from the public house, as they were often told that numbers of young men objected entirely to meetings on licensed premises. He believed be owed his success at the recent election to the fact thathe was a friendly society member and he begged to thank them for their unsolicited help on that occasion.—Bro. Bullock then proposed Court" Jaaziel" He lied noticed that the Court had passed through a very trying year, but it must be a consolation to them all to know that they had emerged from that trial with credit to themselves and with honour to the Court and the Order.—Bro. Bullock then dedlt at some length with the Tontine societies that had recently sprung up amongst them and he asked that all members should endeavour to show their fellow men how utterly useless were these so called societies compared with such as their own. Songs were creditably rendered by Mr. Hignet, Mr. F. Warburton and Mr. R. Hamblett, Mr. Hignet also contributing% piano solo. The speakers and entertainers were thanked on the proposition of Bro.Healey,seconded by Bro. J. Walsh.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>LOCAL MINISTERS AND THE POWER OF THE DRINK TRADE.</head>
        <p>LOCAL MINISTERS AND THE POWER OF THE DRINK TRADE.</p>
        <p>The last public mooting of the season in connection with the Wycliffe Temperance Society was held on Wednesday evening. The R3V. I-1. Wall presided, and said the temperance movement was to him next in importance only to the Christian gospel and the preach- ing of the gospel. Next to winning souls for Christ he believed the great work of the church was that of winning men from the curse of strong drink. There was a great need for hands of hope and temperance societies. He noticed the other day that 000,000,000 were invested in Great Britain alone in the manufac- ture and sale of strong drink. An enemy that was backed by £300,000,000 was not a weak enemy, and one that was likely soon to be defeated. Although the forces arrayed against the temper- ance cause were so strong, yet trusting in God and fighang for the right and the truth, they, who were engaged in the great temperance cause must ultimately prevail. Workingmen had every reason to be interested in the progress of temperance. In the North of Ireland £3,000,000 were invested in the manufacture and sale of strong drink and that capital found employment for 3,000 workpeople. The same amount of capital invested in the manufacture of linen found employment not for 3,000 but 103,000. When they. considered the amount of money invested in the drink trade, was there any wonder that the question of the unemployed was so often brought before them. If that money were invested in some other trades, the large num- bers of men who were now hanging about street corners would be able to get a decent and honestliving. (Hear,boar.)There was much talk about fiscal reform,but he thought if there was a little more reform at home in spending our money on home manufactures instead 'of I intoxicating drink, we would have little ?need to be afraid of foreign competition. If instead of maintaining our great brewing industries we spent money on home manufactures we should be able to find enough work for all the people in Great Britain. (Hear, hear.) The Rev. G. H. Carnson,in speaking of the monotony of town life, said there was no monotony so wretched as that of passing public-houses at every street corner. He knew one particular district where there were 13 public-houses within 200 yards. It was past compre- hension how they were kept up, for no other trade could keep alive 13 shops within such a short distance It had yet to be proved to him that alcohol was good for anybody. He knew that it did a great deal of harm and he was informed that the man who drank • alcoholic drinks day after day did more harm to his system than the man who got tipsy once a month and left it alone for three or four weeks. No man ever gained as the result of tak- ing intoxicating drink, but a great many lost their busi- ness, their health and social standing owing to their indulgence in it. If they gave compensation to the brewers," the trade" would be worth not £300,0 00,000 but £600.000.000 and the condition of the country would be deplorable. The influence of "the trade" was al- ready so great in Warrington that it was almost death to a man to speak on the subject. The ramifications of the business were so widespread that all classes of the people were affected by it. He was in favour of local option, but he was afraid that in Warrington local option would not be of much use in dealing with the trade. He was an advocate of temperance workers providing places for social enjoyment to counteract the attractiveness of the publicenouses for the working-classes. An excellent musical programme had been provided by Mr. T. F. Wallington. Solos were tastefully sung by Mr. W. J. Barnes, Miss A. Travis, Miss L. Travis, Miss F. Smith, and Miss A. Griffiths, the last-named lady being encored. Mr. F. Brown efficiently played as a cornet solo "Flight of ages," and was deservedly encored. A vote of thanks was passed to the speakers and artistes on the proposition of Mr. JOSEPH OWEN, seconded by Mr. JOHN THOMAS.</p>
        <p>An interesting conference on the education question took place on Tuesday. between Archbishop Bonnie and Mr. Lloyd George, M.P., at the former's house at West- minster. "It is understood," says the "Catholic Herald," "that the position of Catholics and Nonconformists under the Education Act was freely discussed, and there lire no irreconcilable differences between the parties." A sensation was caused at Philadelphia on Wednesday by the arrest by the coroner of the proprietors of a private hospital in that city, where it is alleged several young women have died from criminal operations. The coroner declares that a syndicate has existed for the disposal of infants born in this hospital, and further states that scores of infants' bodies have been thrown into the furnace.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, April 2, 1904</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April 2, 1904</p>
        <p>LABOUR, PROTECTION, AND EDUCATIONAL PROGRESS. THE RIGHT HONOURABLE. FROM THE TEACHERS' POINT OF . [By Mr. H. Whitham.l VIEW. I gather from various sources that the Right Hon. Joseph Chamberlain is having considerable difficulty in the unsaying of all that he said in the years that• are fled touching Protection. There was a time when we listened to him, believing or thinking that we were listening to Joseph sober. The Home Rule episode made us doubt his sobriety, and low that he has sprung the Protection trick upon the people the impression is almost a certainty that Joseph is drunk.. It was in 1873 Mr. Chamberlain made his first effort to get into Parliament. Mr. Mundella wished to have him as colleague in the representation of Sheffield. I was at that time a resident of the good old town and a voter. Political feeling was very strong, and the two political parties were pretty 'well broken up. The Trade Unions could not decide about their vote; at the last moment an old political warrior had appeared, and on the Conservative side, one who had always prided himself on being an Independent, but who had at the same time endeared himself to the Sheffield Radicals— John Arthur Roebuck. It was known among the wire-drawers that I knew some little of the great screw manufacturer's doings as an employer of labour and I was surprised one day at having to receive a deputation conveying to me an invitation to do a bit of speech-making on behalf of the Conservative candi- date. This proposition required a bit of thought. Chamberlain was a Radical. I also was a Radical, but it was also well understooi that Roebuck was much more of a Radical than he was Conservative. The end of it was I agreed to go into the fight, and do something towards putting Joseph at the bottom of the poll. Each evening during two long weeks, a carriage duly appeared at the ' mill gates to convey me home, there to make myself fit to appear before the public. My first appearance was at the Cutlers' Hall along with Mr. Roebuck and I suppose I must have said something, as my speech was in the newsboys hands long before I left town for Brightside. I remember speaking one eveaing at the Temperance Hall. Mr. Chamberlain was on the platform. An attempt was made to stop me. It was insisted that I had no vote; that I had not been a resident of the town long enough. 1 was prepared for that move, and had my credentials along with me. Another evening at Zion Chapel, ittercliffe, I was hoisted on the shoulders of a couple of men, and there from the midst of the audience I put my arguments in the shape of ques- tions to the aspirant for Parliamentary honours. The main feature was that Mr. Chamberlain's dealings with his men at Smethwick had been hostile to Labour Unions. His mills had been posted with notices that no Trade Unionists would in the future be employed therein. I remember the night, so well. Mr. Chamberlain rose white with temper, and said "That man follows me around like my shadow." I told him "I intended to do so until he agreed to my terms." It was ultimately decided that I should meet him and Mr. Mundella at the Victoria Hotel. If memory serves me right I think an old ship-mate Mr. David Halstead, went along with me. We had a great time, Chamberlain could not keep his seat, and at last when I had said something that cut very deep he bounced out of the room saying he "would stand it no longer." , Then I talked the matter over with Mundella, and when I again said something (I forget what) Mr. Mundella stopped me and said he would go up to Mr. Chamberlain and ascertain its truth. On his return he conveyed a message that ought I think, to become historical. Mr. Mundella said, " Mr. Chamberlain desires me to say that, he 'ought not to be held responsible for what his manager does, b ut, from this day he will take no further part in the busi- ness, but will devote his life to politics." So your humble servant must be held responsible for the figures that met Mr. Chamberlain's first effort at Parliamentary life which were :—Roebuck, 14,193; Mundella, 12,858; Chamberlain, 11,053 as well as the rest of troubles the nation has had with this South African warrior. Let us move on a little and look into a speech at Birmingham on November 12th, 1835. Mr. Cham- berlain was then opposing Protectionist fallacies. He said, "Now lam going to speak about a thing upon which I know something. I have been out of the screw, trade now for ten years or more, and I don't know anything about it in its present condition; but I do know what happened while I was a member of the screw firm, and really my experience has a rather important bearing upon this question. We made screws by the aid of an American invention —a most beautiful machine, which was imported from America. The same machine was sent to France, to Germany, to Russia, to Italy, to every country in the world almost, and therefore all these countries were able to make screws by the same pro- cess and in the same way as we were. Every one of these countries put a duty on screws from abroad. We, as you know,were perfectly open. Anyone could send screws without paying any duty at all. Now, then, what was the result? This was a case of hard- ship. (Laughter.) Perhaps we ought to have gone whining about the country asking for pro- tection for this wretched manufacture of ours, which was threatened by foreign competition. Now what was the fact ? The fact was this, that at the time of which I am speak- ing we sent screws into every country in the world, and no country was able to send screwa here. (Cheers.) Who benefited by it? Well, we did. (Laughter and cheers.) But the workmen benefited. (Cheers.) There were more of them em- ployed than had ever been employed before, and they were employed at better wages. (Hear, hear.) I travelled abroad at that time, and I went to the French and German and other factories, and I knew all that was going on, and in every case the wages of the working people who were making the same article were lower—much lower—in some cases one half of what we were paying, and the time in every case was longer. In France, for instance, they worked twelve hours a day when we were working nine hours. I say in every case; I should say in every case but one. In America the workpeople got higher wages than they did in England, but the cost of living was very much greater, and their position was really not so good as that of our workmen. Well, I asked you just now who benefited by this? Clearly the working classes in England benefited by our Free Trade system. I will tell you sometking that I don't think I have ever told in public before, a very curious incident in my commercial experience. At the time of which I am speaking, the Americans had a duty of about 100 per cent, upon screws. That was to protect this poor industry, to keep it in the country for the benefit of the working classes. Well in spite of that duty of 100 per cent. we were able to send screws there, and we did send very large quantities. What happened ? The American manufacturers came over here and they said, "We are making 100 per cent. upon our capital. If you continue to send screws to America we shall, of course, be obliged to reduce our prices. That will shut you out, and it will reduce our profits. That won't be good for either of us. Now let us make a bargain. We'll pay you so much a year to sit still, and not send a screw to America." (Laughter.) Well, they:did it— (loud laughter and cheers)—and my firm received a handsome income for years from the American manu- facturers protected as they were by the folly and stupidity of this Protectionist legislation. We received from those manufacturers a very considerable income to sit still with folded arms and send no screws to America. Well, but after a little while these American manufacturers want to Congress, and they said, " See here, here is a native manufacturer desires Protection—employs I. lot of working people—in danger of being ruined by competition from abroad. You must raise the duties." And they did raise the duties to about 120 or 150 per cent., and then, of course, our income ceased, and we were shut out. But now I want you to see who are the losers in this thing. Not the American manufac- turers, who were able to make 100 per cent., upon their capital and pay us a handsome subsidy. The only People who suffered were the working classes of the United States, who had to pay more for every screw they used, and every manufacture in which they were engaged was hampered and trammelled by the additional cost that was put upon.their materials. They, and they alone, bore the burden of this tax upon their industry and their labour. Well, gentlemen, if other people choose to cut off their tails, are you going to be so foolish? (No.) " The above speech contains a great many points that should afford thoughtful study to the workingman outside its politics, and placed in deadly parallel with one more recently delivered on his Imperial Commer- cial Union, in which he said that it was, "the most inspiring idea that has ever entered into the ininds of British statesmen " must lead us to question the right hon. gentlemen as to which shoulder he carries the chip on? March 2nd 1904.</p>
        <p>H. WHITHANI.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>WARRINGTON EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>THE FIRST ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>Mr. May's " Idiosyncracies."</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Education Committee was held in the Council Chamber at the Town Hall on Wednesday evening, for the annual election of officers and sub- committees. Mr. F. Monks, J.P., presided and there were also present: Alderman Bolton, Alderman Bennett, Alderman Pierpoint,Councillors Dodds, Ellison, Brough, Burton, Hulse, Davies and Rogers, Revs. John Yonge, Father Wilson, E. J. Willcocks, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, J. D. Buckton, J. Upson, J. F. Smith, E. Plinston, C. H. Unsworth, T. May, W. Bibby, with Mr. J. Moore Murray (director of education.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>M. MONKS RE-ELECTED CEIATRMAN.</head>
        <p>M. MONKS RE-ELECTED CEIATRMAN.</p>
        <p>Alderman PIERPOINT said he had very great pleasur in proposing that Mr. Monks be re-elected chairman. His work in the past was, they hoped, an earnest of what he would do in the future, and he (the speaker) did not think they could do better than re-elect him. Councillor ROGFIRS seconded the motion which was heartily carried. The CH t.IRMIN briefly returned thanks and expressed the hope that the work would proceed as comfortably next year as it had done in the past.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>DEPUTY-CEIAIRMAN.</head>
        <p>THE DEPUTY-CEIAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>Mr. UPSON moved that Alderman Bennett be re-elected deputy-chairman. Councillor DIVIES seconded.</p>
        <p>Councillor DODDS said he had the pleasure of moving Alderman Bennett's election on the last occasion. At that time Kr. Bennett was an elected councillor of the borough. He (Councillor Dodds) was rather in favour of having an elected deputy chairman, inasmuch as the chairman was a co-opted member. Alderman Bennett had done so well for them, although he was an alderman —which Was very obnoxious—(laughter)--that he (Councillor Dodds) would allow the matter to pass,and support Alderman Bennett's re-election. The motion was carried.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT said that when they appointed him to the office last year, he was so absolutely taken aback that in the stress of the moment he neglected to thank them Mr the honour they had done him. He would make good the omission now. He did not feel he had any special qualifications for the post, but he certainly took a profound interest in the progress of education in the town and throughout the country With such an admirable chairman,he thought they would be able to get on pretty well in the coming year.</p>
        <p>ME. MAY MOVES THE ABOLITION OF THE FINANCE COMMITTEE. AMUSING PASSAGES.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. JOLLY moved that the following members constitute the Finance Committee:—AldermenFairclough and Pierpoint, Councillors Dodds, Tinnion and Wilkin- son, Messrs. J. D Buckton, R. T. Fairclough, E. J. Guest, A. J. Jolley and J. Fulton Smith. Alderman PIERPOINT seconded. Mr. MA.Y moved an amendment "that the duties now performed by the Finance Committee be assigned to the General Purposes Committee, and that the separate control of finance be discontinued and relegated to the General Purposes Committee." He was not taking that course because he had any particular mistrust or dislike, or any such personal feeling against any member of the Finance Committee. His reason was because he had had some forty years' experience of public finance and he knew as a general principle, from his reading and study on the subject which had been very heavy and extensive, that local finances were not subject to proper control and checking in order to prevent irregularities. At the pre- sent moment there was something like 500 million pounds of borrowing in the air for local authorities. (Laughter.) The public funds within a very short period had fallen to the extent of 230 millions. Recently we had the Boer war— Alderman PIERPOINT: I rise to a point of order. The duty before us to-night is to elect the different committees.</p>
        <p>Mr MA.v : lan speaking of public finance, which very seriously affects this committee. The CHAIRMAN: Mr. May is giving his reasons for the amendment.</p>
        <p>Mr. MAY, continuing, said there were something like 100 millions that the British taxpayer was responsible for at the present moment, and the public credit of Great Britain, and especially the finances of local governing bodies were being discredited by such indiscriminate borrowing. The CHAIRMAN: I think that is too general a ques- tion.</p>
        <p>Mr. MAY: lam coming to the point. (Laughter.) It is very difficult for an inexperienced speaker who has not the sympathy of the meeting to do so, when there are so many unnecessary interruptions. It is a large and important question, and when I have the counten- ance of such members of your committee as Alderman Bennett, who has made a life time study of finance-</p>
        <p>Alder Man BENNETT; I don't want tc; interrupt, hal should be very sorry to countenance all the eccentrici- ties of my friend Mr. May—(laughter)—although I think he means well.</p>
        <p>Mr. MAY said Alderman Bennett had already counten- anced the contention:that their financial affairs should be managed more openly and above board and that they should have comparative statements of the expenditure month by month as compared with the previous year. That could now, with great ease and simplicity, be included in the minutes. They would then be able to see whether they were incur ring unnecessary or unauthorised expenditure. As a general principle,they knew that un- necessary taxation was fraud,and any liberal or unneces- sary expenditure was aiding and abetting a deliberate fraud on the ratepayers. ("Oh.") The CffAIRMA.N : I think you should not use such words, Mr. May. Mr. MAY: I don't apply it to this committee. I say as a general principle. The CHAIRMAN : Who does it apply to? Mr. MAY observed that it had been said that the poor did not pay the rates. They were told that only .£3,000 was paid by those whose rent was under Ss. per week. He would remind them that the whisky and lbeer money was to a very large extent paid by the working classes. (Laughter.) The CHAIRMAN: When are you coming to the point? (Laughter.) Mr. MAY said they were told that the great coth- panies had not a voice in the disposal of the rates. They had, however, something better than that in the shape of a monopoly,and they could charge what they liked for conveying'workingmen to and from work. After all,the poor were paying the rates and when the authorities put an unnecessary penny or twopence in the £ on the rates they did not know how many small trades people or members of the working classes they might be crepriv- ing of the necessaries of life. He also wished to draw attention to the question of turning babies, between the ages of three and five years, out of the schools. (Laughter.) The MAYOR: That is a matter for the School Management Committee. Mr. MAY: The matter will affect the rates and I think it ought not to be persevered in. It would bring a scandal on the committee. (Laughter.) I submit that those who pay should have their say as well as those who pocket the rates. (Laughter.) rhere was no seconder and the amendment therefore fell to the ground. Councillor ROGERS was sure that all the members of the Fine/fee Committee were above suspicion. Mr. PLINSTON pointed out that several gentlemen had not been placed on any committees. With all due res- pect to Mr. Fulton Smith, he noticed that that gentle- man was on three committees. He would suggest that Mr. C. H. Unsworth be elected to the Finance Com- mittee in place of Mr. Fulton Smith. The Rev. JOHN YONGE said he did not think there was any regulation that the committees were to consist simply of ten persons. He took it that the Education Committee could appoint any number of persons they chose. Mr. MURRAY: That is so. _ _</p>
        <p>The Rev. JOHN YONGE: I beg to move an amend- ment, which I hope will meet with the acceptance of the committee, that we add the name of Mr. Thomas May to the Finance Committee. (Laughter.) Alderman FIERPOINT: That will get us out of the difficulty, and I have very great pleasure in seconding Mr. FULTON SMITH expressed his willingness to retire from the Finance Committee if it was the wish of the committee. Councillor ROGERS objected to Mr. Fulton Smith's name being withdrawn, as he was very capable of deal- ing with the borough finances. He also supported Mr. May's election. Alderman BENNETT said that while he had the greatest admiration for Mr. May and was quite sure that he meant extremely well, he did not think it would be wise to alter the whole of the arrangements of the committee to satisfy Mr. May's personal idiosyncracies. (Laughter.) He thought Mr. May had every legitimate opportunity of examining the accounts as much as he liked. The committee ought not, in his opinion, practically to stultify themselves and entirely reverse the original arrangements even to oblige Mr. May. He would be glad to see Mr. May on the com- mittee in the ordinary way, but he did not think they ought to have a sort of constitutional revolution even to secure that desirable end. (Laughter.) Mr. MAY: I should be putting myself in a false position by going on the committee, and I have not the least wish to do so. Councillor DODDS said it was proposed to put Mr. May on a committee which meant to work and not to talk and which contained some busy men. He would rather Mr. May was not placed on it. (Laughter.) The CHAIRMAN: The Finance Committee have a lot of work to do. T know it sometimes takes from two to three hours. I don't think it is desirable to extend that time any longer than we can possibly help. (Laughter.) Mr. UNSWORTH said his name had been brought for- ward entirely without his kno ffledge. He would not like to go on the committee at the expense of driving Mr. Fulton Smith off.</p>
        <p>The amendment that Mr. May be placed on the cora• mittee was then voted upon and lost. Alderman PIERPoncr pointed out that his attendance at the committee meetings gave him a great deal of trouble, taken in conjunction with Corporation work. He offered to retire in favour of Mr. May or Mr. Unsworth. Alderman BENNETT said the Finance Committee ought to includean equal number if not a majority of members of the Corporation. The names as proposed by Mr. Jolley were then approved. THE SCHOOL MANAGEMENT COM:VIITTEE.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN moved that the School Management Committee be constituted as follows : —Alderman Pierpoint, Councillor Craik, Messrs. E. J. Guest, A. J. Jolley, J. Fulton Smith, and John Upson, Revs. M. Stevenson, E. J. Willcocks, Neville Wilson, and John Yonge,_</p>
        <p>The MAYOR thought there ought to be more members of the Council on the committee. There were plenty of clergymen but he did not know whether that was any advantage on a School Management Committee. The CHAIRMAN remarked that the clergymen had done as much work as any other members of the committee. (Hear, hear.) The Ms YOR. : I have nothing against them personally Mr. PLINSTON said Alderman Sutton, Councillors Lord and Burton, and Mr. Unsworth were on no com- mittees whatever. He moved that Councillor Burton be placed on the School Management Committee. Mr. MAY seconded. Councillor BURTON declined, as his time was so limited. - •</p>
        <p>The Rev. Father WILSON proposed that Ur. Unsworth be elected on the committee. The CHAIRICIN: I will add his name. _ _</p>
        <p>The IVIA.YOR proposed Alderman Sutton. Mr. IVIA.Y seconded. It was, be said, very wrong in principle to have so many professional members on that committee--gentlemen who represented the spending department and whose friends %s ere those who received the money. (Laughter.) The CHAIRMAN said that on behalf of the Rev. Morley Stevenson, who had written regretting his in- ability to attend the meeting, he would accept the two names proposed. The constitution of the committee was then agreed to. SCEIOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE. A GRIEVANCE.</p>
        <p>Councillor ELLISON moved that the following gentle- men form the School Attendance Committee :—Coun cillors Brough, Davies, Evans, Ellison, Maginn, and Rogers, Messrs. William Bibby, Thomas May, Edwin Plinston, and George Walker. Councillor ROGERS seconded. He pointed out that several members of the committee very seldom attended the meetings, and it would be a good thing to add three or four extra names.</p>
        <p>It was decided to place Councillor Lord on the com- mittee._ . _</p>
        <p>Mr. UVON said the School Management Committee met twice as often as any other sub-committee, and he did not think it desirable that the members of that com- mittee should be summoned to attend meetings of the School Attendanco Committee. Their business on the first-named committee sometimes occupied three hours or more, and they met almost weekly. On the return which had been issued it was recorded that members had been absent from a certain number of meetings, when they were not members of the School Attendance Committee. He rather prided himself upon his attend- ances. Although he wo.s.a member of three sab-com- mittees of the School Management Committee, he had never missed a meeting. Yet the return showed that he had missed seven. He did not think such a report should go forth. The Rev. ;JOHN YONGE supported Mr. J Upson's remarks because he was a fellow sufferer. (Laughter.) He would very much like to know who authorized what was knownas the rota, as he did not recollect its being approved by the committee. He objected to being summoned to meetings of the School Attendance Com- mittee for more reasons than one. He did not think it desirable for ministers to be on the committee.</p>
        <p>The MA.YOR said he had been summoned to School Attendance Committee meetings five times and had attended none. Some members had been summoned to 11 meetings and had attended 26. Mr. AURRAY said the rota was brought about by a resolution of the committee' the object being to spread out the work among all the members. There were gentlemen who attended the rota meetings voluntarily and did good work. (Hear, hear.) Councillor ELLISON proposed that they revert to the old system and that the School Attendance Committee do the work. _ _</p>
        <p>',The CHAIRMAN: That is a matter for yourselves and not for this meeting.</p>
        <p>The names as prciposed, with the addition of Coun- cillor Lord, were then approved. THE SCHOOL ACCONINIODATION QUESTION.</p>
        <p>WHO IS THE TELL-TALE?</p>
        <p>The MAYoR moved that the following gentlemen compose the Sites and Buildings Committee : Alder- man Bolton. Alderman Smethurst, Councillors Craik, Evans, Forshaw, Hulse, John Roberts,Tinnion, Mr. R.T. Fairclough, Mr. J. Fulton Smith. Alderman PrEnPoThrr seconded.</p>
        <p>Councillor HULSE pointed out that Councillor For- shaw had not attended a single meeting of this sub- committee. When spoken to on this subject, he under- stood that Mr. Forshaw said he did not intend to attend. He suggested that Mr. Unsworth's name be substituted.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR said every member attended the last meeting of the sub-committee, and he asked whether they would be willing to serve for another year. Councillor Hur,sE said Counpillor Forshaw was absent.</p>
        <p>Rev. JOHN YONGE : He may have some professional reason for not wishing to sit on that committee, and if that is So we ought to respect it. The MA.YOR : He has not notified me that he wishes to retire. _ . .</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said there was one thing in connec- tion with this committee which he would like to mention. This committee would require to purchase a consider- able number of sites for new schools. They had already been met with the difficulty that information had leaked out of the committee and led to the purchase of property which the sub-committee wanted to acquire, and for which a much larger price was afterwards asked. If the sub-committee could devise some plan by which they could avoid blackmail being levied upon them, it I would be worth consideration. The sub-committee had , already met with this difficulty and it would occur again and again if they did not mind. The MAYOR observed that one ; way out of the difficulty was for the School Attend- ance Committee to formulate a plan showing where additional accommodation was likely to be required in the near future, and that would give the Sites and Buildings sub-committee plenty of time to look round for suitable sites. When the sub-committee singled out a definite locality like School-brow, Fennel- street, or Latchford, then everyone was on the look-out and almost immediately the value went up. If the School Attendance Committee would suggest a thoroug h scheme for meeting the lack of school accommodation he thought the Sites and Buildings Committee would be able to look round and purchase what they considered were suitable sites. It was much better to purchase outright than to proceed by arbitration and pay for the compulsory sale of land or buildings. That was a- very expensive way and would cost as much for the site as the school building. Councillor ROGERS Said it was a groat pity the Chair man was not able to give the name of the mem her o the committee'that would give the show away in such manner as to fill the mouths of outsiders, who werea always prepared to bite and screw the last penny out of them. It was contemptible that any member should do such a thing. Councillor DAVIES said he always understood that more school accommodation should be provided, and that the Sites and Buildings Committee had been left to meet the deficiency. He did not see that the School Attendance Committee could form a plan. Alderman BOLTON pointed out that if the Sites and Buildings Committee had a plan showing what districts were overcrowded and were likely to need addi- tional accommodation, the Committee could act Upon that information.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. UPSON said it was not advisable to appoint members on committees if they would not attend. He saw from the return of attendances that one member had made only one attendance out of a possible 34. He suggested that this member be brought before the School Attendance Committee. (Laughter.) He would like to propose that the name of Councillor Dodds be substituted for that of Councillor Forshaw.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: I have no objection, personally. The CHAIRMAN: Then you will have the same diffi- culty complained of, that Mr, Forshaw is not on any sub-committee.</p>
        <p>- Mr. PLDISTON : There is no reason to have him on a sub-committee if he will not serve.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. D. BUCKTON :He has been a member of this committee for one year and has attended only one meeting. Councillor ROGERS: If it-is Mr. Forshaw's wish that he should be taken off the sub-committee, let him be taken oft. I have overy ren)ect for Mr. Forshaw who is a very decent fellow, and I would not like to see him driven off._</p>
        <p>It waq ultimately decided to retain the name of Councillor ForshaW on the sub-cemmatee and add the name of Councillor Dodds. It was also decided that the General Purposes Com- mittee consist of all the members of the Education Com- mittee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Question of Salaries.</head>
        <p>The Question of Salaries.</p>
        <p>Mr. Edward Wallington presided at the annual meet- ing of the Warrington and District Teachers' Associa- tion which was held in the Technical Institute on Saturday morning. There was a good attendance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>YEAR'S WORK.</head>
        <p>THE YEAR'S WORK.</p>
        <p>The annual report was presentod by Mr. John Upson (hon. secretary and treasurer) as follows:— We began the year with a membership of 81. We close it with 101, showing a net gain of 20 members. During the year eight members have left the association, of whom one has left the profession, four have joined other associations, and of three I can gain no informa- tion. None have ceased to be members who have remained in the association area, and lam glad to say that no member has died during the year. Since the close of the year. howover, I regret to have to announce that Mr. R. Chandley, of Warzrave, a very old member of the association, has died. -Twenty-eight new mem- bers have joined, seven of these come from other asso- ciations, eight left the training colleges in July last, and thirteen teachers have been out one or more years, and have paid an entrance fee of ss. 'Mille I the committee is pleased to report this increase, it I must point out the serious fact that only eight teachers out of the great number which has come into the association area from the training colleges have joined the association. Early in September I sent out 65 special invita- tions to these young teachers pointing out the advantages of the union and urging them to join, with„l must confess, very disappointing results. It is , impossible for your officers to do a great deal in this • direction unless they have the hearty support and cor- dial co-operation of the headteachers. Nor do I think • that the burdens of canvassing new members,inaddition , to the great and growing work of the association,should be thrust upon the secretary's shoulders. As sho-m by the attendances at the meetings, the members have never taken so much interest in the work of the asso- ciation before, as they have done this year. In conclu- sion I would point out that with every member taking a keen interest in the association, and regarding its welfare as his or her own, there is no reason why every certificated teacher in Warrington and district should not become a member of the association. (Applause) The report was adopted. THE N.U.T.</p>
        <p>Mr. Bradley, of Mluche3ter, ws nominited a mem- ber of the Executive Committee of the N.U.T.</p>
        <p>FINANCIAL.</p>
        <p>The balance sheet wa,s presented and adopted. It showed a balance in hand of about £B.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>The following officers were elected for the ensuing year :—President, Mr. Edward Wallington; vice-presi- dent, Mr. S. W. Jeffrey; hon. secretary and treasurer, Mr. John Upson ; assistant secretary, Mr. W. II Long- shaw ; press correspondent, Mr. S. VeJeffrey; secretary of Benevolent and Orphan Funds, Hr. Cann; committee, the Misses Shaw and Brookes and Messrs. Stansfield, Wright, Cann and Edden. HOW THE NEW ACT IS BEING WORKED. THE SALARIES QUESTION.</p>
        <p>In the course of an address the PRESANT said the Education Act had now been in operation for 12 months, and as teachers they were in a position to take a survey of the work which had been done. In the first place they must admit that the Education Committee had done a splendid work, and the teachers were feeling the benefit of it in many ways. The arrangements for cleaning the schools were a great improvement. Many schools had not been as ()lean as they ought to have been owing to the lack of funds, but by the appointment of a caretaker' who was made responsible for the cleanliness of the buildings, the teachers had been relieved of great anxiety. The Act treated caretakers well. They were interested in their duty and desired to carry out the wishes of the Education Committee to the letter. The next point to which he wished to refer was the supply of material. W the past it had been inadequate in many schools owing to the lack of funds. Teachers had had to work on the mini- mum of apparatus, furniture and books, but now the poorest school was supplied with a sufficient quantity of good furniture and apparatus. The poorest schools were put on an equality as far as material was con- cerned with the best schools. There had been some de- lay in supplying the material, but evidently this was not the fault of the committee. Dealing with the increase and improvement of the staff, the President expressed the belief that in the near future it would be impossible to find a teacher in charge of a large class. The inspector and the education authority were determined that there should be no class of 70, 80, or 90. Opinions differed as to the action of the educational authority in the treatment of teachers under Article 50, but there could be no two opinions as to the justice of the treatment with regard to females. Many considered the committee had been a littie pre- cipitate about the females, but it must be remembered that teachers belonged to an honourable and a noble profession, and they should, therefore, be careful not to allow, if they could help it, unqualified teachers serv- ing in the schools. (Applause.) Personally he was glad that the teachers under Article 50 were being compelled to get their certifi- cates. They must confess that with regard to salaries, the committee had dealt fairly with the assistant teachers. The scale for head teachers was also a fair one. The class teachers had been generously treated seeing that their past services were being recog- nized in the application of the scale. The head teachers claimed the same consideration, and he thought it was undignifiad for them to have to go cap in hand and ask for recognition of past services. (Applause.) He con- sidered it would have come with good grace had the committee recognized the claims of the head teachers in the application of the scale for past services as they had done in the case of the assistants. He was glad to see, however, from the reports in the newspapers, that there was a. good prospect of head teachers receiving fair treatment. But all these im- provements in the schools brought with them corresponding responsibilities. The teacher had been relieved from much financial anxiety, and he was free to give all his energy to the education of the chil- dren, although in spite of poor staff Na minimum supply of apparatus and furniture the Warrington schools had done well in the past. The inspectors and the Education Committee would expect intelligent results in the future, and the testing of the work of the teachers would become more searching. In a certain sense the teachers were now the servants of the Central Committee, and what any teacher did affected the whole. in conclusion</p>
        <p>r. Wallington appealed to teachers to conduct their schools in such a manner as not to forfeit the confidence of the Education Committee and cause rules and regula- tions to be imposed which many would regard as an indignity. ( Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>THANKS.</head>
        <p>THANKS.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. STANSFIELD, seconded by Mr UPsoN, Mr. Wallington was thanked for his interesting address. A hearty vote of thanks was also accorded to Mr. Upson for his services as the representative on the Education Committe e.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>BILLIARDS.</head>
        <p>BILLIARDS.</p>
        <p>Warrington Lib3rals D3Faat th?,</p>
        <p>Conservatives,</p>
        <p>The billiard matches between the members of th Warrington Liberal and Conservative Clubs wer renewed on Tuesday after having lapsed for some year The Liberals had the opportunity of playing on their own tables and made such good use of the advantage that they won 12 games out of 15, and secured the sub- stantial majority of 321 points. There was some very good play on both sides, but the Liberals excelled them- selves. The game of the evening was played by J. McKenzie who defeated a formidable opponent in T Horra,bin, while Dr. Smith was seen at his best in opposi- tionto Sangster, the Conservative Club manager. Mr. Sangster was obviously, however not doing himself justice. Aftqr the conclusion of the games there was much conviviality,and suitable speeches were delivered by Messrs. Oldfleld and florrabin for the visitors, and Councillor Tinnion and Mr. E. Pierpoint for the Liberals The individual scores were as follow.</p>
