      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>EXCLUSION OF A LADY MEMBER.</head>
        <p>THE EXCLUSION OF A LADY MEMBER.</p>
        <p>The Rev. JOHN YO.NGE said he wished to point out, what he ventured to point out some months ago that the Town Council was not fulfilling its duty with regard to the Ilcomplete constitution of the committee. On page 3 of the scheme, section 4, it *as stated that "The Council shall appoint at least one woman a member of the Committee." That had never been done. He had never been able to fatbam the reason why. The MAYOR; Shall Ibe in order in telling you, why? • The Rev. JOHN YONGE : If you can I shall be glad. The MA.YoR : We asked for this lady member to come from the Training College. The College sent the Rev. Morley Stevenson instead. I don't know whether he is the best apology they had. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>The Rev. JOHN YONGE : I propose that a lady be appointed. The CHAIRMAN; That will rest with the Town Council.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: You cannot expect a lady to come from the Town Council because we haven't got one. (Laughter.) The Rev. JOHN YONGE : If I were disposed to be humorous I might have something to say about the constitution of the Town Council. (Loud laughter.)' The Town Council ought never to have sanctioned this scheme if they did not mean to carry it out. The. clause said distinctly and definitely that the Council. shall appoint one woman a member of the committee.. The CHAIRMAN said the mistake was made by the Museum Committee in not appointing the Rev. Morle- y as one of the co-optative members. If the- Museum Committee had done that, the Training College- Committee would in all probability have appointed a lady.</p>
        <p>Mr. Councillor DODDS said the Museum Committee did not appoint the Rev. Morley Stevenson because he had already been appointed to represent another body.- The CHAIRMAN said the difficulty had presented itself to other education authorities. At present there- did not seem any way out of it. Councillor Dorms observed that he was in full sympathy with Mr. Yonge and he would undertake, if he remained in the Council to take up this question and see if a lady could not be appointed on the committee. The DIRECTOR Of EDUCATION pointed out that the- only representatives co-opted by the Town Council were those selected by the Technical Sub-committee, and it was stipulated that they must have had experi- ence in education and be acquainted with the needs of the schools in the town.</p>
        <p>The Rev. JOHN YONGE asked what was the use of putting it in the scheme that a lady was to be appointed if the Council could not carry it out. The DIRECTOR OF EDUCATION said it was inserted because the Board of Education insisted that it should be put in. It was stipulated in the sixth section, that three members should be appointed on the Education' Committee from the Technical Sub-committee, which, should consist entirely of men. Alderman PIERPOINT : Shame. •</p>
        <p>The subject then dropoed and the committee rose</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-02_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>PUBLICANS AND THEIR ASSESSMENTS.</head>
        <p>PUBLICANS AND THEIR ASSESSMENTS.</p>
        <p>m.a.a A Batch of Appeals.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Union Assessment Com-- mittee was held on Thursday. Mr. A. J. Jolley presided' and there were also present: Messrs. William Hough- ton, A.Jolley, T. Halton, J. Clarke, J. Billinge, and J. Sutton (clerk), William Owen (valuer), and W. H. Wood- cock (assistant overseer).</p>
        <p>THE VALUATION LIST. Mr. WOODCOCK submitted the supplemental valuation list for the Borough. The gross value of the assessment of the Borough, he said, was £307,047 10s. and £251,203 rateable,which showed an increase for the year of .£4,673 10s. gross and £3812 rateable, and for th 3 six months of £1,314 gross and £1,066 10s. rateabla. The list was approved. THE MERSEY HOTEL.</p>
        <p>- Mr. A. BROWNE appealed on behalf of Sarah J. Butter- worth against the assessment of the Mersey Hotel,. Mersey-street. He observed that although the place 's called an hotel it was merely a beerhouse, consisting of vaults, two small rooms, bedrooms upstairs. kitchen BGc. It was formerly assessed at £35 but now it had been jumped up to £66 14s. for no earthly reason so far as he could see. - The CHAIRIVAN said the real figures were £7O gross and £59 10s. rateable, and not £66 145., as Mr. Browne stated.</p>
        <p>Mr. BROWNS. But still I submit that £59 10s. is an outrageous sum for a small beerhouse like this. The actual number of barrels sold tivera,ges only four a week. The committee decided to make no change. THE NORTON ARMS HOTEL. Mr. SAMPSON appealed against the assessment of the Norton Arms Hotel, which stood at £165 gross, and £l4O rateable, with stables at £35 gross and £3O rate- able. He remarked that the assessment was too high in view of the altered conditions. The inauguration of the tramways had materially affected his takings. He estimated that 20 per cent, fewer people drove to the hotel than formerly. The trams now took the people past the hotel. His rent was £l5O per annum. It was decided to reduce the assessment of the hotel to gross £155, rateablej£l32 10s. The assessment of the stables was left as before. The alteration means a reduction of £lO gross. THE FATE OF THE GEORGE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas Halton applied for a temporary reduction. of the Assessment of the George Inn, which stood at £95 gross and £Bl rateable. He pointed out that the Corporation had acquired the property for the purpose of widening Bridge-street and the Town Clerk had reduced the rent to £2O. The stables had been pulled down already and the premises themselves would be removed this year. The CHAIRMAN: You are now only in temporary occupation.</p>
        <p>Tho ASSISTANT OVERSEER said the overseers were quite satisfied that this was a case that the Assessment Committee should deal with. The stables from which. Mr. Halton derived the bulk of his custom had practic- ally all gone. The overseers were quite willing to have it reduced to £2O gross, £l7 rateable. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY: Is the George Inn going out altogether? Mr. WILLIAM OWEN: It Will die. Mr. HALTON . The whole place is going to be swept away. Theassessment was reduced to £2O gross,2l7 rateable.. THE LONGFORD HOTEL. Mr. Joseph Horlock,of the Longford Hotel,Winwick- road appealed against the assessment of his hotel which stood at £125 gross and £lO5 ratoable. The rent of the hotel, he said was £75. He had been doing only 25 or 3 barrels this winterand was losing money on the hotel. It was decided to reduce the assessment to £llO gross.</p>
        <p>THE VALUER'S REPORT. Air. Owen reported that since the last meeting he had made the following valuations in the township of War- rington. Johnson Brothers (Dyers) Limited, lock-up shop, Bridee-street, gross £5O, rateable £42 10s. St. Ann's Vicarage, Orford-lane,gross £4o,rateable £34 The Alliance Box Company, Orford-lane for the works as now used and occupied, gross £153, rateable £122. The Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Company Limited were still in occupation of a portion orthesepremises, which was separately assessed to them. The report was adopted. The thanks of the committee were extended to the Chairman and Vice-chairman for their services during the past year.</p>
        <p>BREAKING A RECORD.—The Town Council meeting of Southport was a record one on Tuesday night, only- lasting half a minute. The meeting was summoned to appoint representatives to the Water Board. The gentlemen had already been appointed at a previous meeting, but it was discovered that as they had been elected thirty-one days prior to April 5 they were not legal appointments.</p>
