        <p>Coming Events. Cycling. The Rev. John Yonge will conduct Easter Services Saturday was by no means an ideal day for the :at Wycliffe church to-morrow (Sunday). Warrington Cycling Club's opening run. We have to The Padgate Amateur Dramatic Society will give be thankful for small mercies in the way of fine 'two performances in aid of the Warrington Infirmary weather nowadays, and as there was no rain. the 21 in the National Schoolroom,Padgate, on Thursday and members and friends who made the journey cheerfully Friday next. put up with the cold wind. An excellent tea was A public meeting in connection with the Warring- provided at the Saracen's Head by Mrs. Barff. A ,ton branch of the National Service League will be pleasant time was afterwards spent on the bowling held in the Parr Hall on Friday, the 15th inst. Lord green and in strolling round the pretty village. Newton and Mr. George Shee, M.A. (secretary of the On the whole the run will rank as a most successful league) will be the speakers. Mr. F. W. Monks will Preside. The Feather's Hotel. At the Borough Police Court on Thursday, Thomas Hellen was granted temporary authority to sell at the Feathers' Hotel, Bridge street, in lieu of Joseph Hatton. Cab Accident. John Stokes, aged forty-four, of 71., Pierpaint-street Warrington, was admitted to the Warrington Infirm- ary on Tuesday night suffering from a broken leg caused by being run over by a cab. UNDER the CLOCK for GOOD BOOTS.—Advt. •Postponement of the Council Meeting. The General Purposes Committee has decided that in consequence of the day preceding the one fixed , for the Council Meeting being a Bank aoliday,to post- pone the Council meeting from the sth to the 12th -April next. Accident to a Shunter. On Tuesday morning John Edward Greenway (25), ,of 35, Factory-lane, a foreman shunter in the employ of Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Soas, Limited, was •at work in one of the sidings when he was caught between the buffers of an engine and a waggon and sustained severe injuries. He was taken to the In- firmary and is progressing favourably. FOR CLASS AND CHIN&amp; of every description try J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street. Warrington.—Advt. . , Octogenarian's Sudden Death. Ellen Me Cormick, aged 87, of no settled residence, Was found dead in an outbuilding at Rixton On Sat- airday. The deceased had been on tramp the greater apart of her life, but for the past four months had been an inmate of the Salford Borough Workhouse, from Which institution she took her discharge on Friday. LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS and SHOES, UNDER the CLOCK, BRID GE-STREET.—Athit- ?he Recent Election of Auditors. A return of the expenses incurred by the four can- didates during the recent cantest for borough auditors has been handed in to the Town Clerk. The expenses incurred were as follow: James Ball, £2 19s. 4d.; Edwin Bradshaw. £5 Bs. 3d.; L. Bolton, £2 7s. 9d.; Joseph Rigby Williams, £5 Bs. 6d. Ragged School Band of Hope. A splendid concert was given by the members in Ihe upper schoolroom on Monday evening. Mr. J. Skelland presided and also gave an excellent address. The following contributed to the programme : Misses Maggie Higgeinson, T. Makin, T. Williams, Nellie Virainwright, S. Richards, N. and P. Higginson, N. Berry, S. Crooks, M. Green, M. I. Tichard,L. Filling- ham. S. Potts, S. A. Tichard, E. Makin, Masters Ted Fear, Walter Wright, H. Brandwood, J. Wright, W. Titchard, J. Savage, Harry Wright, W. Parton and T. Qrozier. A most successful concert was concluded by the singincr of the National Anthem. PUBLIC 7BENEFIT, UNDER the CLOCK for BESA BOOTS in LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Fire at Messrs. Brunner, Mond's Works. At 5.30 on Tuesday morning a fire occurred at the Winnington Chemical Works, Northwich, at Messrs. Brunner,Mond, and Co. It commenced in the waggon shop, and the firm's brigade, under Mr. Mathieson, Was promptly on the scene. There was a splendidl Supply of water and after an hour's efforts the fire was extinguished. The building, as well as a quantity of the contents,was partially destroyed. Being a wooden structure, the flames rose to a great height. The dam- age is roughly estimated at £l,OOO, which is fully covered by insurance. The cause of the outbreak is tUIkIIOIVD. FIRE GRATES Of every description.—.T. S. GAR- LAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warringion.—Advt. Ebenezer Church, Buttermarket-street, Band of Hope. A Baud of IlOpe meeting was held on Monday night when a programme consisting of various items, given by the Buckley-street Band of Hope, was rendered. MI. H. Wooley occupie I the chair, and delivered an .41,. , ',clress. The following took part in the pro,gramme : Vss Jane Sankey Master John Hewitt, Miss arrison, 'Liss Whitmore, Miss H Worrall, Miss E. Parker, A. Plinston, Miss Eliza Ryder. Miss Plinston was the accompanist. A short address was given by Mr. Merlin, who also proposed a vote of thanks to the 13nckley-street members, which was seconded by Mr. Morgan, and carried unanimously. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLA.Sb IVIDTERAL WATERS.—AdVt. Attendances at Education Meetings. A return or the attendances of members at the meetings of the Education Committee and sub-com- iiiittees during the year ended March 31st, has just been issued. Mr. A. J. Jolley comes out with the best record, having been present at 61 meetings out of a Possible 64. Mr. Frederick Monks (chairman) made .51 attendances out of a possible 88; Alderman Arthur Bennett (deputy chairman), 46 out of a possible 88; Mr. 3. Fulton Smith, 46 out of a possible 51; Mr. John Upson, 45 out of a possible 40; Rev. Neville Wilson, 45 out of 49' Rev. John Yonge • 40 out of 49 ; Mr. R. T. Fairclough 38 out of 41; Mr. Edwin Flinston 34 out of 36 ; Mr. Councillor Ellison 33 out of 36; Mr. E. J. Guest 34 out of 64; Rev Morley Stevenson, 31 out of 49 ;Rev. E. J. Willcocks; 31 out of 49 • Mr. George Walker, 30 out of 36 ; Mr. 'Thomas May, 30 out of 36. BASSINETTES AND MAIL CARTS in latest art Shades.—J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Shocking Railway' Accident. At St. Helens Town Hall on Tuesday an inquiry Was held into the circumstances attending the death 'DT George Helsby, fitter, aged fifty-four, of Wellfield- street, Warrington.—The evidence showed that Helsby Worked for the London and North-Western Railway Company. On Saturday morning he was testing Signals and points at St. Helens with his assistant, .Alfred Starkie. They were w-irking at the points leading from the St. Helens and Widnes line to the Sidings at the Ravenhead branch about 9.45 on Satur- 'cLay morning. A. passenger train and a goods train Were approaching in opposite directions. Helsby ?topped out of the way of the goods train and was knocked down and killed by the passenger engine. The back of his head was shockingly injured, and his 1-u ,elt arm was cut off .—A verdict of "Accidental death" was returned. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING 01 Laxics.-63, SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt. ,Presentation to an Ex-police Constable. Police Constable Frederick Whittaker, who has just resigned his position in the Force, after eight years service_ to go into business, was on Wednesday last made the recipient of a handsome silver-mounted lking stick from his former comrades of all ranks. he presentation was made, in the presence of a large .Tuster of the force, by Chief Inspector Phillips, on uchalf of the Chief Constable. Inspector Phillips said that as soon as it was known Constable Whittaker Was. leaving them, a general feeling was expressed that they should do something to show their ree_gard for him, both as a comrade and an athlete and member of their Athletic Club. rsonally he (the inspector) was sorry Whittaker Waseho going. He had been a smart officer, attentive to -duties, and taking a pride in his uniform, and lleoreov_bi_ r done great credit to the force as an athlete. at d come prominently before the police and public athletic meetings throughout this part of the of 4'117, and the Athletic Club, as a slight recognition s services, had pleasure in making him an honorary 4_elober on his retirement from amongst them. s Pector Phillips had much pleasure in hand- ing _him the stick on behalf of the Warring- tv.". Force.—lnspector Rowbotham, Sergeant (secretary of the Police Athletic Club) Sergeant Smith also spoke in high terms an.' Constable Whittaker. They considered .him ficient police officer and a loyal comrade. • Ilot rnstable Whittaker in reply said he really co id greatild words to properly thank them all for their en.,,4ndness. As they knew he had been fortunate t on, to win many prizes at different times but he totti9 ssure them there was none of them he valued etilghly as he did this mark of their respect. The 84NthWhich is aebony one and handsomely . h • black r e -mounted, bears the recipient's monogram beauti- ngraved, and also the following inscription,— Cou__ Presented by the members of the Warrington n_tY Borough Police Force, March 17th, 1904." It miPplied to the Police order by Mr. Mounfield, " inwi k e -street.</p>
