        <p>THE LANCASHIRE CONSTITUENCIES. THE ELECTORAL ROLL FOR 1904. Contrasts and Anomalies. The annual return of the constituencies of the King- dom has just been issued and is of special interest in view of the approach of a general election. In Lancashire there are to-day 702,893 electors, as com- pared with 692,464 last year. There is thus an average electorate per member of 12,331. It is interesting to recall that after the last Redistribution Act in 1885 the number of electors was only 532,511, and at the general election in 1900, 658,352. The increase of 170,000 in 18 years is almost equal to one-third of the electorate in 1885. The electorate of Cheshire is 144,726, which gives an average of 11,113 for each of the thirteen members of the County. In Warrington there are now 10,281 voters, as com- pared with 9,974 last year and 9,360 in 1900. Going further back, it is of interest to notice that in 1885 the electorate of the borough was only 7,730. 2,551 less than it is to-day. With the exception of 28 lodgers all the electors are occupiers. Takinc, the general average of the Kingdom at 10,625 per member,Lancashire is entitled to 66 members,instead of 57 as at present. There are 34 borough seats in the county with an average electorate of 10,601. The fol- lowing boroughs have more than one member :- No. of Electors Aver- Electors members. 1904. age per 1900. member. Liverpool 9 84,585 9,398 82,874 Manchester Salford Blackburn 2 20,600 10,300 19,496 Bolton Oldham 2 32,723 16,361 29,295 Preston 2 18,228 9,114 16,867 We may contrast Oldham, with its two members and 32,000 voters, with the ancient city of Bath, which enjoys the same representation, but has only 7661 elec- tors. It may also be noted than Oldham has more voters than Salford, which possesses a third member. Com- pared with other great cities, Liverpool is over-repre- sented. For example, Glasgow has 92,000 electors and only seven members, and Leeds 71,000 voters, and only five members. Or, if we compare the representation of Liverpool with the county divisions of Lancashire, the former has a Member of Parliament for every 3,398 electors, and the latter a member for every 14,889 elec- tors. If one of the Liverpool members were given to , Oldham, the representation of the above group would be fairly equitable. We next give a list of the single- member boroughs -- Electors 1904 Electors,l9oo 1904. 1904. Ashton-under-Lyne 8,016 7,753 Barrow-in-Furness 8,748 7,755 • Burnley 15,946 14,393 Bury 8,962 8,581 Rochdale 13,704 12,968 St. Helens 11,649 10,763 Warrington 10,281 9,360 Wigan 8,575 8,059 Though Lancashire has no very small boroughs,it may be pointed out that the aggregate electorate of Ashton, Barrow, Bury, and Wigan is only 344)0, as compared with 81,000 in the four largest county divisions. It is improbable, however' that under any redistribution scheme these boroughs would lose their separate repre- sentation, and the principal effect of such a measure would be to increase the number of members for the county divisions. At present in the 23 county con- stituencies there are 342,449 electors, which gives the very high average of 14,889 per division. The following are the geographical groups of the county seats :- NORTH LANCASHIRE.-NOrth Len.sdale, 1904, SBOB, 1900, 9537; Lancaster 13,879-12,334, Black- pool 19,353-16,807, and Chorley 13,023-12,288. NORTH-EAST LINCASHIRE.-Darwen 16,058- 15,308, Clitheroe 20,127-18,584, Accrington 15,047 -14,221, Rossendale 12,557-12,148. SoUTH-EAST LANCASHIRE.-West Houghton 17,320-15,827, Heywood 10,391-10,022, Middleton 14,314-13,707, Raddliffe-cum-Farnworth 12,901- 12,244, Eccles 16,308-14,674, Stretford 22,394- 18,909, Gorton 15,709-14,511, and Prestwich 18,101 -15,732. SOUTH-WEST LANCASHIRE.-Southport 14,174- 13,164, Ormskirk 12,068-11,052' Bootle 19,531- 17,439, Widnes 10,310-9,447, Newton 13,214-12,062, Ince 12,767-11,781, and Leigh 13,085-11,907. In contrast with the four boroughs containing 34,000 electors, we may group the four largest countyconstitu- encies, viz., Stretford 22,391, Clitheroe 20,127, B ootle 19,531, and Blackpool 19,353-an aggregate of 81,000 and having the same representation. Leaving Lancashire and turning to the Kingdom as a whole, the outstanding feature of the new return is the continued decline in the Irish electorate. Since 1885 the Scottish electorate has increased from 560,580 to 703,997, but in the same period the number of electors in the Irioh constituencies has fallen from 737,758 to 694,009, and yet Ireland has 101 representatives as com- pared 70 for Scotland. The following table shows the comparative figures for every part of the Kingdom:- *No. of Electors Aver- Electors members. 1904. age per 1900. members. England and Wales ... 490 5,624,936 11,479 5,269,775 Scotland Ireland lOl 694,009 6871- 757,849 United Kingdom *Excluding University Constituencies. - The representation of Ireland may also be compared with the following important groups of English con- stituencies :- Average per Members. Electors. Member. Lancashire 57 702,893 12,331 • Yorkshire 52 651,960 12,538 London 62 673,604 10,864 It will be seen from the figures that Lancashire has 8,000 more electors than there are in Ireland, but it has 44 fewer members. On the basis of proportionate re- presentation, Ireland should have 65 instead of 101 members. But not only is there serious inequality in the repro- sentatiol; of various portions of the Kingdom; there are also gross anomalies in the size of individual con- stituencies, as will be seen from the following ecreparison Large Elee- Small Elee- Constituencies. tors. Constituencies. tors. Romford Division 41,759 Kilkenny 1,489 W'althamstow „ 32,945 Newry 1,939 Wandsworth 27,403 Durham 2,638 Barrow Division 26,538 Bury St. Edmunds 2,739 Cardiff District 26,144 Wick District 2,767 Handsworth Division 23,766 Sutherlandshi re 2,283 178,555 14,355 A vote in any of the constituencies in the second group has twelve times the value of a vote in the first group, a striking proof that we are still a long way from the ideal of" One Vote One Value." RUNCORN MOTHER AND HER BABY. At Runcorn, on Monday, Maria Evans, anold offender was charged with being drunk on Saturday night in charge of an eleven-months-old child. Police-constable Bratt said the woman was sitting on the wall of the Bridgewater Canal, and she appeared likely to drop the baby on the ground. She was very drunk. The prisoner was sentenced to a month's hard labour. CLARKE'. w. L,-fr MED BLoon MIETUKX" Is a guaran- tee' Ore tar all 00 a If is the most • (writhing blond cleanser overidiseevered,and it will free th system from all ittlo unities from whatever cause arising. For crofula, Scurvy, Bczeina, Bad Legs. Pimples and Sores of all kinds, its effects are marvellous. Thousands of Testi- ri °nigh. Bold everywhere at 2s. 9d. per bottle. Beware of worthless imitations and sulestitutv.--Advt. 1. CLANEWS Pi 41 PIMA; are warranz,a to cure, in sex, all acquired or constitutional Discharges from the Urinary Organs, Gravel and Pains in the back. Free from Mercury. Established upwards of 30 years. In boxes 4s. 6d. each, of all Chemists and Patent Medicine Vendor. through- out the world, or sent for sixty stamps by the makers. The Lincoln and, Midland Counties DrugOompany. s-Yoe TBX TEXTB AicD Mix.tra.---Thorotagby cleanses the teeth from all parasites or impurities, harden the gums, prevtoto tartar, stops decay, and gives to the teeth a peculiar pearly whiteness and a delightful fragrance to the breath. Price fe. 6d. for the liquid, or Is. per jar for the Flortitne "Pa's der." cf all Chemists and Perfumers.- 4 gist YALIJABLX DISCOVERY TOR TBE iIAIIL-11 your hair Is tvirning grey or white or falling off use the ?diatoms Kith I:pspwin, for it will positively restore, in every case, grey or white hair to its original colour. It makes the ban' charmingly teautiful, as we.: as tromoting the giowth. Price as 6d fer tattle.- if To MOTXXRB.-51rs. Wine low's Soot